// Auto-generated offline chapter bundle for Junior Coders Book (scripts/build-books.js)
window.BOOK_LARAVEL_CHAPTERS = {
  "part-01-introduction/ch01-what-is-laravel.md": "# Chapter 1: What is Laravel?\n\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\n\nImagine your school decides to open a new library. You could cut the wood, build every shelf and print every library card yourself. Or you could start with ready-made shelves, a card system and a front desk, and spend your energy on choosing good books. Laravel is the second choice for web apps. It gives you the shelves and the front desk, so you can focus on what your app is for.\n\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\n\n- Explain what a **framework** is and how Laravel helps a PHP developer.\n- Compare writing an app in plain PHP with writing it in Laravel.\n- Install the tools you need on your own computer (XAMPP, Composer).\n- Create your first Laravel project and see it running in the browser.\n- Meet the school library app that you will build across this book.\n\n## 🔥 What is Laravel?\n\n**Laravel** is a free, open-source **PHP web framework**. Taylor Otwell first released it in 2011. Today it is one of the most popular ways to build websites with PHP.\n\nA **framework** is a ready-made structure for an app. It gives you folders, tools and rules. You fill in the parts that are special to your app. **Open source** means anyone can read the code and help improve it.\n\nYou already know PHP and MySQL. Laravel does not replace them. It is written in PHP, and it talks to MySQL. It simply organises your work.\n\n## ⚖️ Plain PHP vs Laravel\n\n| Task | Plain PHP | Laravel |\n|------|-----------|---------|\n| Page addresses (URLs) | One `.php` file per page | Clean routes in `routes/web.php` |\n| Database queries | Write SQL by hand with PDO | **Eloquent** models, plus SQL when you need it |\n| HTML pages | PHP mixed into HTML | **Blade** templates |\n| Checking form input | Many `if` statements | One `$request->validate([...])` call |\n| Login system | Build it all yourself | Starter kits give you a tested one |\n| Form security | You must remember it | `@csrf` tokens built in |\n\nLaravel helps protect your app from common attacks when you use its tools. It does not make every app safe by itself. You still need to think carefully about every page you build.\n\n## 🧰 The Tools You Need\n\nYou will build every example on **your own computer**. Laravel cannot run inside a web page, so this book has no in-browser lab. Your computer is the lab.\n\n1. **XAMPP**: gives you PHP and MySQL. Laravel 12 needs **PHP 8.2 or newer**.\n2. **Composer**: the PHP package manager. A **package** is code someone else wrote that you can reuse. Install Composer from getcomposer.org with the Windows installer.\n3. **A code editor**, such as VS Code.\n4. **A terminal**: Command Prompt, PowerShell or Git Bash.\n\nCheck your versions in the terminal:\n\n```bash\nphp -v\ncomposer --version\n```\n\nOutput (your numbers may be different or higher):\n\n```text\nPHP 8.2.12 (cli) (built: Oct 24 2023 21:15:15) (ZTS Visual C++ 2019 x64)\nComposer version 2.8.12 2025-09-19 13:41:59\n```\n\nIf you see `'php' is not recognized`, add `C:\\xampp\\php` to your Windows PATH, then open a new terminal.\n\n## 🚀 Create Your First Project\n\nGo to the XAMPP web folder and ask Composer to create a new Laravel app called `library-app`:\n\n```bash\ncd C:\\xampp\\htdocs\ncomposer create-project laravel/laravel library-app\n```\n\nComposer downloads Laravel and the packages it needs. This takes a few minutes the first time. When it finishes, go into the folder and check the version:\n\n```bash\ncd library-app\nphp artisan --version\n```\n\nOutput (your number may be higher):\n\n```text\nLaravel Framework 12.61.0\n```\n\n**Artisan** is Laravel's command-line helper. You will use it in almost every chapter. Every Artisan command starts with `php artisan`.\n\n## 🌐 See It in the Browser\n\nStart Laravel's small development server:\n\n```bash\nphp artisan serve\n```\n\nOutput:\n\n```text\n   INFO  Server running on [http://127.0.0.1:8000].\n\n  Press Ctrl+C to stop the server\n```\n\nOpen `http://127.0.0.1:8000` in your browser. You will see the Laravel welcome page. Keep this terminal open while you work. Press **Ctrl+C** to stop the server.\n\n## 📚 Our Project: The School Library App\n\nIn this book you will build one app, step by step: a **school library app**. It has three main parts:\n\n```text\n+---------+    +---------+    +---------+\n|  Books  |    |  Loans  |    | Members |\n| title   |<---| book    |--->| name    |\n| author  |    | member  |    | grade   |\n| copies  |    | due on  |    | email   |\n+---------+    +---------+    +---------+\n```\n\n- **Books**: what the library owns.\n- **Members**: the students and teachers who borrow books.\n- **Loans**: a record that a member borrowed a book, and when it is due back. (In this book, a \"loan\" always means borrowing a library book.)\n\nBy Chapter 24 the app will list books, let librarians add and edit them, record loans, check form input, and keep the librarian pages behind a login.\n\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\n\n**Running Artisan in the wrong folder.**\n\n```bash\nC:\\xampp\\htdocs> php artisan serve\n```\n\nWhat happens:\n\n```text\nCould not open input file: artisan\n```\n\nThe `artisan` file lives inside your project folder. Fix it by moving into the project first:\n\n```bash\nC:\\xampp\\htdocs> cd library-app\nC:\\xampp\\htdocs\\library-app> php artisan serve\n```\n\n## ✍️ Try It\n\nCreate the `library-app` project on your own computer. Run `php artisan --version` and `php artisan serve`, then open the welcome page in your browser.\n\n*Hint:* if Composer seems slow, wait. It is downloading many packages. Do not close the terminal halfway.\n\n## 📝 Recap\n\n- Laravel is a free PHP framework. It gives your app a clear structure and ready-made tools.\n- It still uses the PHP and MySQL you already know.\n- Composer installs Laravel; Artisan (`php artisan ...`) is its command-line helper.\n- `php artisan serve` runs your app at `http://127.0.0.1:8000`.\n- This book builds one app: a school library with books, members and loans.\n\n## 🏆 Practice\n\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\nWhat is a framework, and which two tools do you use to create and run a Laravel project?\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\nA framework is a ready-made structure (folders, tools and rules) for building an app. You create the project with **Composer** (`composer create-project laravel/laravel library-app`) and run it with **Artisan** (`php artisan serve`).\n\n</details>\n\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\nA classmate types `php artisan serve` and sees `Could not open input file: artisan`. What two things should they check?\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\n1. Are they inside the project folder? Run `cd library-app` first.\n2. Did `composer create-project` finish without errors? If it stopped halfway, the `artisan` file may be missing. Delete the half-made folder and run the command again.\n\n</details>\n\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\nPlan your library app before you code it.\n\n1. Write the three main parts of the app.\n2. For each part, list three facts the app should store.\n3. Write two things a librarian should be able to do that a student should not.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\n\n1. Books, members, loans.\n2. Books: title, author, number of copies. Members: name, grade, email. Loans: which book, which member, due date.\n3. Only a librarian should add or delete books, and only a librarian should mark a book as returned.\n\nYou will build all of this. Chapter 24 shows how to keep librarian pages behind a login.\n\n</details>\n",
  "part-01-introduction/ch02-mvc-architecture.md": "# Chapter 2: MVC Architecture\n\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\n\nThink about how your school library works. A student walks up to the **front desk** and asks for a book. The librarian at the desk does not hold every book in her hands. She checks the **shelves and records**, finds the book, and hands it over with a neat **issue slip**. Three jobs, three places: the desk decides, the shelves store, the slip shows. Laravel organises every web app in the same way. We call it **MVC**.\n\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\n\n- Name the three parts of MVC: Model, View and Controller.\n- Describe what each part does in the school library app.\n- Follow one request on its journey: route → controller → model → view.\n- Find where each part lives in a Laravel project.\n\n## 🧩 The Three Parts of MVC\n\n**MVC** stands for **Model, View, Controller**. It is a way to split an app into three parts, each with one job.\n\n| Part | Job | In the library |\n|------|-----|----------------|\n| **Model** | Talks to the database | The shelves and record book |\n| **View** | Shows HTML to the user | The issue slip or notice board |\n| **Controller** | Decides what to do | The librarian at the desk |\n\nLaravel adds one more piece: the **route**. A route is like the signboard at the library door. It says, \"Questions about books? Go to the books desk.\"\n\n## 🛣️ The Request Journey\n\nWhen someone visits a page, a **request** travels through your app. A request is the message a browser sends to a server, such as \"please give me `/books`\". The server sends back a **response**, usually an HTML page.\n\n```text\nBrowser asks for /books\n        |\n        v\n+----------------+\n| Route          |  routes/web.php\n+----------------+\n        |\n        v\n+----------------+\n| Controller     |  BookController\n+----------------+\n     |      ^\n     v      | books\n+----------------+\n| Model          |  Book (books table)\n+----------------+\n        |\n        v\n+----------------+\n| View           |  books/index.blade.php\n+----------------+\n        |\n        v\nBrowser shows the HTML page\n```\n\n1. The **route** matches the address `/books` and calls a controller method.\n2. The **controller** asks the **model** for the books.\n3. The **model** runs a database query and gives the books back.\n4. The controller passes the books to a **view**.\n5. The view turns them into HTML, and the browser shows it.\n\n## 📁 Where Each Part Lives\n\nHere is a sneak peek at the real files. You will write each one properly in later chapters, so do not worry about every word yet. Just notice where each file lives.\n\n**The route** (Chapter 4):\n\n```php\n// routes/web.php\nuse App\\Http\\Controllers\\BookController;\n\nRoute::get('/books', [BookController::class, 'index']);\n```\n\n**The controller** (Chapter 7):\n\n```php\n// app/Http/Controllers/BookController.php\npublic function index()\n{\n    $books = Book::all();                            // ask the model\n    return view('books.index', ['books' => $books]); // hand to the view\n}\n```\n\n**The model** (Chapter 13):\n\n```php\n// app/Models/Book.php\nclass Book extends Model\n{\n    // Laravel links this class to the \"books\" table.\n}\n```\n\n**The view** (Chapter 10):\n\n```blade\n{{-- resources/views/books/index.blade.php --}}\n<h1>Library Books</h1>\n@foreach ($books as $book)\n    <p>{{ $book->title }}</p>\n@endforeach\n```\n\nIf the `books` table holds two books, the browser shows:\n\n```text\nLibrary Books\nClean Water for Every Village\nThe Story of Numbers\n```\n\n## 🤔 Why Split the Work?\n\nIn plain PHP, one file often does everything. It connects to MySQL, runs SQL, loops through rows and prints HTML. That works for a tiny page. As the app grows, the file becomes long and hard to fix.\n\nMVC helps in three ways:\n\n- **Easy to find things.** A wrong colour? Look in the view. Wrong data? Look in the model or controller.\n- **Easy to share work.** One student designs views while another writes controllers.\n- **Easy to reuse.** The same `Book` model serves the book list, the search page and the loan form.\n\n## 🕵️ Code Detective\n\nWhich part of the app should do each job?\n\n| Job | Part |\n|-----|------|\n| Save a new loan in the `loans` table | Model |\n| Decide to show the \"Book returned\" message | Controller |\n| Draw the table of books with HTML | View |\n| Send `/members` to the members controller | Route |\n\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\n\n**Putting database work inside the view.**\n\n```blade\n{{-- resources/views/books/index.blade.php (wrong) --}}\n@php\n    $books = \\App\\Models\\Book::all();\n@endphp\n```\n\nIt runs, but now the view does the controller's job. Later, when you want only the books that are on the shelf, you must dig through HTML to change it. Keep the view for showing data. Fetch the data in the controller:\n\n```php\n// app/Http/Controllers/BookController.php (right)\n$books = Book::all();\nreturn view('books.index', ['books' => $books]);\n```\n\n## ✍️ Try It\n\nOn paper, draw the journey of a request for `/members`. Label the route, the controller (`MemberController`), the model (`Member`) and the view (`members/index.blade.php`).\n\n*Hint:* copy the journey diagram above and change each name.\n\n## 📝 Recap\n\n- MVC splits an app into Model (data), View (HTML) and Controller (decisions).\n- In Laravel a **route** sends each address to a controller method.\n- The journey is always: route → controller → model → view.\n- Routes live in `routes/web.php`, controllers in `app/Http/Controllers`, models in `app/Models`, views in `resources/views`.\n\n## 🏆 Practice\n\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\nWhat do the letters M, V and C stand for, and what is the job of each?\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\n- **Model**: talks to the database (reads and saves data).\n- **View**: shows the HTML page.\n- **Controller**: receives the request, asks the model, and picks the view.\n\n</details>\n\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\nA member opens `/members/7`. Put these steps in the right order: (a) the view prints the member's name, (b) the route matches `/members/7`, (c) the model finds member 7 in the database, (d) the controller method `show` runs.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\n**b → d → c → a.** Route, then controller, then model, then view.\n\n</details>\n\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\nThe library wants a page that lists **overdue loans**: books not returned by their due date.\n\n1. Name the route address you would use.\n2. Name the controller and method.\n3. Say which model the controller asks.\n4. Name the view file.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\n\n1. `/loans/overdue`\n2. `LoanController`, method `overdue`\n3. The `Loan` model. It finds loans whose due date has passed and that have no return date.\n4. `resources/views/loans/overdue.blade.php`\n\n</details>\n",
  "part-01-introduction/ch03-project-structure.md": "# Chapter 3: Laravel Project Structure\n\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\n\nOn your first day in a new school library, everything feels confusing. Then a kind librarian shows you around. \"Science books are here. The front desk is there. The records room is at the back.\" After the tour, you can find anything. A new Laravel project has many folders too. This chapter is your tour, so you always know where to look.\n\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\n\n- Name the main folders of a Laravel project and what goes in each.\n- Find the four MVC places: routes, controllers, models and views.\n- Use the `.env` file to connect Laravel to a MySQL database in XAMPP.\n- Run your first migration to create Laravel's starter tables.\n\n## 🗺️ The Folder Map\n\nOpen `C:\\xampp\\htdocs\\library-app` in your code editor. You will see this (some small files are left out):\n\n```text\nlibrary-app/\n├── app/\n│   ├── Http/Controllers/\n│   ├── Models/\n│   └── Providers/\n├── bootstrap/\n├── config/\n├── database/\n│   ├── factories/\n│   ├── migrations/\n│   └── seeders/\n├── public/\n├── resources/\n│   ├── css/  js/\n│   └── views/\n├── routes/\n│   ├── web.php\n│   └── console.php\n├── storage/\n├── tests/\n├── vendor/\n├── .env\n├── artisan\n└── composer.json\n```\n\n## 📂 What Each Folder Is For\n\n| Folder or file | What lives there |\n|----------------|------------------|\n| `app/` | Your app's PHP classes: controllers, models, middleware |\n| `app/Http/Controllers/` | **Controllers** (the C in MVC) |\n| `app/Models/` | **Models** (the M in MVC). A `User` model is already there |\n| `bootstrap/` | Start-up code. `bootstrap/app.php` sets up routes and middleware |\n| `config/` | Settings files, such as `database.php` and `app.php` |\n| `database/` | Migrations, seeders and factories (Part 6) |\n| `public/` | The only folder visitors reach. Holds `index.php`, images, built CSS |\n| `resources/views/` | **Views**: Blade templates (the V in MVC) |\n| `routes/web.php` | **Routes** for web pages |\n| `storage/` | Logs, uploaded files and cached views. Laravel writes here |\n| `vendor/` | Packages Composer downloaded. **Never edit it** |\n| `.env` | Secret settings for this computer |\n| `artisan` | The file behind every `php artisan` command |\n\n### Why `public/` matters\n\nEvery request enters through `public/index.php`. That file starts Laravel and hands the request to your routes. Your other folders stay hidden from visitors. This is safer, because nobody can download your `.env` file or your code. When you use `php artisan serve`, Laravel points the server at `public/` for you.\n\n## 🔑 The `.env` File\n\n`.env` (say \"dot env\") holds settings that change from one computer to another. Here are some important lines:\n\n```ini\nAPP_NAME=Laravel\nAPP_ENV=local\nAPP_KEY=base64:...a long random string...\nAPP_DEBUG=true\nAPP_URL=http://localhost\n\nDB_CONNECTION=sqlite\n```\n\n- `APP_KEY` is a secret key Laravel uses to encrypt data. Composer made it for you.\n- `APP_DEBUG=true` shows detailed error pages. That is helpful while learning. A live website must use `false`, because error pages can reveal secrets.\n\n### Keep it secret (*Amānah*)\n\nThe `.env` file holds passwords and keys. They are a trust (*amānah*) you must protect. Laravel's `.gitignore` already stops Git from uploading `.env`. Never paste it online or share it in a group chat. Share `.env.example` instead; it has the names but no secrets.\n\n## 🐬 Connect to MySQL in XAMPP\n\nA new Laravel 12 project uses **SQLite**, a database stored in one file. We want MySQL, because you already know it and phpMyAdmin shows the tables.\n\n1. Start **Apache** and **MySQL** in the XAMPP Control Panel.\n2. In `.env`, change the database lines to this:\n\n```ini\nDB_CONNECTION=mysql\nDB_HOST=127.0.0.1\nDB_PORT=3306\nDB_DATABASE=library_app\nDB_USERNAME=root\nDB_PASSWORD=\n```\n\nXAMPP's MySQL user is `root` with an empty password. That is fine on your own computer only.\n\n3. Save the file. Laravel reads `.env` on every request, so there is nothing to restart. (If you ever run `php artisan config:cache`, run `php artisan config:clear` after changing `.env`.)\n\n## 🏗️ Your First Migration\n\nA **migration** is a PHP file that creates or changes database tables. Part 6 explains migrations in full. For now, run the ones Laravel already wrote:\n\n```bash\nphp artisan migrate\n```\n\nIf the `library_app` database does not exist yet, Laravel asks:\n\n```text\n WARN  The database 'library_app' does not exist on the 'mysql' connection.\n\n ┌ Would you like to create it? ────────────────────────────────┐\n │ Yes                                                          │\n └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n```\n\nChoose **Yes**. Then you see something like this (times will differ):\n\n```text\n   INFO  Preparing database.\n\n  Creating migration table ............................. 12.30ms DONE\n\n   INFO  Running migrations.\n\n  0001_01_01_000000_create_users_table ................. 41.85ms DONE\n  0001_01_01_000001_create_cache_table ................. 12.07ms DONE\n  0001_01_01_000002_create_jobs_table .................. 33.62ms DONE\n```\n\nOpen phpMyAdmin. The `library_app` database now has tables such as `users`, `sessions`, `cache`, `jobs` and `migrations`. Laravel keeps its login sessions in the `sessions` table, so this step matters even before you write any code.\n\n## 🏠 Where the Welcome Page Comes From\n\nOpen `routes/web.php`:\n\n```php\n<?php\n\nuse Illuminate\\Support\\Facades\\Route;\n\nRoute::get('/', function () {\n    return view('welcome');\n});\n```\n\nThis route says: when someone visits `/`, show the view called `welcome`. That view is the file `resources/views/welcome.blade.php`. You will learn routes in Chapter 4.\n\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\n\n**Editing files inside `vendor/`.**\n\nA student wants to change a Laravel message, finds it in `vendor/laravel/framework/...`, and edits it. It works today. Next week, `composer update` downloads fresh files, and the change disappears. Worse, a teammate never had the change at all.\n\nFix: never edit `vendor/`. Put your own code in `app/`, `routes/`, `resources/` and `config/`. Laravel gives you proper ways to change its behaviour from there.\n\n**Wrong database name in `.env`.** If you see `SQLSTATE[HY000] [1049] Unknown database 'library_ap'`, check the spelling of `DB_DATABASE`.\n\n## ✍️ Try It\n\nSwitch your `library-app` to MySQL and run `php artisan migrate`. Then open phpMyAdmin and count the tables in `library_app`.\n\n*Hint:* if you see `SQLSTATE[HY000] [2002]`, MySQL is not running. Start it in the XAMPP Control Panel.\n\n## 📝 Recap\n\n- Routes are in `routes/web.php`, controllers in `app/Http/Controllers`, models in `app/Models`, views in `resources/views`.\n- Visitors only reach `public/`; everything else stays hidden.\n- `.env` holds this computer's settings and secrets. Never share it or commit it.\n- Set `DB_CONNECTION=mysql` and `DB_DATABASE=library_app` to use XAMPP's MySQL.\n- `php artisan migrate` creates Laravel's starter tables, including `users` and `sessions`.\n\n## 🏆 Practice\n\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\nIn which folder do you put (a) a view, (b) a controller, (c) a model? Which folder must you never edit?\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\n(a) `resources/views/`, (b) `app/Http/Controllers/`, (c) `app/Models/`. Never edit `vendor/`; Composer manages it.\n\n</details>\n\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\nWrite the six `.env` lines that connect Laravel to a MySQL database called `school_library` on XAMPP.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\n```ini\nDB_CONNECTION=mysql\nDB_HOST=127.0.0.1\nDB_PORT=3306\nDB_DATABASE=school_library\nDB_USERNAME=root\nDB_PASSWORD=\n```\n\n</details>\n\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\nYour friend wants to share their project on GitHub so you can help.\n\n1. Which file must **not** be uploaded, and why?\n2. Which file should they upload instead so you know the setting names?\n3. After you download the project, which two commands set it up? (Hint: one installs packages, one creates tables.)\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\n\n1. `.env`, because it holds secret keys and passwords. Protecting them is an *amānah*.\n2. `.env.example`. You copy it to `.env` and fill in your own values.\n3. `composer install` (downloads the `vendor/` packages) and `php artisan migrate` (creates the tables). You also run `php artisan key:generate` once to make your own `APP_KEY`.\n\n</details>\n",
  "part-02-routing/ch04-basic-routes.md": "# Chapter 4: Basic Routes\n\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\n\nAt the entrance of a big school there is a signboard. \"Library: first floor, left. Science lab: second floor. Office: ground floor.\" Without it, visitors would wander around lost. In Laravel, **routes** are that signboard. Each route says, \"If someone asks for this address, here is what to do.\"\n\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\n\n- Explain what a route is and where routes are written.\n- Write GET routes that return text, data and views.\n- Use the shortcuts `Route::view()` and `Route::redirect()`.\n- Name the main HTTP methods: GET, POST, PUT, PATCH and DELETE.\n- Connect a route to a controller method.\n\n## 🛠️ What is a Route?\n\nA **route** links a URL to some code. When a browser visits a URL, Laravel looks through your routes from top to bottom. It runs the first route that matches.\n\nAll the routes for web pages live in **`routes/web.php`**.\n\n```php\n// routes/web.php\nuse Illuminate\\Support\\Facades\\Route;\n\nRoute::get('/', function () {\n    return view('welcome');\n});\n```\n\nRead it like a sentence: \"When the browser **gets** the address `/`, run this function.\" The function is a **closure**, which means a function with no name, written right where it is used.\n\n## 📋 Basic GET Routes\n\nA route can return plain text:\n\n```php\n// routes/web.php\nRoute::get('/hello', function () {\n    return 'Welcome to the school library!';\n});\n```\n\nVisit `http://127.0.0.1:8000/hello`. The browser shows:\n\n```text\nWelcome to the school library!\n```\n\nA route can return a PHP array. Laravel turns it into **JSON** (a text format for data) for you:\n\n```php\nRoute::get('/status', function () {\n    return ['library' => 'open', 'books' => 120];\n});\n```\n\nVisit `/status`. The browser shows:\n\n```text\n{\"library\":\"open\",\"books\":120}\n```\n\n## 🖼️ Returning a View\n\nMost pages are HTML. A **view** is an HTML file in `resources/views/` whose name ends in `.blade.php`. (Blade is Laravel's template language. Chapter 10 teaches it.)\n\nCreate `resources/views/about.blade.php`:\n\n```html\n<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html>\n<head><title>About</title></head>\n<body>\n    <h1>About Our Library</h1>\n    <p>We lend books to every student, free of charge.</p>\n</body>\n</html>\n```\n\nAdd a route:\n\n```php\n// routes/web.php\nRoute::get('/about', function () {\n    return view('about');\n});\n```\n\nNotice you write `view('about')`, not `view('about.blade.php')`. Laravel adds the ending for you. Visit `/about`. The browser shows:\n\n```text\nAbout Our Library\nWe lend books to every student, free of charge.\n```\n\n### Two handy shortcuts\n\nIf a route only shows a view, use `Route::view()`:\n\n```php\nRoute::view('/rules', 'rules');   // shows resources/views/rules.blade.php\n```\n\nIf an old address should send visitors to a new one, use `Route::redirect()`:\n\n```php\nRoute::redirect('/library-home', '/');\n```\n\nVisiting `/library-home` now jumps to `/`.\n\n## 📮 HTTP Methods\n\nEvery request has a **method** that says what kind of action it is. You know GET and POST from HTML forms in PHP.\n\n| Method | Meaning | Library example |\n|--------|---------|-----------------|\n| `GET` | Read a page | Show the book list |\n| `POST` | Send new data | Add a new book |\n| `PUT` / `PATCH` | Change data | Fix a book's title |\n| `DELETE` | Remove data | Remove a lost book |\n\nLaravel has a route function for each:\n\n```php\nRoute::get('/books', ...);\nRoute::post('/books', ...);\nRoute::put('/books/1', ...);\nRoute::delete('/books/1', ...);\n```\n\nThe same address can have different routes for different methods. A GET to `/books` and a POST to `/books` run different code. GET routes must never change data. Part 7 explains why.\n\n## 🔁 Route to a Controller\n\nClosures are fine for tiny pages. Real pages use a **controller**, a class that groups related actions (Chapter 7). The route names the class and the method:\n\n```php\n// routes/web.php\nuse App\\Http\\Controllers\\HomeController;\n\nRoute::get('/', [HomeController::class, 'index']);\nRoute::get('/about', [HomeController::class, 'about']);\n```\n\n`HomeController::class` gives the full class name, `App\\Http\\Controllers\\HomeController`. The `use` line at the top lets you write the short name.\n\n## 🔍 See All Your Routes\n\nArtisan can list every route:\n\n```bash\nphp artisan route:list\n```\n\nOutput (the dots stretch to fit your window, and Laravel may list a few of its own routes too):\n\n```text\n  GET|HEAD   / ............................\n  GET|HEAD   about ........................\n  GET|HEAD   hello ........................\n  GET|HEAD   status .......................\n\n                            Showing [4] routes\n```\n\n`HEAD` is a GET request that only asks for the page's headers. Laravel adds it to every GET route.\n\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\n\n**Writing the file ending in `view()`.**\n\n```php\nreturn view('about.blade.php');   // wrong\n```\n\nWhat happens: Laravel shows an error page saying `View [about.blade.php] not found.` It reads each dot as a folder, so it looks for `about/blade/php.blade.php`.\n\n```php\nreturn view('about');             // right\n```\n\n**Forgetting the `use` line** for a controller gives `Target class [HomeController] does not exist.` Add `use App\\Http\\Controllers\\HomeController;` at the top of `routes/web.php`.\n\n## ✍️ Try It\n\nAdd three routes to your `library-app`: `/hello` returns a welcome sentence, `/status` returns an array with the opening hours, and `/about` shows a view. Visit each one.\n\n*Hint:* the array could be `['opens' => '8:00', 'closes' => '15:00']`.\n\n## 📝 Recap\n\n- Routes live in `routes/web.php` and link a URL to code.\n- `Route::get('/path', function () { ... })` answers GET requests.\n- A route can return text, an array (sent as JSON) or `view('name')`.\n- `Route::view()` and `Route::redirect()` are shortcuts.\n- `php artisan route:list` shows every route.\n\n## 🏆 Practice\n\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\nWhich file holds the web routes? What does `view('about')` look for?\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\n`routes/web.php`. `view('about')` looks for the file `resources/views/about.blade.php`.\n\n</details>\n\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\nWrite a route for `/opening-hours` that returns the text `Open 8:00 to 15:00, Sunday to Thursday.` and a route `/timings` that redirects to `/opening-hours`.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\n```php\n// routes/web.php\nRoute::get('/opening-hours', function () {\n    return 'Open 8:00 to 15:00, Sunday to Thursday.';\n});\n\nRoute::redirect('/timings', '/opening-hours');\n```\n\n</details>\n\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\nBuild the library's simple pages.\n\n1. Create views `home`, `about`, `rules` and `contact` in `resources/views/`.\n2. Add a route for each page. Use `Route::view()` where you can.\n3. Add a `/status` route that returns `['library' => 'open', 'members' => 85]`.\n4. Run `php artisan route:list` and check you see all five.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\n\n```php\n// routes/web.php\nuse Illuminate\\Support\\Facades\\Route;\n\nRoute::view('/', 'home');\nRoute::view('/about', 'about');\nRoute::view('/rules', 'rules');\nRoute::view('/contact', 'contact');\n\nRoute::get('/status', function () {\n    return ['library' => 'open', 'members' => 85];\n});\n```\n\nVisiting `/status` shows `{\"library\":\"open\",\"members\":85}`.\n\n</details>\n",
  "part-02-routing/ch05-route-parameters.md": "# Chapter 5: Route Parameters\n\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\n\nIn a library, every book has a number on its spine. You do not need a separate sign for each book. You just say, \"Please bring me book number 42,\" and the librarian finds it. The *request* stays the same; only the *number* changes. Route parameters work the same way. One route can serve book 1, book 42 and book 300.\n\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\n\n- Capture part of a URL with a **route parameter** like `{id}`.\n- Use two parameters in one route.\n- Make a parameter optional and give it a default value.\n- Limit a parameter to numbers or letters with constraints.\n\n## 🧩 Your First Parameter\n\nPut a name inside curly braces in the route's address. Laravel passes that part of the URL to your function:\n\n```php\n// routes/web.php\nRoute::get('/books/{id}', function ($id) {\n    return \"You asked for book number $id.\";\n});\n```\n\n| You visit | The browser shows |\n|-----------|-------------------|\n| `/books/1` | You asked for book number 1. |\n| `/books/42` | You asked for book number 42. |\n\nA **route parameter** is a changing part of the URL. The name in braces (`{id}`) and the function's variable (`$id`) should match. It makes the code easy to read.\n\n## 🔢 Two Parameters\n\nA route can capture more than one part:\n\n```php\nRoute::get('/members/{member}/loans/{loan}', function ($member, $loan) {\n    return \"Member $member, loan $loan.\";\n});\n```\n\nVisit `/members/7/loans/3`. The browser shows:\n\n```text\nMember 7, loan 3.\n```\n\nLaravel fills the parameters **in order**. The first `{...}` goes to the first variable.\n\n## ❓ Optional Parameters\n\nAdd `?` after the name to make a parameter optional. Then give the variable a default value:\n\n```php\nRoute::get('/shelf/{subject?}', function ($subject = 'general') {\n    return \"Showing the $subject shelf.\";\n});\n```\n\n| You visit | The browser shows |\n|-----------|-------------------|\n| `/shelf/science` | Showing the science shelf. |\n| `/shelf` | Showing the general shelf. |\n\nWithout the default value, `/shelf` would cause an error, because PHP would have no value for `$subject`.\n\n## 🛡️ Constraints: Only Allow Good Values\n\nRight now `/books/hello` also matches `/books/{id}`. That makes no sense. A book number should be a number. Add a **constraint**, a rule the parameter must follow:\n\n```php\nRoute::get('/books/{id}', function ($id) {\n    return \"You asked for book number $id.\";\n})->whereNumber('id');\n```\n\n| You visit | Result |\n|-----------|--------|\n| `/books/42` | You asked for book number 42. |\n| `/books/hello` | 404 Not Found |\n\n**404** is the HTTP code for \"page not found\". Laravel shows its own 404 page when no route matches.\n\nLaravel has a few ready-made constraints:\n\n| Method | Allows |\n|--------|--------|\n| `->whereNumber('id')` | Digits only: `42` |\n| `->whereAlpha('subject')` | Letters only: `science` |\n| `->whereAlphaNumeric('code')` | Letters and digits: `B12` |\n| `->whereIn('shelf', ['a', 'b'])` | Only the listed values |\n\nYou can also write your own pattern with `where()` and a **regular expression** (a short pattern that describes text). This one allows a grade from 6 to 10:\n\n```php\nRoute::get('/grade/{grade}', function ($grade) {\n    return \"Books for grade $grade.\";\n})->where('grade', '[6-9]|10');\n```\n\n`/grade/9` shows `Books for grade 9.` and `/grade/12` shows the 404 page.\n\n## 🧭 Order Matters\n\nLaravel checks routes from top to bottom. Look at this file:\n\n```php\nRoute::get('/books/{id}', function ($id) {\n    return \"Book $id\";\n});\n\nRoute::get('/books/new', function () {\n    return 'Add a new book';\n});\n```\n\nVisiting `/books/new` shows `Book new`. The first route matched, because `new` filled `{id}`. There are two fixes:\n\n1. Put the fixed route `/books/new` **above** the route with the parameter.\n2. Add `->whereNumber('id')`, so `new` can never match `{id}`.\n\nUsing both is the safest choice.\n\n## 🔒 Never Trust a Parameter\n\nA parameter comes from the address bar. Anyone can type anything there. Remember what you learned in PHP: never put user input straight into SQL or HTML. Constraints are a first fence. In later chapters, Eloquent (Part 5) and Blade's `{{ }}` (Part 4) add more protection.\n\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\n\n**Forgetting the default value for an optional parameter.**\n\n```php\nRoute::get('/shelf/{subject?}', function ($subject) {   // wrong\n    return \"Showing the $subject shelf.\";\n});\n```\n\nVisiting `/shelf` gives an error page: `Too few arguments to function`. Fix it with a default:\n\n```php\nRoute::get('/shelf/{subject?}', function ($subject = 'general') {   // right\n    return \"Showing the $subject shelf.\";\n});\n```\n\n**Using a parameter without braces.** `Route::get('/books/id', ...)` only matches the exact word `id`. Write `{id}`.\n\n## ✍️ Try It\n\nAdd a route `/members/{id}` that shows `Member card number 15.` for `/members/15`. Make it reject letters.\n\n*Hint:* chain `->whereNumber('id')` after the closing `)` of `Route::get`.\n\n## 📝 Recap\n\n- `{name}` in a route captures part of the URL and passes it to your function.\n- Parameters are filled in order.\n- `{name?}` makes it optional; give the variable a default value.\n- Constraints like `whereNumber()` reject bad values with a 404.\n- Fixed routes like `/books/new` go above routes with parameters.\n\n## 🏆 Practice\n\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\nWhat does `?` after a parameter name do, and what else must you add?\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\n`?` makes the parameter optional. You must also give the function's variable a default value, such as `$subject = 'general'`.\n\n</details>\n\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\nWrite a route `/authors/{name}` that shows `Books by Maryam.` for `/authors/Maryam`, and returns 404 for `/authors/123`.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\n```php\n// routes/web.php\nRoute::get('/authors/{name}', function ($name) {\n    return \"Books by $name.\";\n})->whereAlpha('name');\n```\n\n</details>\n\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\nThe library groups books by year of publishing.\n\n1. Write a route `/books/year/{year}` that shows `Books published in 2024.`\n2. Allow only four digits.\n3. Make the year optional, with `2026` as the default.\n4. Make sure `/books/42` (from this chapter) still works. Where should each route go?\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\n\n```php\n// routes/web.php\nRoute::get('/books/year/{year?}', function ($year = '2026') {\n    return \"Books published in $year.\";\n})->where('year', '[0-9]{4}');\n\nRoute::get('/books/{id}', function ($id) {\n    return \"You asked for book number $id.\";\n})->whereNumber('id');\n```\n\n`/books/year` shows `Books published in 2026.` and `/books/year/2024` shows `Books published in 2024.` The two routes do not clash, because `year` is not a number. Keeping the more specific route first is still a good habit.\n\n</details>\n",
  "part-02-routing/ch06-named-routes.md": "# Chapter 6: Named Routes\n\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\n\nYour school library moves from the first floor to a new building. If every poster in school says \"Library: Room 104\", someone must find and fix every single poster. But if the posters just say \"Library\", and only the front office map shows the room number, you change one map and you are done. **Named routes** are like that. Your pages say \"the books page\", and only one line says where it lives.\n\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\n\n- Give a route a name with `->name()`.\n- Build URLs from route names with the `route()` helper.\n- Pass parameters to a named route.\n- Redirect to a named route.\n- Group routes that share a URL prefix and a name prefix.\n\n## 🏷️ Naming a Route\n\nAdd `->name('...')` to the end of a route:\n\n```php\n// routes/web.php\nRoute::get('/books', function () {\n    return 'All library books';\n})->name('books.index');\n\nRoute::get('/books/{id}', function ($id) {\n    return \"Book $id\";\n})->name('books.show');\n```\n\nNames usually use dots: **thing.action**. So `books.index` means \"the list of books\" and `books.show` means \"one book\". Chapter 8 uses exactly these names, so it is a good habit to start now.\n\n## 🔗 Making URLs with `route()`\n\nThe `route()` helper turns a name into a full URL:\n\n```php\nRoute::get('/test-links', function () {\n    return route('books.index');\n});\n```\n\nVisit `/test-links` while `php artisan serve` is running. The browser shows:\n\n```text\nhttp://127.0.0.1:8000/books\n```\n\n### Passing parameters\n\nIf the route has a parameter, give its value as the second argument. Use an array with the parameter's name:\n\n```php\nreturn route('books.show', ['id' => 42]);\n```\n\nOutput:\n\n```text\nhttp://127.0.0.1:8000/books/42\n```\n\nIf the route has only one parameter, you can pass just the value: `route('books.show', 42)` gives the same URL.\n\n### In a view\n\nInside a Blade view, `{{ ... }}` prints a PHP value (Chapter 10 explains it fully). Use it to build links:\n\n```blade\n<a href=\"{{ route('books.index') }}\">All books</a>\n<a href=\"{{ route('books.show', ['id' => 42]) }}\">Book 42</a>\n```\n\nThe browser receives:\n\n```html\n<a href=\"http://127.0.0.1:8000/books\">All books</a>\n<a href=\"http://127.0.0.1:8000/books/42\">Book 42</a>\n```\n\n## 🔁 Why Names Beat Typed URLs\n\nSuppose the library decides that `/books` should become `/catalogue`. You change one line:\n\n```php\nRoute::get('/catalogue', function () {\n    return 'All library books';\n})->name('books.index');\n```\n\nEvery `route('books.index')` in your app now gives `http://127.0.0.1:8000/catalogue`. No link breaks. If you had typed `href=\"/books\"` in twenty views, you would need to find and fix all twenty.\n\n## ↪️ Redirecting to a Named Route\n\nAfter an action, you often send the visitor to another page. Use `redirect()->route()`:\n\n```php\nRoute::get('/old-catalogue', function () {\n    return redirect()->route('books.index');\n});\n```\n\nVisiting `/old-catalogue` jumps to `/books`. Later, after saving a new book, you will write `return redirect()->route('books.index');` many times.\n\n## 📦 Route Groups\n\nLibrarian pages often share the same start, like `/librarian/...`. A **route group** lets you write the shared parts once:\n\n```php\nRoute::prefix('librarian')->name('librarian.')->group(function () {\n    Route::get('/reports', function () {\n        return 'Monthly reports';\n    })->name('reports');\n\n    Route::get('/overdue', function () {\n        return 'Overdue books';\n    })->name('overdue');\n});\n```\n\n| Name | URL |\n|------|-----|\n| `librarian.reports` | `/librarian/reports` |\n| `librarian.overdue` | `/librarian/overdue` |\n\n`prefix()` adds to the URL. `name()` adds to the start of each name. Notice the dot at the end of `'librarian.'`. Chapter 9 adds a third shared part to groups: middleware.\n\n## 🔍 Check Names with `route:list`\n\n```bash\nphp artisan route:list --name=books\n```\n\nOutput (spacing depends on your window):\n\n```text\n  GET|HEAD   books ................ books.index\n  GET|HEAD   books/{id} ........... books.show\n\n                            Showing [2] routes\n```\n\nThe name appears on the right. The `--name` option shows only routes whose name contains `books`.\n\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\n\n**Using a name that does not exist.**\n\n```php\nreturn route('book.index');   // typo: \"book\", not \"books\"\n```\n\nWhat happens: Laravel shows an error page: `Route [book.index] not defined.` Check the exact name with `php artisan route:list`.\n\n**Forgetting a required parameter.**\n\n```php\nreturn route('books.show');   // which book?\n```\n\nWhat happens: `Missing required parameter for [Route: books.show] [URI: books/{id}] [Missing parameter: id].` Pass the value: `route('books.show', ['id' => 42])`.\n\n## ✍️ Try It\n\nName your `/about` and `/rules` routes `about` and `rules`. Then add a route `/links` that returns both URLs joined with a space.\n\n*Hint:* `return route('about') . ' ' . route('rules');`\n\n## 📝 Recap\n\n- `->name('books.index')` gives a route a name.\n- `route('name')` builds the URL; pass parameters as a second argument.\n- `redirect()->route('name')` sends the visitor to a named route.\n- Names keep links working when URLs change.\n- `Route::prefix()->name()->group()` shares a URL start and name start.\n\n## 🏆 Practice\n\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\nWhat does `route('books.show', ['id' => 5])` return while `php artisan serve` is running, if the route is `/books/{id}`?\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\n`http://127.0.0.1:8000/books/5`\n\n</details>\n\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\nWrite a route `/members/{id}` named `members.show`, and a Blade link to member 12.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\n```php\n// routes/web.php\nRoute::get('/members/{id}', function ($id) {\n    return \"Member $id\";\n})->whereNumber('id')->name('members.show');\n```\n\n```blade\n<a href=\"{{ route('members.show', ['id' => 12]) }}\">Member 12</a>\n```\n\n</details>\n\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\nBuild the librarian area with a group.\n\n1. Use the prefix `librarian` and the name prefix `librarian.`.\n2. Add `/dashboard` (named `librarian.dashboard`) and `/returns` (named `librarian.returns`).\n3. Add a route `/staff` outside the group that redirects to the librarian dashboard by name.\n4. Change the prefix to `staff-area`. What happens to `/staff`?\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\n\n```php\n// routes/web.php\nRoute::prefix('librarian')->name('librarian.')->group(function () {\n    Route::get('/dashboard', function () {\n        return 'Librarian dashboard';\n    })->name('dashboard');\n\n    Route::get('/returns', function () {\n        return 'Books returned today';\n    })->name('returns');\n});\n\nRoute::get('/staff', function () {\n    return redirect()->route('librarian.dashboard');\n});\n```\n\n4. After the change, `/staff` redirects to `/staff-area/dashboard`. You did not touch the redirect, because it uses the name.\n\n</details>\n",
  "part-03-controllers/ch07-creating-controllers.md": "# Chapter 7: Creating Controllers\n\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\n\nWhen the library was small, one teacher handled everything from her own desk. Now the library has hundreds of books and a whole school of members. So it has a proper **front desk** with trays: one for new books, one for returns, one for questions. Each request goes to the right tray. Routes written as closures are like the small desk. A **controller** is the proper front desk, with one method for each kind of job.\n\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\n\n- Explain why we move code from route closures into controllers.\n- Create a controller with `php artisan make:controller`.\n- Write controller methods and connect routes to them.\n- Pass data from a controller to a view and print it.\n- Stop with a 404 page when something is not found, using `abort()`.\n\n## 🗂️ Why Controllers?\n\nIn Part 2 you wrote code inside routes:\n\n```php\nRoute::get('/books', function () {\n    return 'All library books';\n});\n```\n\nThat is fine for five routes. A real app has fifty. If all the code sits in `routes/web.php`, the file becomes a long, messy list. A **controller** is a PHP class that groups the actions for one part of the app. `BookController` holds the book actions, and `MemberController` holds the member actions. The routes file stays short: it only says which method handles which URL.\n\n## 🛠️ Make a Controller\n\nArtisan writes the empty class for you:\n\n```bash\nphp artisan make:controller BookController\n```\n\nOutput (the path is the full path on your computer):\n\n```text\n   INFO  Controller [C:\\xampp\\htdocs\\library-app\\app\\Http\\Controllers\\BookController.php] created successfully.\n```\n\nOpen the new file:\n\n```php\n<?php\n\nnamespace App\\Http\\Controllers;\n\nuse Illuminate\\Http\\Request;\n\nclass BookController extends Controller\n{\n    //\n}\n```\n\n- `namespace` is the class's \"address\" inside the app (you met namespaces in PHP OOP).\n- `extends Controller` means it inherits from Laravel's base `Controller` class.\n- Controller names are singular and end in `Controller`: `BookController`, not `BooksController`.\n\n## ✏️ Add Methods\n\nEach public method is an **action**. We have no database yet, so we keep a small sample list inside the class. Part 5 replaces it with real data.\n\n```php\n// app/Http/Controllers/BookController.php\nclass BookController extends Controller\n{\n    private array $books = [\n        1 => ['title' => 'Clean Water for Every Village', 'author' => 'Aisha Rahman'],\n        2 => ['title' => 'The Story of Numbers', 'author' => 'Yusuf Ali Khan'],\n        3 => ['title' => 'Bees and Their Hives', 'author' => 'Maryam Siddiqui'],\n    ];\n\n    public function index()\n    {\n        return view('books.index', [\n            'count' => count($this->books),\n        ]);\n    }\n\n    public function show($id)\n    {\n        if (! isset($this->books[$id])) {\n            abort(404);\n        }\n\n        return view('books.show', [\n            'book' => $this->books[$id],\n        ]);\n    }\n}\n```\n\n`abort(404)` stops the request and shows Laravel's \"404 Not Found\" page. Use it whenever a visitor asks for something that does not exist.\n\n## 🔗 Connect the Routes\n\n```php\n// routes/web.php\nuse App\\Http\\Controllers\\BookController;\n\nRoute::get('/books', [BookController::class, 'index'])->name('books.index');\nRoute::get('/books/{id}', [BookController::class, 'show'])\n    ->whereNumber('id')\n    ->name('books.show');\n```\n\nThe route parameter `{id}` arrives as the method's `$id`, just like with closures.\n\n## 🖼️ Pass Data to a View\n\nThe second argument of `view()` is an array. Each **key** becomes a variable inside the view: the key `'book'` becomes `$book`.\n\nViews can live in folders. `view('books.show')` means the file `resources/views/books/show.blade.php`. The dot stands for a folder.\n\nInside a view, `{{ $variable }}` prints a value safely. Chapter 10 explains it in detail.\n\n```blade\n{{-- resources/views/books/show.blade.php --}}\n<h1>{{ $book['title'] }}</h1>\n<p>Written by {{ $book['author'] }}</p>\n<a href=\"{{ route('books.index') }}\">Back to all books</a>\n```\n\n```blade\n{{-- resources/views/books/index.blade.php --}}\n<h1>Library Books</h1>\n<p>We have {{ $count }} titles on the shelf.</p>\n```\n\nVisit `/books/2`. The browser shows:\n\n```text\nThe Story of Numbers\nWritten by Yusuf Ali Khan\nBack to all books\n```\n\nVisit `/books`. The browser shows:\n\n```text\nLibrary Books\nWe have 3 titles on the shelf.\n```\n\nVisit `/books/9`. You see the **404 Not Found** page.\n\n### A shortcut: `compact()`\n\nPHP's `compact()` builds the same array from variable names:\n\n```php\n$book = $this->books[$id];\nreturn view('books.show', compact('book'));   // same as ['book' => $book]\n```\n\n## 🎯 Single-Action Controllers\n\nSome controllers do only one job, like showing opening hours. Make an **invokable** controller. It has one method called `__invoke`:\n\n```bash\nphp artisan make:controller OpeningHoursController --invokable\n```\n\n```php\n// app/Http/Controllers/OpeningHoursController.php\npublic function __invoke()\n{\n    return 'Open 8:00 to 15:00, Sunday to Thursday.';\n}\n```\n\n```php\n// routes/web.php\nuse App\\Http\\Controllers\\OpeningHoursController;\n\nRoute::get('/hours', OpeningHoursController::class);\n```\n\nThere is no method name in the route, because there is only one.\n\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\n\n**Variable name in the view does not match the key.**\n\n```php\nreturn view('books.show', ['item' => $this->books[$id]]);\n```\n\n```blade\n<h1>{{ $book['title'] }}</h1>\n```\n\nWhat happens: an error page with `Undefined variable $book`. The key was `item`, so the view only has `$item`. Make them match:\n\n```php\nreturn view('books.show', ['book' => $this->books[$id]]);\n```\n\n**Missing `use` line** in `routes/web.php` gives `Target class [BookController] does not exist.`\n\n## ✍️ Try It\n\nCreate a `MemberController` with an `index` method that passes `'school' => 'Crescent Valley School'` to a view `members.index`. The view prints `Members of Crescent Valley School`.\n\n*Hint:* the view file is `resources/views/members/index.blade.php`.\n\n## 📝 Recap\n\n- A controller is a class in `app/Http/Controllers` that groups related actions.\n- `php artisan make:controller BookController` creates one.\n- Routes point to methods: `[BookController::class, 'index']`.\n- `view('books.show', ['book' => $book])` passes data; the key becomes `$book` in the view.\n- `abort(404)` shows the \"Not Found\" page.\n\n## 🏆 Practice\n\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\nWhich file does `view('members.show')` load? Which Artisan command creates `MemberController`?\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\n`resources/views/members/show.blade.php`. The command is `php artisan make:controller MemberController`.\n\n</details>\n\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\nAdd a method `about` to `BookController` that returns the view `books.about` with a variable `$shelves` set to `12`. Write the route `/books/about`. Where must this route go in the file?\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\n```php\n// app/Http/Controllers/BookController.php\npublic function about()\n{\n    return view('books.about', ['shelves' => 12]);\n}\n```\n\n```php\n// routes/web.php\nRoute::get('/books/about', [BookController::class, 'about'])->name('books.about');\n```\n\nPut it **above** `/books/{id}`. Our `{id}` route also has `whereNumber`, so `about` cannot match it anyway, but fixed routes first is a safe habit.\n\n</details>\n\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\nBuild a `MemberController` with sample data.\n\n1. Keep a private array of three members (name and grade), with keys 1, 2 and 3. Use names such as Bilal Ahmed, Zainab Hussain and Ibrahim Noor.\n2. Add `index` (shows how many members) and `show` (shows one member, or 404).\n3. Add named routes `members.index` and `members.show`.\n4. In the `show` view, add a link back to the list using `route()`.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\n\n```php\n// app/Http/Controllers/MemberController.php\nclass MemberController extends Controller\n{\n    private array $members = [\n        1 => ['name' => 'Bilal Ahmed', 'grade' => 9],\n        2 => ['name' => 'Zainab Hussain', 'grade' => 10],\n        3 => ['name' => 'Ibrahim Noor', 'grade' => 9],\n    ];\n\n    public function index()\n    {\n        return view('members.index', ['count' => count($this->members)]);\n    }\n\n    public function show($id)\n    {\n        if (! isset($this->members[$id])) {\n            abort(404);\n        }\n        return view('members.show', ['member' => $this->members[$id]]);\n    }\n}\n```\n\n```php\n// routes/web.php\nuse App\\Http\\Controllers\\MemberController;\n\nRoute::get('/members', [MemberController::class, 'index'])->name('members.index');\nRoute::get('/members/{id}', [MemberController::class, 'show'])\n    ->whereNumber('id')->name('members.show');\n```\n\n```blade\n{{-- resources/views/members/show.blade.php --}}\n<h1>{{ $member['name'] }}</h1>\n<p>Grade {{ $member['grade'] }}</p>\n<a href=\"{{ route('members.index') }}\">All members</a>\n```\n\n</details>\n",
  "part-03-controllers/ch08-resource-controllers.md": "# Chapter 8: Resource Controllers\n\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\n\nEvery library in the world does the same few things with its books. It shows the list of books. It adds a new book. It shows one book's details. It fixes a book's record. It removes a lost book. Because these jobs are always the same, librarians use the same forms for them everywhere. Laravel noticed this too. A **resource controller** gives you all these standard jobs in one go, with standard names.\n\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\n\n- Explain the idea of **CRUD**: Create, Read, Update, Delete.\n- Create a resource controller with `--resource`.\n- Register all seven routes with one line: `Route::resource()`.\n- Read the table of the seven actions, their URLs and route names.\n- Keep only the actions you need with `only()` and `except()`.\n\n## 🔄 CRUD: The Four Jobs\n\nAlmost every part of a data app does four things. Together they are called **CRUD**:\n\n| Letter | Job | Library example |\n|--------|-----|-----------------|\n| **C** | Create | Add a new book |\n| **R** | Read | List books, view one book |\n| **U** | Update | Fix a misspelled author |\n| **D** | Delete | Remove a book that was lost |\n\nBooks need CRUD. Members need CRUD. Loans need CRUD. Writing every route and method by hand would be slow and easy to get wrong.\n\n## 🛠️ Make a Resource Controller\n\nAdd `--resource` to the Artisan command. (If you already made `BookController` in Chapter 7, delete that file first, or use a new name to practise.)\n\n```bash\nphp artisan make:controller BookController --resource\n```\n\nOutput:\n\n```text\n   INFO  Controller [C:\\xampp\\htdocs\\library-app\\app\\Http\\Controllers\\BookController.php] created successfully.\n```\n\nThe new file already has seven empty methods (comments shortened here):\n\n```php\nclass BookController extends Controller\n{\n    public function index() { }                                  // list all\n    public function create() { }                                 // show \"add\" form\n    public function store(Request $request) { }                  // save new\n    public function show(string $id) { }                         // show one\n    public function edit(string $id) { }                         // show \"edit\" form\n    public function update(Request $request, string $id) { }     // save changes\n    public function destroy(string $id) { }                      // delete\n}\n```\n\n`Request $request` gives the method the submitted form data. You will use it in Part 7.\n\n## 🔗 One Line, Seven Routes\n\n```php\n// routes/web.php\nuse App\\Http\\Controllers\\BookController;\n\nRoute::resource('books', BookController::class);\n```\n\nThat one line creates all of these:\n\n| Method | URL | Action | Route name |\n|--------|-----|--------|------------|\n| GET | `/books` | `index` | `books.index` |\n| GET | `/books/create` | `create` | `books.create` |\n| POST | `/books` | `store` | `books.store` |\n| GET | `/books/{book}` | `show` | `books.show` |\n| GET | `/books/{book}/edit` | `edit` | `books.edit` |\n| PUT/PATCH | `/books/{book}` | `update` | `books.update` |\n| DELETE | `/books/{book}` | `destroy` | `books.destroy` |\n\nNotice three things:\n\n1. The parameter is called `{book}`, the singular of `books`.\n2. `create` and `edit` only **show forms**. `store` and `update` **save** what the forms send.\n3. The same URL `/books/{book}` does three jobs, decided by the HTTP method: GET shows, PUT updates, DELETE removes.\n\nCheck it with Artisan:\n\n```bash\nphp artisan route:list --name=books\n```\n\nOutput (spacing depends on your window):\n\n```text\n  GET|HEAD   books ......... books.index › BookController@index\n  POST       books ......... books.store › BookController@store\n  GET|HEAD   books/create  books.create › BookController@create\n  GET|HEAD   books/{book} .... books.show › BookController@show\n  PUT|PATCH  books/{book}  books.update › BookController@update\n  DELETE     books/{book}  books.destroy › BookController@destroy\n  GET|HEAD   books/{book}/edit . books.edit › BookController@edit\n\n                            Showing [7] routes\n```\n\n## ✏️ Fill In Two Methods\n\nWe still have no database, so return simple text to test the routes:\n\n```php\n// app/Http/Controllers/BookController.php\npublic function index()\n{\n    return 'List of all books';\n}\n\npublic function show(string $id)\n{\n    return \"Details of book $id\";\n}\n```\n\nVisit `/books/3`. The browser shows:\n\n```text\nDetails of book 3\n```\n\n## ✂️ Only the Actions You Need\n\nThe library has no page to edit members' loan history, and loans are never deleted (they are records). Keep only what you need:\n\n```php\nRoute::resource('loans', LoanController::class)->only(['index', 'create', 'store', 'show']);\n```\n\nOr list the ones to leave out:\n\n```php\nRoute::resource('members', MemberController::class)->except(['destroy']);\n```\n\nUse **only** when you need a few actions, and **except** when you need most of them.\n\n### Add a model hint\n\nWhen you have models (Part 5), add `--model=Book`. Then the methods receive a `Book` object instead of a plain `$id`:\n\n```bash\nphp artisan make:controller BookController --resource --model=Book\n```\n\nYou will use this in Chapter 14.\n\n## 🌐 But Forms Only Send GET and POST\n\nAn HTML form can only use `method=\"GET\"` or `method=\"POST\"`. So how does a browser send PUT or DELETE? Laravel's answer is a hidden field written with `@method('PUT')` or `@method('DELETE')` inside a POST form. Chapter 19 shows it step by step.\n\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\n\n**Adding a custom route below the resource.**\n\n```php\nRoute::resource('books', BookController::class);\nRoute::get('/books/popular', [BookController::class, 'popular']);   // never reached\n```\n\nWhat happens: visiting `/books/popular` runs `show` with `$id = 'popular'`, because `/books/{book}` matched first. Put extra routes **above** the resource line:\n\n```php\nRoute::get('/books/popular', [BookController::class, 'popular'])->name('books.popular');\nRoute::resource('books', BookController::class);\n```\n\n**Plural controller URLs, singular controller names.** It is `Route::resource('books', BookController::class)`, not `'book'` and not `BooksController`.\n\n## ✍️ Try It\n\nCreate `MemberController` with `--resource`, register it with `Route::resource('members', ...)` but without `destroy`, and run `php artisan route:list --name=members`. Count the routes.\n\n*Hint:* you should see six routes.\n\n## 📝 Recap\n\n- CRUD means Create, Read, Update, Delete.\n- `php artisan make:controller BookController --resource` writes the seven CRUD methods.\n- `Route::resource('books', BookController::class)` registers all seven routes with standard names.\n- `create`/`edit` show forms; `store`/`update` save them; `destroy` deletes.\n- `->only([...])` and `->except([...])` trim the list.\n\n## 🏆 Practice\n\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\nName the seven resource actions in order, and say which two only show forms.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\n`index`, `create`, `store`, `show`, `edit`, `update`, `destroy`. The two that only show forms are `create` and `edit`.\n\n</details>\n\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\nFill the table for `Route::resource('members', MemberController::class)`: what are the method and URL for `members.update` and `members.create`?\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\n- `members.update`: PUT or PATCH to `/members/{member}`\n- `members.create`: GET to `/members/create`\n\n</details>\n\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\nPlan the loan routes.\n\n1. Loans can be listed, created and shown, but not edited or deleted.\n2. There is also a page `/loans/overdue` handled by `LoanController@overdue`, named `loans.overdue`.\n3. Write the routes in the right order.\n4. Write how many routes `php artisan route:list --name=loans` should show.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\n\n```php\n// routes/web.php\nuse App\\Http\\Controllers\\LoanController;\n\nRoute::get('/loans/overdue', [LoanController::class, 'overdue'])->name('loans.overdue');\nRoute::resource('loans', LoanController::class)->only(['index', 'create', 'store', 'show']);\n```\n\nIt shows **5** routes: `loans.overdue`, `loans.index`, `loans.create`, `loans.store` and `loans.show`.\n\n</details>\n",
  "part-03-controllers/ch09-middleware-basics.md": "# Chapter 9: Middleware Basics\n\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\n\nBefore you reach the library's front desk, you pass the **door attendant**. He checks a few things. Is the library open right now? Do you have your library card? Are you carrying food that could damage the books? If something is wrong, he politely stops you at the door. If all is well, he lets you walk on to the desk. In Laravel, that door attendant is called **middleware**.\n\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\n\n- Explain what middleware is and where it sits in the request journey.\n- Name a few middleware that Laravel already has, such as `auth` and `throttle`.\n- Create your own middleware with `php artisan make:middleware`.\n- Attach middleware to one route or to a group of routes.\n- Give your middleware a short alias in `bootstrap/app.php`.\n\n## 🚪 Where Middleware Sits\n\n**Middleware** is code that runs **before** (and sometimes after) your controller. It can let the request continue, or stop it and send a different response.\n\n```text\nBrowser request\n      |\n      v\n+-------------+\n| Route match |\n+-------------+\n      |\n      v\n+-------------+   stop? -> \"Closed\" page\n| Middleware  |----------------------->\n+-------------+\n      | continue\n      v\n+-------------+\n| Controller  |\n+-------------+\n      |\n      v\n   Response\n```\n\nMany middleware can stand in a line, like several checkpoints. The request passes through each one in turn.\n\n## 🧰 Middleware Laravel Already Has\n\nYou will use these often:\n\n| Alias | What it checks |\n|-------|----------------|\n| `auth` | Is the visitor logged in? If not, send them to the login page |\n| `guest` | Is the visitor logged **out**? (For login and register pages) |\n| `verified` | Has the user confirmed their email address? |\n| `throttle:60,1` | No more than 60 requests per minute |\n\nLaravel also runs some middleware on **every** web route automatically. One starts the **session** (the memory of a visitor between pages). Another checks **CSRF tokens** on forms (Chapter 21).\n\nUsing built-in middleware takes one word:\n\n```php\n// routes/web.php\nRoute::get('/librarian/reports', [ReportController::class, 'index'])\n    ->middleware('auth');\n```\n\nWe have no login yet; Part 8 adds it. Today we build our own door attendant.\n\n## 🛠️ Make Your Own Middleware\n\nThe library desk is open from 8:00 to 15:00. Outside those hours, the borrowing pages should show a polite message.\n\n```bash\nphp artisan make:middleware EnsureLibraryIsOpen\n```\n\nOutput:\n\n```text\n   INFO  Middleware [C:\\xampp\\htdocs\\library-app\\app\\Http\\Middleware\\EnsureLibraryIsOpen.php] created successfully.\n```\n\nOpen the file and fill in `handle`:\n\n```php\n<?php\n\nnamespace App\\Http\\Middleware;\n\nuse Closure;\nuse Illuminate\\Http\\Request;\nuse Symfony\\Component\\HttpFoundation\\Response;\n\nclass EnsureLibraryIsOpen\n{\n    public function handle(Request $request, Closure $next): Response\n    {\n        $hour = now()->hour;   // 0 to 23\n\n        if ($hour < 8 || $hour >= 15) {\n            return response('The library desk is closed. Please come back at 8:00.', 403);\n        }\n\n        return $next($request);\n    }\n}\n```\n\nRead it slowly:\n\n- `handle()` runs for every request that uses this middleware.\n- `now()` is a Laravel helper that gives the current date and time. `->hour` is the hour as a number.\n- If the desk is closed, we **return a response** straight away. The controller never runs. `403` is the HTTP code for \"Forbidden\".\n- `return $next($request);` means \"all is well, pass the request on\". Forgetting this line is the most common middleware bug.\n\n### Check the clock\n\n`now()` uses the timezone in `config/app.php`, which is `'UTC'` by default. Set it to your own, for example `'timezone' => 'Asia/Kolkata'`, or the hours will be wrong.\n\n## 🔗 Attach It to Routes\n\nYou can use the class name directly:\n\n```php\n// routes/web.php\nuse App\\Http\\Middleware\\EnsureLibraryIsOpen;\n\nRoute::get('/borrow', [LoanController::class, 'create'])\n    ->middleware(EnsureLibraryIsOpen::class);\n```\n\nVisit `/borrow` at 16:30. The browser shows:\n\n```text\nThe library desk is closed. Please come back at 8:00.\n```\n\nVisit it at 10:00, and the controller runs as normal.\n\n### Give it a short alias\n\nLong class names are tiring to type. Register an **alias** (a short nickname) in `bootstrap/app.php`. Find the `withMiddleware` part and add this:\n\n```php\n// bootstrap/app.php\n->withMiddleware(function (Middleware $middleware): void {\n    $middleware->alias([\n        'library.open' => \\App\\Http\\Middleware\\EnsureLibraryIsOpen::class,\n    ]);\n})\n```\n\nNow you can write `->middleware('library.open')`.\n\n### Protect a whole group\n\nAdd middleware to a group, and every route inside gets it:\n\n```php\nRoute::middleware('library.open')->group(function () {\n    Route::get('/borrow', [LoanController::class, 'create'])->name('loans.create');\n    Route::post('/borrow', [LoanController::class, 'store'])->name('loans.store');\n});\n```\n\nYou can join it with the prefix and name you learned in Chapter 6:\n\n```php\nRoute::middleware('library.open')\n    ->prefix('desk')\n    ->name('desk.')\n    ->group(function () {\n        // routes...\n    });\n```\n\nYou can also list several middleware: `->middleware(['auth', 'library.open'])`. They run in the order you list them.\n\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\n\n**Forgetting `return $next($request);`.**\n\n```php\npublic function handle(Request $request, Closure $next): Response\n{\n    if (now()->hour < 8) {\n        return response('Closed.', 403);\n    }\n    // forgot to pass the request on\n}\n```\n\nWhat happens: during opening hours you get an error page, because `handle()` returned nothing instead of a `Response`. Always end with `return $next($request);`.\n\n**Using an alias you never registered.** `->middleware('library-open')` (dash instead of dot) gives `Target class [library-open] does not exist.` Check the spelling in `bootstrap/app.php`.\n\n## ✍️ Try It\n\nChange `EnsureLibraryIsOpen` so the desk is also closed on Fridays. Test it by temporarily changing the condition.\n\n*Hint:* `now()->isFriday()` returns `true` on Fridays.\n\n## 📝 Recap\n\n- Middleware runs before the controller and can stop a request.\n- Built-in aliases include `auth`, `guest`, `verified` and `throttle`.\n- `php artisan make:middleware Name` creates a class with a `handle()` method.\n- Return a response to stop; `return $next($request);` to continue.\n- Attach with `->middleware(...)` on a route or a group; add aliases in `bootstrap/app.php`.\n\n## 🏆 Practice\n\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\nWhat does `return $next($request);` do in middleware?\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\nIt passes the request on to the next middleware, or to the controller if this was the last one. Without it, the request never reaches the controller.\n\n</details>\n\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\nWrite a route group that applies the `library.open` middleware to `/borrow` and `/return`, both handled by `LoanController` (methods `create` and `returnForm`).\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\n```php\n// routes/web.php\nRoute::middleware('library.open')->group(function () {\n    Route::get('/borrow', [LoanController::class, 'create'])->name('loans.create');\n    Route::get('/return', [LoanController::class, 'returnForm'])->name('loans.return');\n});\n```\n\n</details>\n\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\nDuring exam week, the library is for **quiet study only**, and borrowing stops.\n\n1. Create middleware `EnsureNotExamWeek`.\n2. Read a setting from `.env` with `env('EXAM_WEEK')`. (Strictly, Laravel prefers reading `.env` through a config file, but `env()` is fine for practice.)\n3. If it is `true`, return `Borrowing is paused during exam week. Quiet study is welcome!` with code 403.\n4. Give it the alias `not.exam` and add it to the borrowing group from Level 2.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\n\n```php\n// app/Http/Middleware/EnsureNotExamWeek.php\npublic function handle(Request $request, Closure $next): Response\n{\n    if (env('EXAM_WEEK') === true) {\n        return response('Borrowing is paused during exam week. Quiet study is welcome!', 403);\n    }\n\n    return $next($request);\n}\n```\n\n```ini\n# .env\nEXAM_WEEK=true\n```\n\n```php\n// bootstrap/app.php\n$middleware->alias([\n    'library.open' => \\App\\Http\\Middleware\\EnsureLibraryIsOpen::class,\n    'not.exam' => \\App\\Http\\Middleware\\EnsureNotExamWeek::class,\n]);\n```\n\n```php\n// routes/web.php\nRoute::middleware(['library.open', 'not.exam'])->group(function () {\n    // borrowing routes\n});\n```\n\nLaravel's `env()` turns the text `true` into the PHP value `true`, so `=== true` works.\n\n</details>\n",
  "part-04-blade-templates/ch10-blade-syntax.md": "# Chapter 10: Blade Syntax\n\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\n\nThe library prints a **reading certificate** for every student who finishes ten books. The certificate design is the same for everyone. Only a few blanks change: the name, the class and the date. The librarian does not redesign the certificate each time. She fills in the blanks. A **Blade template** is that certificate design. Your controller fills in the blanks.\n\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\n\n- Explain what Blade is and where Blade files live.\n- Print variables and PHP expressions with `{{ }}`.\n- Explain why `{{ }}` is safe, and when (rarely) to use `{!! !!}`.\n- Write Blade comments that never reach the browser.\n- Recognise Blade **directives** like `@if` and `@foreach`.\n\n## 📋 What is Blade?\n\n**Blade** is Laravel's **template engine**: a tool that mixes HTML with small pieces of data. Blade files:\n\n- end in **`.blade.php`**,\n- live in **`resources/views/`**,\n- are loaded with `view('name')`.\n\nBehind the scenes, Laravel turns each Blade file into plain PHP and saves it in `storage/framework/views/`. That is why Blade is fast. You never edit those saved files.\n\n## 🖨️ Printing Data with `{{ }}`\n\nDouble curly braces print a value. The controller passes the data:\n\n```php\n// app/Http/Controllers/CertificateController.php\npublic function show()\n{\n    return view('certificate', [\n        'name' => 'Aisha',\n        'grade' => 9,\n        'books' => 10,\n    ]);\n}\n```\n\n```blade\n{{-- resources/views/certificate.blade.php --}}\n<h1>Reading Certificate</h1>\n<p>This is to certify that {{ $name }} of Grade {{ $grade }}</p>\n<p>has read {{ $books }} books this term.</p>\n```\n\nThe browser shows:\n\n```text\nReading Certificate\nThis is to certify that Aisha of Grade 9\nhas read 10 books this term.\n```\n\n### Any PHP expression works\n\nInside `{{ }}` you can put any PHP **expression** (a piece of code that gives a value):\n\n```blade\n<p>{{ strtoupper($name) }}</p>\n<p>Next target: {{ $books + 5 }} books</p>\n<p>Total pages read: {{ number_format(1250) }}</p>\n<p>Library: {{ $library ?? 'Crescent Valley School Library' }}</p>\n```\n\nOutput (here `$library` was not passed):\n\n```text\nAISHA\nNext target: 15 books\nTotal pages read: 1,250\nLibrary: Crescent Valley School Library\n```\n\nThe `??` operator from PHP gives a default when a variable is missing or `null`.\n\n## 🛡️ Why `{{ }}` is Safe\n\nImagine a book review form. A visitor types this as their review:\n\n```text\n<b>Science Fair</b>\n```\n\nIf you printed it as raw HTML, the browser would follow the tags. A harmful visitor could type a `<script>` tag instead, and run their code in other people's browsers. This attack is called **XSS** (cross-site scripting).\n\n`{{ }}` **escapes** the text first. Escaping turns special characters like `<` into safe codes like `&lt;`. So this view:\n\n```blade\n<p>{{ $review }}</p>\n```\n\nsends this HTML:\n\n```html\n<p>&lt;b&gt;Science Fair&lt;/b&gt;</p>\n```\n\nand the browser shows the tags as plain text:\n\n```text\n<b>Science Fair</b>\n```\n\nNothing runs. Behind the scenes `{{ $review }}` becomes `<?php echo e($review); ?>`. The `e()` helper calls PHP's `htmlspecialchars()`, which you met in the PHP book.\n\n### `{!! !!}`: only for trusted HTML\n\nSometimes you *want* HTML to work, such as a notice that **you** wrote with bold text:\n\n```blade\n{!! $noticeHtml !!}\n```\n\n`{!! !!}` prints **without** escaping. Use it only for HTML that you or your app made and fully trust. **Never** use it for anything a visitor typed: names, reviews, search words, form fields. When unsure, use `{{ }}`.\n\n## 💬 Blade Comments\n\n```blade\n{{-- This note is for developers only. --}}\n<!-- This HTML comment is sent to the browser. -->\n```\n\nA **Blade comment** `{{-- --}}` is removed before the page is sent. Visitors cannot see it, even with \"View Source\". An HTML comment `<!-- -->` *is* sent, so never put secrets in it.\n\n## 🧭 Directives: A Preview\n\nWords that start with `@` are **directives**: short Blade commands that stand for PHP code.\n\n```blade\n@if ($books >= 10)\n    <p>Well done! You earned the certificate.</p>\n@else\n    <p>Keep reading. Only {{ 10 - $books }} more to go!</p>\n@endif\n```\n\nWith `$books = 10`, the browser shows:\n\n```text\nWell done! You earned the certificate.\n```\n\nEvery opening directive has a matching end: `@if` ... `@endif`, `@foreach` ... `@endforeach`. Chapter 12 covers loops and conditions in depth. Chapter 11 covers layouts.\n\n### Printing curly braces\n\nSome JavaScript tools also use `{{ }}`. To print the braces themselves, add `@` in front: `@{{ name }}` shows `{{ name }}` in the browser, and Blade leaves it alone.\n\n## 🔄 Plain PHP vs Blade\n\n| Plain PHP | Blade |\n|-----------|-------|\n| `<?php echo htmlspecialchars($name); ?>` | `{{ $name }}` |\n| `<?php if ($x): ?> ... <?php endif; ?>` | `@if ($x) ... @endif` |\n| `<?php // note ?>` | `{{-- note --}}` |\n\nBlade is shorter, and it escapes by default, so the safe choice is also the easy one.\n\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\n\n**Using `{!! !!}` for user input \"to make the formatting work\".**\n\n```blade\n<p>{!! $member->bio !!}</p>   {{-- wrong: a member typed this --}}\n```\n\nWhat happens: if a member types a script into their bio, it runs for every visitor who opens the page. That breaks the trust (*amānah*) of everyone using the library app. Fix:\n\n```blade\n<p>{{ $member->bio }}</p>     {{-- right --}}\n```\n\n**Forgetting `.blade.php`.** A file named `certificate.php` will not understand `{{ }}`. You will see the braces printed on the page. Rename it to `certificate.blade.php`.\n\n## ✍️ Try It\n\nMake a `certificate` view and a route that passes your own name, grade and number of books. Add a Blade comment and an HTML comment, then use \"View Source\" in the browser. Which comment can you see?\n\n*Hint:* only the HTML comment appears in the source.\n\n## 📝 Recap\n\n- Blade files end in `.blade.php` and live in `resources/views/`.\n- `{{ $value }}` prints a value and escapes it, which blocks XSS.\n- `{!! $html !!}` prints raw HTML. Use it only for trusted HTML you made.\n- `{{-- --}}` comments never reach the browser.\n- Directives like `@if` and `@foreach` replace PHP tags; each has an `@end...`.\n\n## 🏆 Practice\n\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\nWhat is the difference between `{{ $text }}` and `{!! $text !!}`?\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\n`{{ $text }}` escapes special characters, so any HTML in the text is shown as plain text. `{!! $text !!}` prints it as real HTML, with no escaping. Use `{!! !!}` only for HTML you fully trust.\n\n</details>\n\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\n`$title` is `Bees & Their Hives`. What HTML does `<h1>{{ $title }}</h1>` send, and what does the browser show?\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\nIt sends `<h1>Bees &amp; Their Hives</h1>`. The browser shows `Bees & Their Hives`. Escaping changed `&` to `&amp;`, and the browser turns it back into `&` for display.\n\n</details>\n\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\nBuild a \"Book of the Week\" card.\n\n1. A controller passes `title`, `author`, `pages` and `review` (typed by a student).\n2. The view shows the title in capitals, \"by\" the author, the pages with a thousands comma, and the review safely.\n3. If `pages` is over 300, show `A long read: take your time!`; otherwise show `A quick read for the weekend.`\n4. Add a developer note that visitors cannot see.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\n\n```blade\n{{-- resources/views/book-of-week.blade.php --}}\n{{-- Data comes from BookOfWeekController@show --}}\n<div class=\"card\">\n    <h2>{{ strtoupper($title) }}</h2>\n    <p>by {{ $author }}</p>\n    <p>{{ number_format($pages) }} pages</p>\n    <blockquote>{{ $review }}</blockquote>\n\n    @if ($pages > 300)\n        <p>A long read: take your time!</p>\n    @else\n        <p>A quick read for the weekend.</p>\n    @endif\n</div>\n```\n\nThe review uses `{{ }}` because a student typed it.\n\n</details>\n",
  "part-04-blade-templates/ch11-layouts-and-sections.md": "# Chapter 11: Layouts & Sections\n\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\n\nLook at your school's printed notices. Every notice has the same school logo at the top and the same address at the bottom. Only the middle part changes: one is about the science fair, another about library week. The office keeps one **letterhead** and prints each new notice on it. In Blade, a **layout** is the letterhead. Each page only writes its own middle part.\n\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\n\n- Explain why repeating the same HTML in every view causes problems.\n- Build a layout file with `@yield`.\n- Make pages that use the layout with `@extends` and `@section`.\n- Reuse small pieces such as a navigation bar with `@include`.\n- Recognise Blade **components** (`<x-...>`), which starter kits use.\n\n## 😩 The Problem: Copy and Paste\n\nSo far each view has its own `<html>`, `<head>` and menu. With three pages, that is three copies. If the library changes its menu, you must edit every copy. Miss one, and the site looks broken. Layouts solve this: **write the shared parts once**.\n\n## 🏗️ Step 1: The Layout\n\nCreate a folder `resources/views/layouts/` and a file `app.blade.php` inside it:\n\n```blade\n{{-- resources/views/layouts/app.blade.php --}}\n<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n<head>\n    <meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n    <meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">\n    <title>@yield('title', 'School Library')</title>\n</head>\n<body>\n    <header>\n        <h1>Crescent Valley School Library</h1>\n    </header>\n\n    <main>\n        @yield('content')\n    </main>\n\n    <footer>\n        <p>Open 8:00 to 15:00, Sunday to Thursday</p>\n    </footer>\n</body>\n</html>\n```\n\n`@yield('content')` is a **blank to fill**. It says, \"a page will put its content here\". `@yield('title', 'School Library')` has a **default**: if a page gives no title, the tab shows `School Library`.\n\n## 📄 Step 2: A Page That Uses It\n\n```blade\n{{-- resources/views/books/index.blade.php --}}\n@extends('layouts.app')\n\n@section('title', 'All Books')\n\n@section('content')\n    <h2>Library Books</h2>\n    <p>We have 3 titles on the shelf.</p>\n@endsection\n```\n\n- `@extends('layouts.app')` means \"print me on the `layouts/app.blade.php` letterhead\". It must be the **first line**.\n- `@section('title', 'All Books')` is the short form for a small value.\n- `@section('content') ... @endsection` holds a longer block of HTML.\n\nThe browser tab shows **All Books**, and the page shows:\n\n```text\nCrescent Valley School Library\nLibrary Books\nWe have 3 titles on the shelf.\nOpen 8:00 to 15:00, Sunday to Thursday\n```\n\nHere is how the pieces fit together:\n\n```text\nlayouts/app.blade.php\n+--------------------------+\n| header: library name     |\n+--------------------------+\n| @yield('content')        |\n|   ^ filled by the page's |\n|   @section('content')    |\n+--------------------------+\n| footer: opening hours    |\n+--------------------------+\n```\n\nA second page, `members/index.blade.php`, uses the same layout with its own content. Change the footer once in the layout, and every page changes.\n\n## 🧩 Including Small Pieces\n\nThe menu is a small piece of HTML that many layouts might share. Put it in its own file and pull it in with `@include`:\n\n```blade\n{{-- resources/views/partials/nav.blade.php --}}\n<nav>\n    <a href=\"{{ route('books.index') }}\">Books</a>\n    <a href=\"{{ route('members.index') }}\">Members</a>\n    <a href=\"{{ route('loans.index') }}\">Loans</a>\n</nav>\n```\n\n```blade\n{{-- inside layouts/app.blade.php, under the <h1> --}}\n@include('partials.nav')\n```\n\nThe dot means a folder, just like in `view()`. Files used only by `@include` are often called **partials**, because they are part of a page.\n\nAn included file can see the page's variables. You can also pass extra ones:\n\n```blade\n@include('partials.notice', ['message' => 'Library week starts on Monday!'])\n```\n\n```blade\n{{-- resources/views/partials/notice.blade.php --}}\n<div class=\"notice\">{{ $message }}</div>\n```\n\nOutput:\n\n```text\nLibrary week starts on Monday!\n```\n\n## ➕ Adding to a Section: `@parent`\n\nSometimes a page wants to *add* to a layout's section instead of replacing it. In the layout, write a section with `@show` instead of `@yield`:\n\n```blade\n{{-- layouts/app.blade.php --}}\n@section('sidebar')\n    <p>Today's tip: return books on time.</p>\n@show\n```\n\n```blade\n{{-- a page --}}\n@section('sidebar')\n    @parent\n    <p>New: science magazines on shelf B.</p>\n@endsection\n```\n\n`@parent` keeps the layout's content. The page shows both lines:\n\n```text\nToday's tip: return books on time.\nNew: science magazines on shelf B.\n```\n\n## 🧱 Components: The Other Style\n\nLaravel has a second way to build layouts: **components**. You will see them in starter kits (Chapter 22). A component file lives in `resources/views/components/` and is used like an HTML tag that starts with `x-`:\n\n```blade\n{{-- resources/views/components/layout.blade.php --}}\n<html>\n<body>\n    <h1>Crescent Valley School Library</h1>\n    {{ $slot }}\n</body>\n</html>\n```\n\n```blade\n{{-- resources/views/about.blade.php --}}\n<x-layout>\n    <p>We lend books to every student.</p>\n</x-layout>\n```\n\n`{{ $slot }}` is filled with whatever sits between `<x-layout>` and `</x-layout>`. Both styles work. This book uses `@extends` because it shows clearly how the blanks are filled. Pick one style per project and keep it.\n\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\n\n**Writing content outside any section.**\n\n```blade\n@extends('layouts.app')\n\n<h2>Library Books</h2>      {{-- outside a section --}}\n\n@section('content')\n    <p>We have 3 titles.</p>\n@endsection\n```\n\nWhat happens: the `<h2>` does not appear where you expect. With `@extends`, only content inside sections is placed into the layout's blanks. Move it inside:\n\n```blade\n@section('content')\n    <h2>Library Books</h2>\n    <p>We have 3 titles.</p>\n@endsection\n```\n\n**Mismatched names.** `@yield('content')` in the layout and `@section('main')` in the page do not connect. The page shows an empty middle. The names must match exactly.\n\n## ✍️ Try It\n\nCreate `layouts/app.blade.php` and change your `about` and `books.index` views to use it. Then change the footer text once and check that both pages update.\n\n*Hint:* each page starts with `@extends('layouts.app')`.\n\n## 📝 Recap\n\n- A layout holds the shared HTML; `@yield('name')` marks the blanks.\n- Pages use `@extends('layouts.app')` and fill blanks with `@section ... @endsection`.\n- `@yield('title', 'Default')` gives a default value.\n- `@include('partials.nav')` pulls in a small reusable file.\n- Components (`<x-layout>` with `{{ $slot }}`) are another layout style used by starter kits.\n\n## 🏆 Practice\n\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\nWhich directive goes in the layout to mark a blank, and which two directives does a page use to fill it?\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\nThe layout uses `@yield('content')`. The page uses `@extends('layouts.app')` and `@section('content') ... @endsection`.\n\n</details>\n\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\nWrite a page `members/index.blade.php` that uses `layouts.app`, sets the title to `Members`, and shows a heading `Library Members` with the paragraph `85 students and teachers.`\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\n```blade\n@extends('layouts.app')\n\n@section('title', 'Members')\n\n@section('content')\n    <h2>Library Members</h2>\n    <p>85 students and teachers.</p>\n@endsection\n```\n\n</details>\n\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\nImprove the library layout.\n\n1. Move the menu into `partials/nav.blade.php` and include it in the layout.\n2. In the layout, show a notice box only on pages that fill a section called `notice`. (Hint: `@hasSection('notice')` ... `@endif` checks whether a page filled a section.)\n3. Make the books page fill the `notice` section with `New arrivals this week!`\n4. Check that the members page shows no empty notice box.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\n\n```blade\n{{-- layouts/app.blade.php (body part) --}}\n<header>\n    <h1>Crescent Valley School Library</h1>\n    @include('partials.nav')\n</header>\n\n@hasSection('notice')\n    <div class=\"notice\">@yield('notice')</div>\n@endif\n\n<main>\n    @yield('content')\n</main>\n```\n\n```blade\n{{-- books/index.blade.php --}}\n@extends('layouts.app')\n\n@section('notice', 'New arrivals this week!')\n\n@section('content')\n    <h2>Library Books</h2>\n@endsection\n```\n\nThe books page shows the notice box. The members page does not fill `notice`, so `@hasSection` is false and no box appears.\n\n</details>\n",
  "part-04-blade-templates/ch12-loops-and-conditionals.md": "# Chapter 12: Loops & Conditionals\n\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\n\nEvery morning the library assistant reads the **returns list** aloud. For each book on the list she says its title. If a book is late, she adds, \"Please remind this member.\" If the list is empty, she says, \"No returns today.\" She repeats one action for every item, and makes small decisions along the way. Blade's loops and conditionals do exactly this inside your HTML.\n\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\n\n- Make decisions in a view with `@if`, `@elseif`, `@else` and `@unless`.\n- Check for missing or empty values with `@isset` and `@empty`.\n- Repeat HTML for every item with `@foreach`.\n- Handle an empty list neatly with `@forelse`.\n- Use the `$loop` variable to number rows and spot the first and last item.\n\n## 📦 Today's Data\n\nWe still use sample data from the controller. Part 5 brings real database rows.\n\n```php\n// app/Http/Controllers/BookController.php\npublic function index()\n{\n    $books = [\n        ['title' => 'Clean Water for Every Village', 'copies' => 3],\n        ['title' => 'The Story of Numbers', 'copies' => 0],\n        ['title' => 'Bees and Their Hives', 'copies' => 1],\n    ];\n\n    return view('books.index', ['books' => $books]);\n}\n```\n\n## 🔀 Conditionals\n\n### `@if`, `@elseif`, `@else`\n\n```blade\n@php $copies = 1; @endphp\n\n@if ($copies > 2)\n    <p>Plenty of copies on the shelf.</p>\n@elseif ($copies > 0)\n    <p>Only a few left. Borrow soon!</p>\n@else\n    <p>All copies are out. Please check back later.</p>\n@endif\n```\n\nOutput with `$copies = 1`:\n\n```text\nOnly a few left. Borrow soon!\n```\n\n(`@php ... @endphp` runs plain PHP. We use it here only to set a test value. In real views, data comes from the controller.)\n\n### `@unless`\n\n`@unless` is the opposite of `@if`. It runs when the condition is **false**:\n\n```blade\n@unless ($libraryOpen)\n    <p>The library is closed today.</p>\n@endunless\n```\n\nRead it as \"unless the library is open, show this\".\n\n### `@isset` and `@empty`\n\n```blade\n@isset($notice)\n    <div class=\"notice\">{{ $notice }}</div>\n@endisset\n\n@empty($books)\n    <p>No books yet.</p>\n@endempty\n```\n\n- `@isset($notice)` runs if `$notice` exists and is not `null`.\n- `@empty($books)` runs if `$books` is empty, such as `[]`.\n\n## 🔁 Loops\n\n### `@foreach`\n\n```blade\n<ul>\n@foreach ($books as $book)\n    <li>{{ $book['title'] }} ({{ $book['copies'] }} on the shelf)</li>\n@endforeach\n</ul>\n```\n\nOutput:\n\n```text\n• Clean Water for Every Village (3 on the shelf)\n• The Story of Numbers (0 on the shelf)\n• Bees and Their Hives (1 on the shelf)\n```\n\n### `@forelse`: a loop with an empty message\n\nLists are often empty: no loans today, no search results. `@forelse` handles that in one block:\n\n```blade\n@forelse ($books as $book)\n    <li>{{ $book['title'] }}</li>\n@empty\n    <li>The library has no books yet.</li>\n@endforelse\n```\n\nIf `$books` is `[]`, the output is:\n\n```text\n• The library has no books yet.\n```\n\n### `@for` and `@while`\n\nThese work like PHP's `for` and `while`:\n\n```blade\n@for ($shelf = 1; $shelf <= 3; $shelf++)\n    <p>Shelf {{ $shelf }}</p>\n@endfor\n```\n\nOutput:\n\n```text\nShelf 1\nShelf 2\nShelf 3\n```\n\nYou will use `@foreach` far more than `@for`, because data usually arrives as a list.\n\n## 🔢 The `$loop` Variable\n\nInside `@foreach` and `@forelse`, Blade gives you a free variable called `$loop`:\n\n| Property | Meaning |\n|----------|---------|\n| `$loop->iteration` | Which round, starting at 1 |\n| `$loop->index` | Which round, starting at 0 |\n| `$loop->count` | How many items in total |\n| `$loop->first` | `true` on the first round |\n| `$loop->last` | `true` on the last round |\n| `$loop->remaining` | Rounds still to go |\n\nNumber the rows and mark the last one:\n\n```blade\n@foreach ($books as $book)\n    <p>\n        {{ $loop->iteration }} of {{ $loop->count }}: {{ $book['title'] }}\n        @if ($loop->last) (end of list) @endif\n    </p>\n@endforeach\n```\n\nOutput:\n\n```text\n1 of 3: Clean Water for Every Village\n2 of 3: The Story of Numbers\n3 of 3: Bees and Their Hives (end of list)\n```\n\n## 🧩 Putting It Together\n\nA real book table with a status column:\n\n```blade\n{{-- resources/views/books/index.blade.php --}}\n@extends('layouts.app')\n\n@section('content')\n<table>\n    <tr><th>#</th><th>Title</th><th>Status</th></tr>\n    @forelse ($books as $book)\n        <tr>\n            <td>{{ $loop->iteration }}</td>\n            <td>{{ $book['title'] }}</td>\n            <td>\n                @if ($book['copies'] > 0)\n                    Available ({{ $book['copies'] }})\n                @else\n                    All borrowed\n                @endif\n            </td>\n        </tr>\n    @empty\n        <tr><td colspan=\"3\">No books yet.</td></tr>\n    @endforelse\n</table>\n@endsection\n```\n\nOutput:\n\n```text\n#  Title                          Status\n1  Clean Water for Every Village  Available (3)\n2  The Story of Numbers           All borrowed\n3  Bees and Their Hives           Available (1)\n```\n\n### Adding a CSS class with `@class`\n\n`@class` adds CSS classes only when a condition is true:\n\n```blade\n<tr @class(['row', 'row-empty' => $book['copies'] === 0])>\n```\n\nFor \"The Story of Numbers\" this prints `<tr class=\"row row-empty\">`. For the others it prints `<tr class=\"row\">`.\n\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\n\n**Forgetting the closing directive.**\n\n```blade\n@foreach ($books as $book)\n    <li>{{ $book['title'] }}</li>\n{{-- forgot @endforeach --}}\n```\n\nWhat happens: an error page such as `syntax error, unexpected end of file`. Every `@if` needs `@endif`, every `@foreach` needs `@endforeach`, and so on. Indent the inside of each block, so a missing end is easy to spot.\n\n**Using `$loop` outside a loop.** `$loop->iteration` only exists between `@foreach` and `@endforeach`. Outside, you get `Undefined variable $loop`.\n\n## ✍️ Try It\n\nPass a list of four members (name and grade) to a view. Show them in a numbered list, and add `(Grade 10 leaver)` after members in Grade 10. If the list is empty, show `No members yet.`\n\n*Hint:* use `@forelse`, `$loop->iteration` and `@if ($member['grade'] === 10)`.\n\n## 📝 Recap\n\n- `@if / @elseif / @else / @endif` make decisions; `@unless` is the opposite of `@if`.\n- `@isset` and `@empty` check for missing or empty values.\n- `@foreach` repeats HTML for every item; `@forelse ... @empty` adds an empty message.\n- `$loop` gives `iteration`, `count`, `first`, `last` and more inside loops.\n- Every directive block needs its matching `@end...`.\n\n## 🏆 Practice\n\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\nWhat is the difference between `$loop->index` and `$loop->iteration`?\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\n`$loop->index` starts counting at **0**; `$loop->iteration` starts at **1**. On the first round, index is 0 and iteration is 1.\n\n</details>\n\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\nRewrite this with `@forelse`:\n\n```blade\n@if (count($loans) > 0)\n    @foreach ($loans as $loan)\n        <li>{{ $loan['title'] }}</li>\n    @endforeach\n@else\n    <li>No books borrowed.</li>\n@endif\n```\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\n```blade\n@forelse ($loans as $loan)\n    <li>{{ $loan['title'] }}</li>\n@empty\n    <li>No books borrowed.</li>\n@endforelse\n```\n\n</details>\n\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\nBuild a \"Due Today\" board.\n\n1. The controller passes `$loans`: each has `member`, `title` and `days_late` (0 means due today).\n2. Show a numbered list of loans.\n3. If `days_late` is more than 0, show `Late by N days`; otherwise show `Due today`.\n4. After the last item, show `Total: N books`.\n5. If there are no loans, show `Nothing due today. Thank you, readers!`\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\n\n```blade\n@forelse ($loans as $loan)\n    <p>\n        {{ $loop->iteration }}. {{ $loan['title'] }} ({{ $loan['member'] }}):\n        @if ($loan['days_late'] > 0)\n            Late by {{ $loan['days_late'] }} days\n        @else\n            Due today\n        @endif\n    </p>\n    @if ($loop->last)\n        <p><strong>Total: {{ $loop->count }} books</strong></p>\n    @endif\n@empty\n    <p>Nothing due today. Thank you, readers!</p>\n@endforelse\n```\n\nWith two loans (Omar, 0 days late; Fatima, 2 days late) the output is:\n\n```text\n1. The Story of Numbers (Omar): Due today\n2. Bees and Their Hives (Fatima): Late by 2 days\nTotal: 2 books\n```\n\n</details>\n",
  "part-05-eloquent-orm/ch13-models-and-eloquent.md": "# Chapter 13: Models & Eloquent\n\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\n\nIn the old days, finding a book meant walking to the records room, opening a heavy register, and searching page by page. Then the school gave the librarian a helpful **assistant** who knows the register by heart. Now she just says, \"Please bring me all science books, newest first,\" and the assistant does the searching. In Laravel, that assistant is **Eloquent**, and each **model** is the assistant in charge of one table.\n\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\n\n- Explain what an **ORM** is and what a **model** does.\n- Create a model with `php artisan make:model`.\n- Know Laravel's naming rules: the `Book` model uses the `books` table.\n- Read data with `all()`, `find()`, `findOrFail()`, `where()`, `orderBy()` and `count()`.\n- Try queries in **Tinker** and show real rows in a view.\n\n## 🧠 What is Eloquent?\n\nIn the PHP book you wrote SQL by hand with PDO:\n\n```php\n$stmt = $pdo->prepare('SELECT * FROM books WHERE id = ?');\n$stmt->execute([2]);\n$book = $stmt->fetch();\n```\n\n**Eloquent** is Laravel's **ORM** (Object-Relational Mapper). An ORM lets you work with database rows as PHP **objects**. The same query becomes:\n\n```php\n$book = Book::find(2);\necho $book->title;\n```\n\nEloquent writes the SQL for you and uses **prepared statements**, so values are sent safely. That protects you from SQL injection, as long as you use Eloquent's methods instead of gluing user input into raw SQL.\n\n| Database | Eloquent |\n|----------|----------|\n| Table `books` | Model class `Book` |\n| One row | One `Book` object |\n| Column `title` | Property `$book->title` |\n\n## 🗄️ Today the Table Already Exists\n\nPart 6 teaches **migrations**, Laravel's way to create tables with PHP. For now, make the `books` table yourself with SQL, as you did in the MySQL book. Open phpMyAdmin, choose the `library_app` database, open the **SQL** tab and run:\n\n```sql\nCREATE TABLE books (\n    id BIGINT UNSIGNED AUTO_INCREMENT PRIMARY KEY,\n    title VARCHAR(255) NOT NULL,\n    author VARCHAR(255) NOT NULL,\n    isbn VARCHAR(20) NOT NULL UNIQUE,\n    copies INT UNSIGNED NOT NULL DEFAULT 1,\n    created_at TIMESTAMP NULL,\n    updated_at TIMESTAMP NULL\n);\n\nINSERT INTO books (title, author, isbn, copies) VALUES\n('Clean Water for Every Village', 'Aisha Rahman', '978-0-00-000001-1', 3),\n('The Story of Numbers', 'Yusuf Ali Khan', '978-0-00-000002-8', 0),\n('Bees and Their Hives', 'Maryam Siddiqui', '978-0-00-000003-5', 1),\n('Stars Over the Desert', 'Hamza Qureshi', '978-0-00-000004-2', 2);\n```\n\n(These ISBNs are made-up practice numbers.) Notice the columns `created_at` and `updated_at`. Eloquent fills them in automatically when you save rows.\n\n## 🛠️ Create the Model\n\n```bash\nphp artisan make:model Book\n```\n\nOutput:\n\n```text\n   INFO  Model [C:\\xampp\\htdocs\\library-app\\app\\Models\\Book.php] created successfully.\n```\n\n```php\n<?php\n\nnamespace App\\Models;\n\nuse Illuminate\\Database\\Eloquent\\Model;\n\nclass Book extends Model\n{\n    //\n}\n```\n\nThat empty class is already enough to read the table. How does it know which table? By **convention** (an agreed naming rule):\n\n| Model (singular, CapitalCase) | Table (plural, lowercase) |\n|------|------|\n| `Book` | `books` |\n| `Member` | `members` |\n| `Loan` | `loans` |\n\nEloquent also expects a primary key called `id`. If your table had a different name, you could write `protected $table = 'library_books';` inside the class. Following the conventions is simpler.\n\n## 🧪 Try Queries in Tinker\n\n**Tinker** is a PHP command line that already knows your Laravel app. Start it:\n\n```bash\nphp artisan tinker\n```\n\nType a query after the `>` prompt and press Enter. Results start with `=`:\n\n```text\n> Book::count()\n[!] Aliasing 'Book' to 'App\\Models\\Book' for this Tinker session.\n= 4\n\n> Book::find(2)->title\n= \"The Story of Numbers\"\n\n> Book::where('copies', '>', 0)->count()\n= 3\n\n> Book::orderBy('title')->pluck('title')\n= Illuminate\\Support\\Collection {#6214\n    all: [\n      \"Bees and Their Hives\",\n      \"Clean Water for Every Village\",\n      \"Stars Over the Desert\",\n      \"The Story of Numbers\",\n    ],\n  }\n```\n\n(The `#6214` number is an internal id. Yours will differ.) Type `exit` to leave Tinker.\n\n## 📖 The Most Useful Read Methods\n\n| Code | What it gives |\n|------|---------------|\n| `Book::all()` | Every book, as a **collection** (a smart list) |\n| `Book::find(2)` | The book with id 2, or `null` |\n| `Book::findOrFail(2)` | The book with id 2, or a 404 page |\n| `Book::where('author', 'Aisha Rahman')->get()` | All matching books |\n| `Book::where('copies', '>', 0)->first()` | The first match, or `null` |\n| `Book::orderBy('title')->get()` | All books sorted A to Z |\n| `Book::count()` | The number of rows |\n\nA query that starts with `where()` or `orderBy()` must end with `get()`, `first()` or `count()`. Those words actually run it.\n\n## 🔗 Real Data in the Controller\n\nReplace the sample array from Part 4:\n\n```php\n// app/Http/Controllers/BookController.php\nuse App\\Models\\Book;\n\npublic function index()\n{\n    $books = Book::orderBy('title')->get();\n\n    return view('books.index', ['books' => $books]);\n}\n```\n\nIn the view, use `->` because each book is now an object:\n\n```blade\n{{-- resources/views/books/index.blade.php --}}\n@foreach ($books as $book)\n    <p>{{ $book->title }} by {{ $book->author }} ({{ $book->copies }} on the shelf)</p>\n@endforeach\n```\n\nOutput:\n\n```text\nBees and Their Hives by Maryam Siddiqui (1 on the shelf)\nClean Water for Every Village by Aisha Rahman (3 on the shelf)\nStars Over the Desert by Hamza Qureshi (2 on the shelf)\nThe Story of Numbers by Yusuf Ali Khan (0 on the shelf)\n```\n\n## 🎁 Route Model Binding\n\nFor one book, Laravel can find it **for you**. Type-hint the model in the method, and give the route parameter the same name:\n\n```php\n// routes/web.php\nRoute::get('/books/{book}', [BookController::class, 'show'])->name('books.show');\n```\n\n```php\n// app/Http/Controllers/BookController.php\npublic function show(Book $book)\n{\n    return view('books.show', ['book' => $book]);\n}\n```\n\nVisiting `/books/3` gives the method the `Book` with id 3. Visiting `/books/99` shows a 404 page automatically. This is called **route model binding**. The resource routes from Chapter 8 already use `{book}`, so it fits them perfectly.\n\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\n\n**Forgetting `get()`.**\n\n```php\n$books = Book::where('copies', '>', 0);   // no get()\n```\n\nWhat happens: `$books` is a **query builder**, a plan for a query that has not run yet. A `@foreach` over it shows nothing useful. Add `->get()`:\n\n```php\n$books = Book::where('copies', '>', 0)->get();\n```\n\n**Forgetting the `use` line in the controller.** Without `use App\\Models\\Book;` at the top, PHP looks for `Book` in the controller's own namespace, and you see `Class \"App\\Http\\Controllers\\Book\" not found`. Add the `use` line.\n\n## ✍️ Try It\n\nRun the SQL above, create the `Book` model, and in Tinker find (a) how many books have no copies left, (b) the author of book 4.\n\n*Hint:* `Book::where('copies', 0)->count()` and `Book::find(4)->author`.\n\n## 📝 Recap\n\n- Eloquent is Laravel's ORM: tables become model classes, rows become objects.\n- `php artisan make:model Book` creates `app/Models/Book.php`, linked to the `books` table.\n- Read with `all()`, `find()`, `findOrFail()`, `where()->get()`, `orderBy()`, `count()`.\n- Tinker (`php artisan tinker`) lets you test queries quickly.\n- Route model binding (`show(Book $book)` with `{book}`) finds the row or shows a 404.\n\n## 🏆 Practice\n\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\nWhich table does a model called `Member` use? What does `findOrFail(7)` do if there is no row 7?\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\nThe `members` table. `findOrFail(7)` stops the request with a **404 Not Found** page.\n\n</details>\n\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\nWrite the Eloquent code for: all books by `Aisha Rahman`, sorted by title, and the number of books with more than 1 copy.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\n```php\n$books = Book::where('author', 'Aisha Rahman')->orderBy('title')->get();\n$many = Book::where('copies', '>', 1)->count();\n```\n\nWith the sample data, `$many` is `2` (3 copies and 2 copies).\n\n</details>\n\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\nBuild an \"Available Now\" page.\n\n1. Add a route `/books/available` named `books.available`, above `/books/{book}`.\n2. In `BookController@available`, get books with at least one copy, sorted by title.\n3. Show them in a view with `@forelse`, and the message `Everything is borrowed right now!` if none.\n4. Show the total with `$books->count()`.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\n\n```php\n// routes/web.php\nRoute::get('/books/available', [BookController::class, 'available'])->name('books.available');\nRoute::get('/books/{book}', [BookController::class, 'show'])->name('books.show');\n```\n\n```php\n// app/Http/Controllers/BookController.php\npublic function available()\n{\n    $books = Book::where('copies', '>', 0)->orderBy('title')->get();\n    return view('books.available', ['books' => $books]);\n}\n```\n\n```blade\n{{-- resources/views/books/available.blade.php --}}\n<h2>Available Now ({{ $books->count() }})</h2>\n@forelse ($books as $book)\n    <p>{{ $book->title }}</p>\n@empty\n    <p>Everything is borrowed right now!</p>\n@endforelse\n```\n\nWith the sample data the heading is `Available Now (3)`.\n\n</details>\n",
  "part-05-eloquent-orm/ch14-crud-with-eloquent.md": "# Chapter 14: CRUD with Eloquent\n\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\n\nA new box of donated books arrives at the library. The librarian **adds** each one to the register. A week later she notices a spelling mistake in an author's name and **fixes** it. One book is damaged by rain, so she **removes** it from the register. Every day she **looks up** books for students. Add, look up, fix, remove: these are the four jobs of every register, and Eloquent can do all four.\n\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\n\n- Create rows with `create()` and with `new` plus `save()`.\n- Protect your models with the `$fillable` list (mass assignment).\n- Update rows with `update()` or by changing properties and calling `save()`.\n- Delete rows with `delete()` and `destroy()`.\n- Change a number safely with `increment()` and `decrement()`.\n\n## 🔄 CRUD Reminder\n\nIn Chapter 8 you met **CRUD**: Create, Read, Update, Delete. Chapter 13 covered **Read**. This chapter covers the other three. We test everything in **Tinker** first. Chapter 19 connects it to real forms.\n\n## ➕ Create: Two Ways\n\n### Way 1: `new` and `save()`\n\n```php\n$book = new Book;\n$book->title = \"A Young Gardener's Guide\";\n$book->author = 'Fatima Zahra';\n$book->isbn = '978-0-00-000005-9';\n$book->copies = 2;\n$book->save();\n```\n\nIn Tinker, the last line shows:\n\n```text\n= true\n```\n\n`save()` runs an SQL `INSERT`. Eloquent fills `id`, `created_at` and `updated_at` for you.\n\n### Way 2: `create()` with an array\n\n`create()` does it in one step. But first you must tell the model which columns may be filled this way:\n\n```php\n// app/Models/Book.php\nclass Book extends Model\n{\n    protected $fillable = ['title', 'author', 'isbn', 'copies'];\n}\n```\n\nRestart Tinker (it only loads code when it starts), then:\n\n```text\n> Book::create(['title' => 'Solar Power at School', 'author' => 'Omar Farooq', 'isbn' => '978-0-00-000006-6', 'copies' => 1])\n= App\\Models\\Book {#6231\n    title: \"Solar Power at School\",\n    author: \"Omar Farooq\",\n    isbn: \"978-0-00-000006-6\",\n    copies: 1,\n    updated_at: \"2026-10-13 09:15:42\",\n    created_at: \"2026-10-13 09:15:42\",\n    id: 6,\n  }\n```\n\nYour times will differ.\n\n## 🛡️ Why `$fillable`? Mass Assignment\n\nFilling many columns from one array is called **mass assignment**. It is handy, and a little risky. Later, `create()` will receive the array of data a visitor sent from a form. Suppose the `members` table had a column `is_librarian`. A sneaky visitor could add a hidden field `is_librarian=1` to the form. Without protection, they would become a librarian!\n\n`$fillable` is a **guest list**. Only the columns on the list are filled by `create()` and `update()`. Anything else is ignored. Keep the list to columns a normal form should change.\n\n## ✏️ Update: Two Ways\n\n### Change properties, then `save()`\n\n```text\n> $book = Book::find(2)\n> $book->copies = 4\n> $book->save()\n= true\n```\n\n(Tinker also prints the value of lines like `$book = Book::find(2)`. We leave those printouts out to keep the examples short.)\n\n### `update()` with an array\n\n```text\n> Book::find(3)->update(['author' => 'Maryam Siddiqui-Ahmed'])\n= true\n```\n\nBoth run an SQL `UPDATE` and refresh `updated_at`. `update()` uses `$fillable` too.\n\n### Counting up and down\n\nWhen a member borrows a book, one copy leaves the shelf. When it returns, one comes back:\n\n```text\n> $book = Book::find(1)\n> $book->decrement('copies')\n= 1\n> $book->copies\n= 2\n> $book->increment('copies')\n= 1\n> $book->copies\n= 3\n```\n\nThe `= 1` means one row changed. `decrement()` and `increment()` change the number inside the database in one step. That is safer than reading the number, changing it in PHP and saving it, because two librarians at two desks could overwrite each other.\n\n## 🗑️ Delete\n\n```text\n> Book::find(6)->delete()\n= true\n\n> Book::destroy(5)\n= 1\n```\n\n- `$book->delete()` deletes a model you already have.\n- `Book::destroy(5)` deletes by id. It returns how many rows it deleted. `Book::destroy([5, 6])` deletes several.\n\nA deleted row is gone. There is no \"undo\" in the database. Real apps ask \"Are you sure?\" before deleting.\n\n## 🔍 Find or Create\n\nSometimes you want \"find this row, and if it is not there, add it\":\n\n```php\n$member = Member::firstOrCreate(\n    ['email' => 'zainab@example.org'],      // look for this\n    ['name' => 'Zainab Hussain', 'grade' => 10]  // add these if new\n);\n```\n\nThis needs a `Member` model and table; you will make them in Chapter 15.\n\n## 🧭 Where CRUD Goes in the Controller\n\nIn the app, each Eloquent call lives in a resource controller method from Chapter 8:\n\n| Action | Eloquent inside |\n|--------|----------------|\n| `index` | `Book::orderBy('title')->get()` |\n| `show(Book $book)` | (route model binding finds it) |\n| `store` | `Book::create($data)` |\n| `update(Book $book)` | `$book->update($data)` |\n| `destroy(Book $book)` | `$book->delete()` |\n\n`$data` will come from a checked form. Chapters 19 and 20 build that. For example, `destroy` will be:\n\n```php\n// app/Http/Controllers/BookController.php\npublic function destroy(Book $book)\n{\n    $book->delete();\n\n    return redirect()->route('books.index');\n}\n```\n\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\n\n**Calling `create()` without `$fillable`.**\n\n```text\n> Book::create(['title' => 'Plants of the Valley', 'author' => 'Ali Hassan', 'isbn' => '978-0-00-000007-3'])\n```\n\nWhat happens:\n\n```text\n   Illuminate\\Database\\Eloquent\\MassAssignmentException  Add [title] to fillable property to allow mass assignment on [App\\Models\\Book].\n```\n\nFix: add the columns to `protected $fillable` in the model, then restart Tinker.\n\n**Deleting with a GET link.** `<a href=\"/books/3/delete\">Delete</a>` is dangerous. Search engines and browser previews follow links, and could delete books. Deleting must use a form that sends `DELETE` (Chapter 19).\n\n## ✍️ Try It\n\nIn Tinker: add a book with `create()`, change its copies to 5 with `update()`, take one copy out with `decrement()`, then delete it. Check phpMyAdmin after each step.\n\n*Hint:* save the new book in a variable first: `$b = Book::create([...])`.\n\n## 📝 Recap\n\n- Create with `new` + `save()`, or `Model::create([...])`.\n- `$fillable` lists the columns that `create()` and `update()` may fill. It blocks sneaky extra fields.\n- Update with `$model->save()` after changing properties, or `$model->update([...])`.\n- `increment()` and `decrement()` change numbers safely in one step.\n- Delete with `$model->delete()` or `Model::destroy($id)`; never with a GET link.\n\n## 🏆 Practice\n\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\nWhat is mass assignment, and which model property controls it?\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\nMass assignment is filling many columns at once from an array, as `create()` and `update()` do. The `$fillable` property lists which columns are allowed.\n\n</details>\n\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\nWrite Eloquent code that (a) finds the book with ISBN `978-0-00-000002-8`, (b) adds 3 copies to it.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\n```php\n$book = Book::where('isbn', '978-0-00-000002-8')->first();\n$book->increment('copies', 3);\n```\n\n`increment()` takes an optional second argument: how much to add.\n\n</details>\n\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\nA box of donated books arrives.\n\n1. Write code that adds three books in a loop from an array of arrays.\n2. For each, if a book with the same ISBN already exists, add its copies instead of creating a duplicate.\n3. At the end, print the total number of books with `Book::count()`.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\n\n```php\n$donations = [\n    ['title' => 'Clean Water for Every Village', 'author' => 'Aisha Rahman', 'isbn' => '978-0-00-000001-1', 'copies' => 2],\n    ['title' => 'Rivers and Rain', 'author' => 'Sara Malik', 'isbn' => '978-0-00-000008-0', 'copies' => 1],\n    ['title' => 'Kind Words', 'author' => 'Hassan Javed', 'isbn' => '978-0-00-000009-7', 'copies' => 4],\n];\n\nforeach ($donations as $item) {\n    $book = Book::where('isbn', $item['isbn'])->first();\n\n    if ($book) {\n        $book->increment('copies', $item['copies']);\n    } else {\n        Book::create($item);\n    }\n}\n\necho Book::count();\n```\n\nThe first book already exists, so its copies go up by 2. The other two are new rows.\n\n</details>\n",
  "part-05-eloquent-orm/ch15-relationships.md": "# Chapter 15: Relationships\n\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\n\nEvery loan slip in the library connects two things: a **member** and a **book**. From a member's card, the librarian can list all the slips that member has. From a slip, she can see the book's title and the member's name. Nothing is written twice; each slip just points to the right card and the right book. Eloquent **relationships** let your models point to each other in the same way.\n\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\n\n- Explain **one-to-many** relationships with foreign keys.\n- Write `hasMany()` and `belongsTo()` methods in models.\n- Read related data, such as `$member->loans` and `$loan->book->title`.\n- Filter related rows, such as loans not yet returned.\n- Avoid the \"N+1\" slowdown with eager loading: `with()`.\n\n## 🔗 One-to-Many\n\nLook at our three tables:\n\n```text\nmembers       loans            books\n+-------+     +-----------+    +-------+\n| id    |<----| member_id |    | id    |\n| name  |     | book_id   |--->| title |\n+-------+     +-----------+    +-------+\n```\n\n- One **member** can have **many** loans.\n- One **book** can have **many** loans (over time).\n- Each **loan** belongs to **one** member and **one** book.\n\nThe columns `member_id` and `book_id` are **foreign keys**: they store the id of a row in another table. You met them in the MySQL book.\n\n## 🗄️ Create the Tables (by Hand for Now)\n\nAs in Chapter 13, run this SQL in phpMyAdmin. Part 6 shows the Laravel way.\n\n```sql\nCREATE TABLE members (\n    id BIGINT UNSIGNED AUTO_INCREMENT PRIMARY KEY,\n    name VARCHAR(255) NOT NULL,\n    email VARCHAR(255) NOT NULL UNIQUE,\n    grade TINYINT UNSIGNED NOT NULL,\n    created_at TIMESTAMP NULL,\n    updated_at TIMESTAMP NULL\n);\n\nCREATE TABLE loans (\n    id BIGINT UNSIGNED AUTO_INCREMENT PRIMARY KEY,\n    book_id BIGINT UNSIGNED NOT NULL,\n    member_id BIGINT UNSIGNED NOT NULL,\n    borrowed_on DATE NOT NULL,\n    due_on DATE NOT NULL,\n    returned_on DATE NULL,\n    created_at TIMESTAMP NULL,\n    updated_at TIMESTAMP NULL,\n    FOREIGN KEY (book_id) REFERENCES books(id),\n    FOREIGN KEY (member_id) REFERENCES members(id)\n);\n\nINSERT INTO members (name, email, grade) VALUES\n('Aisha Khan', 'aisha@example.org', 9),\n('Omar Siddiqui', 'omar@example.org', 10),\n('Fatima Noor', 'fatima@example.org', 9);\n\nINSERT INTO loans (book_id, member_id, borrowed_on, due_on, returned_on) VALUES\n(2, 1, '2026-10-01', '2026-10-15', NULL),\n(1, 1, '2026-09-10', '2026-09-24', '2026-09-20'),\n(3, 2, '2026-10-05', '2026-10-19', NULL);\n```\n\nThen make the two models:\n\n```bash\nphp artisan make:model Member\nphp artisan make:model Loan\n```\n\n## ✍️ Write the Relationships\n\nA relationship is a **method** in the model that returns `hasMany(...)` or `belongsTo(...)`.\n\n```php\n// app/Models/Member.php\nnamespace App\\Models;\n\nuse Illuminate\\Database\\Eloquent\\Model;\nuse Illuminate\\Database\\Eloquent\\Relations\\HasMany;\n\nclass Member extends Model\n{\n    protected $fillable = ['name', 'email', 'grade'];\n\n    public function loans(): HasMany\n    {\n        return $this->hasMany(Loan::class);\n    }\n}\n```\n\n```php\n// app/Models/Loan.php\nnamespace App\\Models;\n\nuse Illuminate\\Database\\Eloquent\\Model;\nuse Illuminate\\Database\\Eloquent\\Relations\\BelongsTo;\n\nclass Loan extends Model\n{\n    protected $fillable = ['book_id', 'member_id', 'borrowed_on', 'due_on', 'returned_on'];\n\n    protected function casts(): array\n    {\n        return [\n            'borrowed_on' => 'date',\n            'due_on' => 'date',\n            'returned_on' => 'date',\n        ];\n    }\n\n    public function book(): BelongsTo\n    {\n        return $this->belongsTo(Book::class);\n    }\n\n    public function member(): BelongsTo\n    {\n        return $this->belongsTo(Member::class);\n    }\n}\n```\n\n```php\n// app/Models/Book.php (add inside the class)\npublic function loans(): HasMany\n{\n    return $this->hasMany(Loan::class);\n}\n```\n\n(Add `use Illuminate\\Database\\Eloquent\\Relations\\HasMany;` at the top of `Book.php` too.)\n\nHow does Eloquent know the column names? Convention again. `belongsTo(Book::class)` looks for `book_id` in the `loans` table. `hasMany(Loan::class)` in `Member` looks for `member_id` in `loans`.\n\nThe `casts()` method tells Eloquent to turn the date columns into date objects. Then you can write `$loan->due_on->format('d M Y')`.\n\n## 📖 Use the Relationships\n\nCall the method **without brackets** to get the related data:\n\n```text\n> $aisha = Member::find(1)\n> $aisha->loans->count()\n= 2\n\n> Loan::find(1)->book->title\n= \"The Story of Numbers\"\n\n> Loan::find(3)->member->name\n= \"Omar Siddiqui\"\n\n> Loan::find(1)->due_on->format('d M Y')\n= \"15 Oct 2026\"\n```\n\n- `$aisha->loans` gives a collection of `Loan` objects.\n- `$loan->book` gives one `Book` object.\n\n### With brackets: keep building the query\n\nCall the method **with brackets** to add conditions before running it:\n\n```text\n> $aisha->loans()->whereNull('returned_on')->count()\n= 1\n```\n\nThis counts Aisha's books that are still out. `whereNull('returned_on')` means \"the return date is empty\".\n\n### Create through a relationship\n\n```php\n$aisha->loans()->create([\n    'book_id' => 4,\n    'borrowed_on' => '2026-10-13',\n    'due_on' => '2026-10-27',\n]);\n```\n\nEloquent fills `member_id` with Aisha's id for you.\n\n## 🖼️ In a View\n\n```php\n// app/Http/Controllers/MemberController.php\npublic function show(Member $member)\n{\n    return view('members.show', ['member' => $member]);\n}\n```\n\n```blade\n{{-- resources/views/members/show.blade.php --}}\n<h2>{{ $member->name }} (Grade {{ $member->grade }})</h2>\n<ul>\n@forelse ($member->loans as $loan)\n    <li>\n        {{ $loan->book->title }}, due {{ $loan->due_on->format('d M Y') }}\n        @if ($loan->returned_on) (returned) @endif\n    </li>\n@empty\n    <li>No books borrowed yet.</li>\n@endforelse\n</ul>\n```\n\nFor `/members/1` the browser shows:\n\n```text\nAisha Khan (Grade 9)\n• The Story of Numbers, due 15 Oct 2026\n• Clean Water for Every Village, due 24 Sep 2026 (returned)\n```\n\n## 🐢 The N+1 Problem and `with()`\n\nLook at this loans page:\n\n```php\n$loans = Loan::all();               // 1 query\n```\n\n```blade\n@foreach ($loans as $loan)\n    {{ $loan->book->title }}        {{-- 1 more query per loan! --}}\n@endforeach\n```\n\nWith 3 loans, that is 1 + 3 = 4 queries. With 500 loans, it is 501. This is called the **N+1 problem**. Fix it with **eager loading**, which fetches all the related rows at once:\n\n```php\n$loans = Loan::with(['book', 'member'])->get();   // 3 queries in total\n```\n\nNow there is one query for loans, one for all their books and one for all their members, however many loans there are.\n\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\n\n**Mixing up brackets.**\n\n```php\n$count = $member->loans->whereNull('returned_on')->count();   // works, but slow\n$title = $loan->book()->title;                                // error\n```\n\n`$loan->book()` returns a **relationship object**, not a book, so `->title` fails with `Undefined property`. Use `$loan->book->title`. And `$member->loans` loads every loan before filtering in PHP. For big lists, use `$member->loans()->whereNull('returned_on')->count()`, which filters in SQL.\n\n**Wrong foreign key name.** If the column is `student_id` instead of `member_id`, `hasMany(Loan::class)` finds nothing. Either rename the column, or pass it: `hasMany(Loan::class, 'student_id')`.\n\n## ✍️ Try It\n\nCreate the tables and models above. In Tinker, find how many loans book 2 has, and the name of the member who borrowed it.\n\n*Hint:* `Book::find(2)->loans->count()` and `Book::find(2)->loans->first()->member->name`.\n\n## 📝 Recap\n\n- One-to-many: a member has many loans; a loan belongs to one member.\n- `hasMany(Loan::class)` and `belongsTo(Book::class)` define relationships, using `*_id` columns by convention.\n- `$member->loans` (no brackets) gives data; `$member->loans()` (brackets) lets you add conditions.\n- `casts()` can turn date columns into date objects.\n- `with([...])` loads related rows in advance and avoids the N+1 problem.\n\n## 🏆 Practice\n\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\nWhich relationship method goes in `Loan` to reach its book, and which column does it use?\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\n`belongsTo(Book::class)` in a method called `book()`. It uses the `book_id` column in the `loans` table.\n\n</details>\n\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\nWrite code that lists the titles of all books Omar (member 2) has **not** returned yet.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\n```php\n$loans = Member::find(2)->loans()->whereNull('returned_on')->with('book')->get();\n\nforeach ($loans as $loan) {\n    echo $loan->book->title . \"\\n\";\n}\n```\n\nOutput with the sample data:\n\n```text\nBees and Their Hives\n```\n\n</details>\n\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\nBuild the **overdue loans** page planned in Chapter 2.\n\n1. Route `/loans/overdue` → `LoanController@overdue`, named `loans.overdue`.\n2. Find loans with no return date and a due date before today. (Hint: `->where('due_on', '<', today())`.)\n3. Eager load the book and member, sort by due date.\n4. Show \"Title, borrowed by Name, due date\" for each, or `No overdue books. Thank you, readers!`\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\n\n```php\n// app/Http/Controllers/LoanController.php\nuse App\\Models\\Loan;\n\npublic function overdue()\n{\n    $loans = Loan::with(['book', 'member'])\n        ->whereNull('returned_on')\n        ->where('due_on', '<', today())\n        ->orderBy('due_on')\n        ->get();\n\n    return view('loans.overdue', ['loans' => $loans]);\n}\n```\n\n```blade\n{{-- resources/views/loans/overdue.blade.php --}}\n@forelse ($loans as $loan)\n    <p>{{ $loan->book->title }}, borrowed by {{ $loan->member->name }},\n       due {{ $loan->due_on->format('d M Y') }}</p>\n@empty\n    <p>No overdue books. Thank you, readers!</p>\n@endforelse\n```\n\n`today()` is a Laravel helper for today's date at midnight. On 16 October 2026, the sample data shows `The Story of Numbers, borrowed by Aisha Khan, due 15 Oct 2026`.\n\n</details>\n",
  "part-06-migrations/ch16-what-are-migrations.md": "# Chapter 16: What are Migrations?\n\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\n\nWhen the library rearranges its shelves, the head librarian writes each change in a **logbook**: \"Monday: added a shelf for science magazines. Tuesday: moved poetry to room 2.\" A new helper can read the logbook from the first page and set up an identical library somewhere else. If a change was a mistake, the logbook also says how to undo it. **Migrations** are that logbook for your database.\n\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\n\n- Explain what a migration is and why teams use them.\n- Read a migration file and its `up()` and `down()` methods.\n- Run migrations and check their status with Artisan.\n- Undo the last batch with `migrate:rollback`.\n- Know which commands delete data, and use them with care.\n\n## 🤔 The Problem with Clicking\n\nIn Chapters 13 and 15 you created tables by running SQL in phpMyAdmin. That works on **your** computer. Now imagine:\n\n- Your teammate downloads the project. Their database is empty. Which SQL should they run, and in what order?\n- You add a `shelf` column next week. How does everyone else find out?\n- The school puts the app on a real server. Someone must repeat every click exactly.\n\nClicks and pasted SQL leave no record. Mistakes creep in.\n\n## 📒 What is a Migration?\n\nA **migration** is a PHP file that describes **one change** to the database: create a table, add a column, rename something. Migrations live in `database/migrations/`, and they are saved with the rest of your code in Git.\n\nAnyone can rebuild the whole database with one command:\n\n```bash\nphp artisan migrate\n```\n\nThink of migrations as **version control for your database**. Git tracks changes to code; migrations track changes to tables.\n\n## 🔍 Read a Real Migration\n\nLaravel already gave you some. Open `database/migrations/0001_01_01_000000_create_users_table.php` (the first part is shown, shortened):\n\n```php\n<?php\n\nuse Illuminate\\Database\\Migrations\\Migration;\nuse Illuminate\\Database\\Schema\\Blueprint;\nuse Illuminate\\Support\\Facades\\Schema;\n\nreturn new class extends Migration\n{\n    public function up(): void\n    {\n        Schema::create('users', function (Blueprint $table) {\n            $table->id();\n            $table->string('name');\n            $table->string('email')->unique();\n            $table->timestamp('email_verified_at')->nullable();\n            $table->string('password');\n            $table->rememberToken();\n            $table->timestamps();\n        });\n    }\n\n    public function down(): void\n    {\n        Schema::dropIfExists('users');\n    }\n};\n```\n\n| Part | Meaning |\n|------|---------|\n| File name | A date-time stamp, then a description. The stamp sets the **order** |\n| `return new class extends Migration` | An **anonymous class**: a class with no name, used once |\n| `up()` | What to do: create the `users` table |\n| `down()` | How to undo it: drop the table |\n| `Schema::create('users', ...)` | Make a new table called `users` |\n| `$table->id()` | An auto-increment `BIGINT UNSIGNED` primary key called `id` |\n| `$table->string('name')` | A `VARCHAR(255)` column |\n| `->unique()` | No two rows may share this value |\n| `->nullable()` | The column may be empty (`NULL`) |\n| `$table->timestamps()` | The `created_at` and `updated_at` columns |\n\nCompare it with the SQL you wrote in Chapter 13. It is the same idea, written in PHP. Laravel turns it into the right SQL for MySQL, SQLite and other databases.\n\n## 🧾 How Laravel Remembers\n\nWhen you ran `php artisan migrate` in Chapter 3, Laravel made a table called `migrations`. It records every migration that has run, and in which **batch** (group run together):\n\n| id | migration | batch |\n|----|-----------|-------|\n| 1 | `0001_01_01_000000_create_users_table` | 1 |\n| 2 | `0001_01_01_000001_create_cache_table` | 1 |\n| 3 | `0001_01_01_000002_create_jobs_table` | 1 |\n\nNext time you run `migrate`, Laravel skips those and only runs **new** files. That is why it is safe to run `migrate` again and again.\n\n## 🧰 The Main Commands\n\nCheck what has run:\n\n```bash\nphp artisan migrate:status\n```\n\nOutput (dots and spacing depend on your window):\n\n```text\n  Migration name .................................. Batch / Status\n  0001_01_01_000000_create_users_table ................... [1] Ran\n  0001_01_01_000001_create_cache_table ................... [1] Ran\n  0001_01_01_000002_create_jobs_table .................... [1] Ran\n```\n\nA file that has not run yet shows `Pending`.\n\n| Command | What it does | Deletes data? |\n|---------|--------------|---------------|\n| `migrate` | Run new migrations | No |\n| `migrate:status` | List each migration and whether it ran | No |\n| `migrate:rollback` | Run `down()` for the **last batch** | Yes, for those tables |\n| `migrate:rollback --step=1` | Undo only the last single migration | Yes, for that table |\n| `migrate:refresh` | Roll back everything, then migrate again | **Yes, all** |\n| `migrate:fresh` | **Drop every table**, then migrate | **Yes, all** |\n\n### Rolling back\n\n```bash\nphp artisan migrate:rollback\n```\n\nOutput (times will differ):\n\n```text\n   INFO  Rolling back migrations.\n\n  0001_01_01_000002_create_jobs_table .................. 21.45ms DONE\n  0001_01_01_000001_create_cache_table .................. 9.87ms DONE\n  0001_01_01_000000_create_users_table ................. 18.02ms DONE\n```\n\nAll three ran in batch 1, so all three are undone, newest first. Run `php artisan migrate` to bring them back (empty).\n\n## 🛡️ Data is a Trust (*Amānah*)\n\n`migrate:fresh` and `migrate:refresh` delete **every row** in your database. On your practice computer, that is fine; you can add sample data again (Chapter 18). On a real school server, those rows are real members' records. Losing them breaks a trust. Before any command that deletes data:\n\n1. Check **which** database `.env` points to.\n2. Make a backup (phpMyAdmin → Export).\n3. Never run `migrate:fresh` on a live server.\n\nLaravel helps: when `APP_ENV=production`, it asks you to confirm before running destructive migration commands.\n\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\n\n**Editing a migration that already ran.**\n\nYou open `create_users_table.php`, add `$table->string('phone');` and run `php artisan migrate`. The output is:\n\n```text\n   INFO  Nothing to migrate.\n```\n\nLaravel sees the file in the `migrations` table and skips it. Your new column never appears. Your teammates have the same problem. The rule: **once a migration has run and been shared, never edit it. Write a new migration** that adds the column (Chapter 17).\n\n(On your own practice project, before sharing, `migrate:rollback` and then `migrate` is fine.)\n\n## ✍️ Try It\n\nRun `php artisan migrate:status`, then `php artisan migrate:rollback`, then `migrate:status` again. What changed? Finish with `php artisan migrate`.\n\n*Hint:* after the rollback, the status column shows `Pending`.\n\n## 📝 Recap\n\n- A migration is a PHP file that describes one change to the database, kept in `database/migrations/`.\n- `up()` makes the change; `down()` undoes it.\n- The `migrations` table remembers what has run, in batches.\n- `migrate` runs new files; `migrate:rollback` undoes the last batch.\n- `migrate:fresh` and `migrate:refresh` delete all data: back up first, never on a live server.\n- Never edit a migration that has run and been shared; write a new one.\n\n## 🏆 Practice\n\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\nWhat is the job of `up()` and of `down()` in a migration?\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\n`up()` makes the change, such as creating a table. `down()` undoes it, such as dropping that table. `migrate` runs `up()`; `migrate:rollback` runs `down()`.\n\n</details>\n\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\nYour teammate runs `php artisan migrate` and sees `Nothing to migrate.`, but the new `phone` column is missing. What went wrong, and what should they do?\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\nSomeone edited an old migration that had already run, so Laravel skipped it. They should undo that edit and create a **new** migration that adds the `phone` column, then run `php artisan migrate`.\n\n</details>\n\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\nWrite a short \"Database Safety Card\" for your class, in five steps, for anyone about to change the library database on the school server.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\n\n1. Check `.env`: which database am I connected to?\n2. Export a backup in phpMyAdmin and keep it safe.\n3. Run `php artisan migrate:status` to see what will run.\n4. Only use `php artisan migrate` on the server. Never `migrate:fresh` or `migrate:refresh`.\n5. Check the app afterwards. If something broke, `migrate:rollback --step=1` and restore the backup if needed.\n\n</details>\n",
  "part-06-migrations/ch17-creating-migrations.md": "# Chapter 17: Creating Migrations\n\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\n\nA carpenter is asked to build the same bookshelf for five schools. He does not guess each time. He writes a **plan**: \"Three shelves, each 90 cm wide, the top one for large atlases.\" Any carpenter can follow the plan and build an identical shelf. When a school later asks for a fourth shelf, he writes a **new note** on the plan instead of rubbing out the old one. You are now going to write the plans for your library's tables.\n\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\n\n- Create a migration with `php artisan make:migration`.\n- Choose column types and modifiers such as `nullable()`, `default()` and `unique()`.\n- Link tables with `foreignId()->constrained()`.\n- Add a column to an existing table with a new migration.\n- Create a model and its migration together with `make:model -m`.\n\n## 🧹 First: Remove the Hand-Made Tables\n\nIn Part 5 you created `books`, `members` and `loans` by hand. Now migrations will create them, so remove the old ones. In phpMyAdmin, run this on your **practice** database (loans first, because it points to the other two):\n\n```sql\nDROP TABLE loans;\nDROP TABLE members;\nDROP TABLE books;\n```\n\nYour models stay. They do not care how a table was made.\n\n## 🛠️ Make a Migration\n\n```bash\nphp artisan make:migration create_books_table\n```\n\nOutput (your date and time will differ):\n\n```text\n   INFO  Migration [C:\\xampp\\htdocs\\library-app\\database\\migrations\\2026_10_13_101500_create_books_table.php] created successfully.\n```\n\nLaravel reads the name `create_books_table` and guesses you want to create a table called `books`. The new file already contains `Schema::create('books', ...)` with `id()` and `timestamps()`. Fill in the columns:\n\n```php\n// database/migrations/2026_10_13_101500_create_books_table.php\npublic function up(): void\n{\n    Schema::create('books', function (Blueprint $table) {\n        $table->id();\n        $table->string('title');\n        $table->string('author');\n        $table->string('isbn', 20)->unique();\n        $table->unsignedInteger('copies')->default(1);\n        $table->timestamps();\n    });\n}\n\npublic function down(): void\n{\n    Schema::dropIfExists('books');\n}\n```\n\n## 📐 Column Types\n\n| Method | MySQL type | Library example |\n|--------|-----------|-----------------|\n| `id()` | BIGINT UNSIGNED, auto-increment, primary key | `id` |\n| `string('title')` | VARCHAR(255) | title, author, email |\n| `string('isbn', 20)` | VARCHAR(20) | a shorter text |\n| `text('summary')` | TEXT | a long description |\n| `integer('pages')` | INT | number of pages |\n| `unsignedInteger('copies')` | INT UNSIGNED (no negatives) | copies |\n| `unsignedTinyInteger('grade')` | TINYINT UNSIGNED (0–255) | grade |\n| `boolean('is_reference')` | TINYINT(1) | true or false |\n| `date('due_on')` | DATE | due date |\n| `timestamps()` | two TIMESTAMP columns | `created_at`, `updated_at` |\n\n### Modifiers\n\nAdd a **modifier** after the type to change a column's rules:\n\n| Modifier | Meaning |\n|----------|---------|\n| `->nullable()` | The column may be empty (`NULL`) |\n| `->default(1)` | The value to use when none is given |\n| `->unique()` | No two rows may share this value |\n| `->after('author')` | Place the column after another (MySQL only) |\n\n## 👥 The Members Table\n\n```bash\nphp artisan make:migration create_members_table\n```\n\n```php\nSchema::create('members', function (Blueprint $table) {\n    $table->id();\n    $table->string('name');\n    $table->string('email')->unique();\n    $table->unsignedTinyInteger('grade');\n    $table->timestamps();\n});\n```\n\n## 🔗 The Loans Table and Foreign Keys\n\n```bash\nphp artisan make:migration create_loans_table\n```\n\n```php\nSchema::create('loans', function (Blueprint $table) {\n    $table->id();\n    $table->foreignId('book_id')->constrained();\n    $table->foreignId('member_id')->constrained();\n    $table->date('borrowed_on');\n    $table->date('due_on');\n    $table->date('returned_on')->nullable();\n    $table->timestamps();\n});\n```\n\n`foreignId('book_id')` makes a BIGINT UNSIGNED column. `constrained()` adds a **foreign key** to the `books` table. Laravel guesses the table from the column name: `book_id` → `books`. Now MySQL refuses a loan for a book that does not exist, and refuses to delete a book that still has loans. That protects your records.\n\n(If you wanted a book's loans deleted together with the book, you would add `->cascadeOnDelete()`. For library records, refusing is safer.)\n\n### Order matters\n\n`loans` points to `books` and `members`, so those must exist first. Migrations run in the order of their date stamps. Because you made `loans` last, it runs last. \n\n## ▶️ Run Them\n\n```bash\nphp artisan migrate\n```\n\nOutput (names and times will differ):\n\n```text\n   INFO  Running migrations.\n\n  2026_10_13_101500_create_books_table ................. 28.40ms DONE\n  2026_10_13_101622_create_members_table ............... 25.13ms DONE\n  2026_10_13_101745_create_loans_table ................. 61.77ms DONE\n```\n\nOpen phpMyAdmin: the three tables are back, empty. Chapter 18 fills them.\n\n## ➕ Changing a Table Later\n\nThe library now wants to store each book's **shelf** code, like `B3`. Do not edit the old migration (Chapter 16 explained why). Write a new one:\n\n```bash\nphp artisan make:migration add_shelf_to_books_table\n```\n\nThe name ends in `to_books_table`, so Laravel uses `Schema::table('books', ...)`, which **changes** an existing table:\n\n```php\npublic function up(): void\n{\n    Schema::table('books', function (Blueprint $table) {\n        $table->string('shelf', 10)->nullable()->after('author');\n    });\n}\n\npublic function down(): void\n{\n    Schema::table('books', function (Blueprint $table) {\n        $table->dropColumn('shelf');\n    });\n}\n```\n\nThe new column is `nullable()` because existing books have no shelf code yet. Run `php artisan migrate` again. Only the new migration runs. Remember to add `'shelf'` to the `$fillable` list in `Book.php`.\n\n## ⚡ Model and Migration Together\n\nFor the next new table, make both in one go:\n\n```bash\nphp artisan make:model Donation -m\n```\n\nOutput:\n\n```text\n   INFO  Model [C:\\xampp\\htdocs\\library-app\\app\\Models\\Donation.php] created successfully.\n\n   INFO  Migration [C:\\xampp\\htdocs\\library-app\\database\\migrations\\2026_10_13_102210_create_donations_table.php] created successfully.\n```\n\n`-m` means \"also make a migration\". Laravel names the table `donations` for you.\n\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\n\n**Creating the loans migration before the books migration.**\n\nIf `create_loans_table` has an earlier date stamp than `create_books_table`, `migrate` stops with an error. XAMPP's database (MariaDB) says something like:\n\n```text\nSQLSTATE[HY000]: General error: 1005 Can't create table `library_app`.`loans` (errno: 150 \"Foreign key constraint is incorrectly formed\")\n```\n\n(MySQL 8 words it differently: `Failed to open the referenced table 'books'`.)\n\nThe foreign key points to a table that does not exist yet. Fix it by renaming the loans file so its date stamp is **later** than the books and members files, then run `php artisan migrate` again.\n\n**Forgetting `down()`.** If `down()` is empty, `migrate:rollback` says DONE but the column stays. Always write the opposite of `up()`.\n\n## ✍️ Try It\n\nDrop the hand-made tables, write the three migrations above, and run `php artisan migrate`. Then add the `shelf` column with a second migration and check it in phpMyAdmin.\n\n*Hint:* `php artisan migrate:status` shows which files ran.\n\n## 📝 Recap\n\n- `php artisan make:migration create_books_table` makes a migration file for a new table.\n- Columns: `string`, `text`, `integer`, `unsignedInteger`, `boolean`, `date`, `timestamps`; modifiers: `nullable`, `default`, `unique`.\n- `foreignId('book_id')->constrained()` links to `books.id` and protects your records.\n- Add columns later with a new `add_..._to_books_table` migration and `Schema::table`.\n- `php artisan make:model Name -m` makes a model and its migration together.\n\n## 🏆 Practice\n\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\nWhich method creates a new table, and which changes an existing one?\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\n`Schema::create('table', ...)` creates a new table. `Schema::table('table', ...)` changes an existing one.\n\n</details>\n\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\nWrite the `up()` method for an `add_pages_to_books_table` migration: a whole-number `pages` column that may be empty, placed after `copies`. Then write `down()`.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\n```php\npublic function up(): void\n{\n    Schema::table('books', function (Blueprint $table) {\n        $table->unsignedInteger('pages')->nullable()->after('copies');\n    });\n}\n\npublic function down(): void\n{\n    Schema::table('books', function (Blueprint $table) {\n        $table->dropColumn('pages');\n    });\n}\n```\n\n</details>\n\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\nThe library accepts **book donations** from families and records them.\n\n1. Make the `Donation` model and migration with one command.\n2. Columns: `donor_name`, an optional `donor_email`, `book_id` linked to `books`, `quantity` (at least 1 by default), `received_on` (a date), timestamps.\n3. Write `down()`.\n4. Add a `donations()` relationship to `Book` and a `book()` relationship to `Donation`.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\n\n```bash\nphp artisan make:model Donation -m\n```\n\n```php\n// database/migrations/..._create_donations_table.php\npublic function up(): void\n{\n    Schema::create('donations', function (Blueprint $table) {\n        $table->id();\n        $table->string('donor_name');\n        $table->string('donor_email')->nullable();\n        $table->foreignId('book_id')->constrained();\n        $table->unsignedInteger('quantity')->default(1);\n        $table->date('received_on');\n        $table->timestamps();\n    });\n}\n\npublic function down(): void\n{\n    Schema::dropIfExists('donations');\n}\n```\n\n```php\n// app/Models/Book.php\npublic function donations(): HasMany\n{\n    return $this->hasMany(Donation::class);\n}\n\n// app/Models/Donation.php\npublic function book(): BelongsTo\n{\n    return $this->belongsTo(Book::class);\n}\n```\n\n</details>\n",
  "part-06-migrations/ch18-seeders-and-factories.md": "# Chapter 18: Seeders & Factories\n\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\n\nBefore a new library opens, the staff practise. They fill the shelves with **practice books**, make **sample library cards** for pretend members, and rehearse lending them. That way they can test the system before real students arrive. Seeders and factories do the same for your app. They fill an empty database with sample data in seconds, so you can test every page.\n\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\n\n- Explain the difference between a **seeder** and a **factory**.\n- Write a seeder that adds fixed rows, and run it with `db:seed`.\n- Write a factory that makes many realistic fake rows with `fake()`.\n- Call seeders from `DatabaseSeeder` and rebuild everything with `migrate:fresh --seed`.\n- Keep sample data honest and free of real people's details.\n\n## 🌱 Seeders and Factories\n\n| Tool | What it does | Library example |\n|------|--------------|-----------------|\n| **Seeder** | A class that puts rows into the database | \"Add these 4 known books\" |\n| **Factory** | A recipe for making **one** fake row, used many times | \"Make 30 pretend members\" |\n\nSeeders often **use** factories: \"make 20 books using the book recipe.\"\n\n## 📗 A Seeder with Fixed Rows\n\n```bash\nphp artisan make:seeder BookSeeder\n```\n\nOutput:\n\n```text\n   INFO  Seeder [C:\\xampp\\htdocs\\library-app\\database\\seeders\\BookSeeder.php] created successfully.\n```\n\nFill in `run()`:\n\n```php\n// database/seeders/BookSeeder.php\nnamespace Database\\Seeders;\n\nuse App\\Models\\Book;\nuse Illuminate\\Database\\Seeder;\n\nclass BookSeeder extends Seeder\n{\n    public function run(): void\n    {\n        Book::create(['title' => 'Clean Water for Every Village', 'author' => 'Aisha Rahman', 'isbn' => '978-0-00-000001-1', 'copies' => 3]);\n        Book::create(['title' => 'The Story of Numbers', 'author' => 'Yusuf Ali Khan', 'isbn' => '978-0-00-000002-8', 'copies' => 0]);\n        Book::create(['title' => 'Bees and Their Hives', 'author' => 'Maryam Siddiqui', 'isbn' => '978-0-00-000003-5', 'copies' => 1]);\n        Book::create(['title' => 'Stars Over the Desert', 'author' => 'Hamza Qureshi', 'isbn' => '978-0-00-000004-2', 'copies' => 2]);\n    }\n}\n```\n\n`create()` works because `Book` has `$fillable` (Chapter 14). Run just this seeder:\n\n```bash\nphp artisan db:seed --class=BookSeeder\n```\n\nOutput:\n\n```text\n   INFO  Seeding database.\n```\n\nCheck in Tinker: `Book::count()` gives `= 4`.\n\n## 🏭 A Factory for Many Rows\n\nTyping 30 members by hand is slow. A **factory** describes what one member looks like, using **fake** values:\n\n```bash\nphp artisan make:factory MemberFactory\n```\n\n```php\n// database/factories/MemberFactory.php\nnamespace Database\\Factories;\n\nuse Illuminate\\Database\\Eloquent\\Factories\\Factory;\n\nclass MemberFactory extends Factory\n{\n    public function definition(): array\n    {\n        return [\n            'name' => fake()->name(),\n            'email' => fake()->unique()->safeEmail(),\n            'grade' => fake()->numberBetween(6, 10),\n        ];\n    }\n}\n```\n\n`fake()` gives you **Faker**, a tool that invents realistic data:\n\n| Code | Example value |\n|------|---------------|\n| `fake()->name()` | `Lina Hart` |\n| `fake()->safeEmail()` | `lina.hart@example.org` |\n| `fake()->numberBetween(6, 10)` | `8` |\n| `fake()->sentence(4)` | `Quia et sunt autem.` |\n| `fake()->isbn13()` | `9781234567897` |\n| `fake()->dateTimeBetween('-1 month')` | a date in the last month |\n\nThe values are random, so yours will differ. `unique()` makes sure no two members get the same email. `safeEmail()` only uses example domains, so no real inbox ever receives mail from your tests.\n\n### Let the model use its factory\n\nAdd the `HasFactory` trait to the model. (A **trait** is a bundle of methods you can add to a class; you met it in PHP OOP.)\n\n```php\n// app/Models/Member.php\nuse Illuminate\\Database\\Eloquent\\Factories\\HasFactory;\n\nclass Member extends Model\n{\n    use HasFactory;\n\n    // ... $fillable and loans() as before\n}\n```\n\nLaravel finds `MemberFactory` by its name. Try it in Tinker:\n\n```text\n> Member::factory()->count(3)->create()->pluck('grade')\n= Illuminate\\Support\\Collection {#6340\n    all: [\n      9,\n      6,\n      10,\n    ],\n  }\n```\n\n(Your grades will differ.) `make()` instead of `create()` builds the objects **without** saving them, which is handy for tests.\n\nYou can fix some values and leave the rest random:\n\n```php\nMember::factory()->count(5)->create(['grade' => 10]);\n```\n\n## 🧺 `DatabaseSeeder`: Run Everything\n\n`database/seeders/DatabaseSeeder.php` is the main seeder. `php artisan db:seed` runs it. Make it call the others in the right order:\n\n```php\n// database/seeders/DatabaseSeeder.php\nuse App\\Models\\Book;\nuse App\\Models\\Loan;\nuse App\\Models\\Member;\n\npublic function run(): void\n{\n    $this->call(BookSeeder::class);        // 4 known books\n\n    Member::factory()->count(30)->create(); // 30 pretend members\n\n    // 10 sample loans, each for a random book and member\n    for ($i = 0; $i < 10; $i++) {\n        Loan::create([\n            'book_id' => Book::inRandomOrder()->first()->id,\n            'member_id' => Member::inRandomOrder()->first()->id,\n            'borrowed_on' => now()->subDays(10),\n            'due_on' => now()->addDays(4),\n        ]);\n    }\n}\n```\n\n`inRandomOrder()->first()` picks one random row. `now()->subDays(10)` means \"10 days ago\".\n\n(Laravel's starter `DatabaseSeeder` also makes one test user. You can keep that part; you will need a user in Part 8.)\n\n## 🔄 Start Fresh with Sample Data\n\nWhile building, you often want a clean database full of sample data. One command does it:\n\n```bash\nphp artisan migrate:fresh --seed\n```\n\nIt **drops every table**, runs all migrations, then runs `DatabaseSeeder`. Output (shortened, times differ):\n\n```text\n  Dropping all tables .................................. 92.40ms DONE\n\n   INFO  Preparing database.\n\n  Creating migration table ............................. 14.02ms DONE\n\n   INFO  Running migrations.\n\n  0001_01_01_000000_create_users_table ................. 40.11ms DONE\n  ...\n\n   INFO  Seeding database.\n\n  Database\\Seeders\\BookSeeder ............................ RUNNING\n  Database\\Seeders\\BookSeeder ........................ 12.84 ms DONE\n```\n\nRemember Chapter 16: this deletes all data. Use it only on your practice database.\n\n## 🤝 Honest Sample Data (*Amānah*)\n\n- **Never seed real people.** Do not copy your classmates' names, emails or phone numbers into a seeder. Seeders live in Git, and Git is shared. Use `fake()` or clearly made-up names.\n- **Label it.** If you show the app to your teacher with sample data, say that it is sample data.\n- **Keep it out of real servers.** Seeders that make fake members are for practice databases only.\n\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\n\n**Forgetting `HasFactory` on the model.**\n\n```text\n> Member::factory()->count(3)->create()\n   BadMethodCallException  Call to undefined method App\\Models\\Member::factory().\n```\n\nFix: add `use HasFactory;` inside the class and the matching `use` line at the top. Then restart Tinker.\n\n**Unique fields without `unique()`.** If the factory used `fake()->safeEmail()` without `unique()`, two members might get the same email. The database's `unique` rule would stop the seeder with `Integrity constraint violation: 1062 Duplicate entry`.\n\n## ✍️ Try It\n\nWrite `BookSeeder` and `MemberFactory`, update `DatabaseSeeder`, then run `php artisan migrate:fresh --seed`. Check how many rows each table has in phpMyAdmin.\n\n*Hint:* you should see 4 books, 30 members and 10 loans (plus the test user).\n\n## 📝 Recap\n\n- A **seeder** puts rows into the database; a **factory** is a recipe for one fake row.\n- `php artisan db:seed` runs `DatabaseSeeder`; `--class=BookSeeder` runs one seeder.\n- `fake()` makes realistic random values; `unique()` avoids duplicates.\n- Models need `use HasFactory;` to use `Model::factory()`.\n- `php artisan migrate:fresh --seed` rebuilds everything with sample data. Practice databases only.\n\n## 🏆 Practice\n\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\nWhat is the difference between `Member::factory()->make()` and `Member::factory()->create()`?\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\n`make()` builds a `Member` object but does **not** save it. `create()` builds it **and** saves it to the database.\n\n</details>\n\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\nWrite a `BookFactory` `definition()` that makes a title of three words with capital letters, a fake name as author, a unique ISBN-13, and 0 to 5 copies.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\n```php\n// database/factories/BookFactory.php\npublic function definition(): array\n{\n    return [\n        'title' => ucwords(fake()->words(3, true)),\n        'author' => fake()->name(),\n        'isbn' => fake()->unique()->isbn13(),\n        'copies' => fake()->numberBetween(0, 5),\n    ];\n}\n```\n\n`words(3, true)` returns the words as one string; `ucwords()` capitalises each word. Remember `use HasFactory;` in `Book`.\n\n</details>\n\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\nMake the sample data more realistic.\n\n1. Use `BookFactory` to add 20 more books after the 4 known ones.\n2. Make 25 student members, plus 5 staff members. Staff have no grade, so give them grade `0` to mean \"staff\".\n3. Make 15 loans where about a third are already returned.\n4. Rebuild with `migrate:fresh --seed`.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\n\n```php\n// database/seeders/DatabaseSeeder.php\npublic function run(): void\n{\n    $this->call(BookSeeder::class);\n    Book::factory()->count(20)->create();\n\n    Member::factory()->count(25)->create();\n    Member::factory()->count(5)->create(['grade' => 0]);   // staff\n\n    for ($i = 1; $i <= 15; $i++) {\n        $borrowed = now()->subDays(fake()->numberBetween(1, 20));\n\n        Loan::create([\n            'book_id' => Book::inRandomOrder()->first()->id,\n            'member_id' => Member::inRandomOrder()->first()->id,\n            'borrowed_on' => $borrowed,\n            'due_on' => $borrowed->copy()->addDays(14),\n            'returned_on' => $i % 3 === 0 ? now() : null,\n        ]);\n    }\n}\n```\n\nEvery third loan (`$i % 3 === 0`) is marked as returned, so 5 of the 15 are returned. `copy()` makes a separate date, so adding 14 days does not change `$borrowed`. A clearer design for staff would be a separate `role` column; you could add it with a migration.\n\n</details>\n",
  "part-07-forms-and-validation/ch19-laravel-forms.md": "# Chapter 19: Laravel Forms\n\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\n\nWhen a family donates a book, the librarian hands them a **paper form**: title, author, number of copies. They fill it in and drop it in the tray at the desk. The librarian reads the form, adds the book to the register, and smiles: \"Thank you, it has been added!\" Web forms work the same way. The browser fills the tray (sends a request), the controller reads it, Eloquent saves it, and the page says thank you.\n\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\n\n- Build an \"Add a book\" form that sends a POST request to `books.store`.\n- Read the submitted data in the controller with `$request`.\n- Save it with Eloquent, redirect, and show a **flash message**.\n- Build an \"Edit\" form that sends PUT with `@method('PUT')`.\n- Build a safe \"Delete\" button that sends DELETE.\n\n## 🗺️ The Plan\n\nWe use the resource routes from Chapter 8:\n\n```php\n// routes/web.php\nRoute::resource('books', BookController::class);\n```\n\n| Step | Route | Method |\n|------|-------|--------|\n| Show the empty form | GET `/books/create` | `create` |\n| Save the new book | POST `/books` | `store` |\n| Show the filled form | GET `/books/{book}/edit` | `edit` |\n| Save the changes | PUT `/books/{book}` | `update` |\n| Delete the book | DELETE `/books/{book}` | `destroy` |\n\n## ➕ The \"Add a Book\" Form\n\n```php\n// app/Http/Controllers/BookController.php\npublic function create()\n{\n    return view('books.create');\n}\n```\n\n```blade\n{{-- resources/views/books/create.blade.php --}}\n@extends('layouts.app')\n\n@section('title', 'Add a Book')\n\n@section('content')\n<h2>Add a Book</h2>\n\n<form method=\"POST\" action=\"{{ route('books.store') }}\">\n    @csrf\n\n    <label for=\"title\">Title</label>\n    <input type=\"text\" id=\"title\" name=\"title\">\n\n    <label for=\"author\">Author</label>\n    <input type=\"text\" id=\"author\" name=\"author\">\n\n    <label for=\"isbn\">ISBN</label>\n    <input type=\"text\" id=\"isbn\" name=\"isbn\">\n\n    <label for=\"copies\">Copies</label>\n    <input type=\"number\" id=\"copies\" name=\"copies\" value=\"1\" min=\"0\">\n\n    <button type=\"submit\">Save book</button>\n</form>\n@endsection\n```\n\nThree things to notice:\n\n1. `method=\"POST\"`: adding data is never a GET.\n2. `action=\"{{ route('books.store') }}\"`: use the route **name**, not a typed URL.\n3. **`@csrf`**: every Laravel form needs this line. It adds a hidden security token. Without it, Laravel rejects the form with a \"419 Page Expired\" error. Chapter 21 explains why.\n\nEach `<label for=\"...\">` matches an input's `id`, so screen readers and taps on the label work.\n\n## 📥 Read and Save in `store`\n\n```php\n// app/Http/Controllers/BookController.php\nuse App\\Models\\Book;\nuse Illuminate\\Http\\Request;\n\npublic function store(Request $request)\n{\n    $data = $request->only(['title', 'author', 'isbn', 'copies']);\n\n    Book::create($data);\n\n    return redirect()\n        ->route('books.index')\n        ->with('status', 'Thank you! The book has been added.');\n}\n```\n\n- `Request $request` gives you everything the browser sent. Laravel fills it in automatically.\n- `$request->input('title')` reads one field. `$request->only([...])` reads several as an array, and **ignores any other fields**. That works with `$fillable` to block sneaky extras.\n- After saving, we **redirect**. If we showed a page straight away, pressing the browser's refresh button would send the form again and add the book twice. This pattern is called **Post/Redirect/Get**.\n\n> ⚠️ This `store` saves whatever was typed, even an empty title. Chapter 20 adds **validation**. Never put a form online without it.\n\n## 💬 Flash Messages\n\n`->with('status', '...')` stores a message in the session for **one** page view only. This is called a **flash** message. Show it in your layout, so every page can use it:\n\n```blade\n{{-- resources/views/layouts/app.blade.php, above @yield('content') --}}\n@if (session('status'))\n    <div class=\"alert\">{{ session('status') }}</div>\n@endif\n```\n\nAfter saving, the book list shows:\n\n```text\nThank you! The book has been added.\n```\n\nRefresh the page, and the message is gone, because flash messages last for one visit.\n\n## ✏️ The Edit Form\n\n```php\npublic function edit(Book $book)\n{\n    return view('books.edit', ['book' => $book]);\n}\n```\n\n```blade\n{{-- resources/views/books/edit.blade.php --}}\n<h2>Edit: {{ $book->title }}</h2>\n\n<form method=\"POST\" action=\"{{ route('books.update', $book) }}\">\n    @csrf\n    @method('PUT')\n\n    <label for=\"title\">Title</label>\n    <input type=\"text\" id=\"title\" name=\"title\" value=\"{{ $book->title }}\">\n\n    <label for=\"copies\">Copies</label>\n    <input type=\"number\" id=\"copies\" name=\"copies\" value=\"{{ $book->copies }}\" min=\"0\">\n\n    <button type=\"submit\">Save changes</button>\n</form>\n```\n\n- `route('books.update', $book)` builds `/books/3` from the book object.\n- HTML forms can only send GET or POST. `@method('PUT')` adds a hidden field, `<input type=\"hidden\" name=\"_method\" value=\"PUT\">`. Laravel reads it and treats the request as PUT, so it reaches `update`.\n- `value=\"{{ $book->title }}\"` fills in the current values, safely escaped.\n\n```php\npublic function update(Request $request, Book $book)\n{\n    $book->update($request->only(['title', 'copies']));\n\n    return redirect()\n        ->route('books.show', $book)\n        ->with('status', 'Book updated.');\n}\n```\n\n## 🗑️ The Delete Button\n\nDeleting changes data, so it must **not** be a link. Use a small form with `@method('DELETE')`:\n\n```blade\n<form method=\"POST\" action=\"{{ route('books.destroy', $book) }}\"\n      onsubmit=\"return confirm('Remove this book from the library?');\">\n    @csrf\n    @method('DELETE')\n    <button type=\"submit\">Delete</button>\n</form>\n```\n\n```php\npublic function destroy(Book $book)\n{\n    $book->delete();\n\n    return redirect()\n        ->route('books.index')\n        ->with('status', 'Book removed.');\n}\n```\n\n`confirm()` shows an \"Are you sure?\" box in the browser. It is a kindness to the user, not a security check. The real protection is that only librarians may reach this route (Chapter 24).\n\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\n\n**Forgetting `@csrf`.**\n\n```blade\n<form method=\"POST\" action=\"{{ route('books.store') }}\">\n    <input type=\"text\" name=\"title\">\n    <button>Save</button>\n</form>\n```\n\nWhat happens: after pressing Save, Laravel shows **419 | Page Expired**. Add `@csrf` as the first line inside every POST form.\n\n**Forgetting `@method('PUT')` in the edit form.** The browser sends POST to `/books/3`, but no POST route exists for that URL. With `APP_DEBUG=true`, Laravel shows `The POST method is not supported for route books/3. Supported methods: GET, HEAD, PUT, PATCH, DELETE.` (HTTP code 405). Add `@method('PUT')`.\n\n## ✍️ Try It\n\nAdd the create, edit and delete forms to your library app. Add a book, change its copies, then delete it. Watch the flash messages and phpMyAdmin.\n\n*Hint:* add links to the forms in your book list: `<a href=\"{{ route('books.create') }}\">Add a book</a>` and `<a href=\"{{ route('books.edit', $book) }}\">Edit</a>`.\n\n## 📝 Recap\n\n- Forms use `method=\"POST\"`, `action=\"{{ route(...) }}\"` and always `@csrf`.\n- `Request $request` holds the submitted data; `$request->only([...])` takes just the fields you want.\n- Save, then **redirect** with `->with('status', '...')` for a one-time flash message.\n- `@method('PUT')` and `@method('DELETE')` let a POST form reach `update` and `destroy`.\n- Deleting is always a form, never a link. Validation comes next, in Chapter 20.\n\n## 🏆 Practice\n\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\nWhy do we redirect after saving a form instead of showing a page straight away?\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\nSo that refreshing the page does not send the form again. Without the redirect, a refresh would save the book a second time. This is the Post/Redirect/Get pattern.\n\n</details>\n\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\nFix this edit form. It has two problems.\n\n```blade\n<form method=\"PUT\" action=\"/books/{{ $book->id }}\">\n    <input type=\"text\" name=\"title\" value=\"{{ $book->title }}\">\n    <button>Save</button>\n</form>\n```\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\n```blade\n<form method=\"POST\" action=\"{{ route('books.update', $book) }}\">\n    @csrf\n    @method('PUT')\n    <input type=\"text\" name=\"title\" value=\"{{ $book->title }}\">\n    <button>Save</button>\n</form>\n```\n\n1. HTML forms cannot use `method=\"PUT\"`; use POST plus `@method('PUT')`.\n2. `@csrf` was missing. (Using `route()` instead of a typed URL is also better.)\n\n</details>\n\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\nBuild a \"New Member\" form.\n\n1. Fields: name, email (use `type=\"email\"`), grade (a `<select>` with grades 6 to 10).\n2. `MemberController@store` saves with `only()` and redirects to `members.index` with `Welcome to the library, NAME!`\n3. Show the flash message in the layout.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\n\n```blade\n{{-- resources/views/members/create.blade.php --}}\n<form method=\"POST\" action=\"{{ route('members.store') }}\">\n    @csrf\n    <label for=\"name\">Name</label>\n    <input type=\"text\" id=\"name\" name=\"name\">\n\n    <label for=\"email\">Email</label>\n    <input type=\"email\" id=\"email\" name=\"email\">\n\n    <label for=\"grade\">Grade</label>\n    <select id=\"grade\" name=\"grade\">\n        @for ($g = 6; $g <= 10; $g++)\n            <option value=\"{{ $g }}\">Grade {{ $g }}</option>\n        @endfor\n    </select>\n\n    <button type=\"submit\">Join the library</button>\n</form>\n```\n\n```php\n// app/Http/Controllers/MemberController.php\npublic function store(Request $request)\n{\n    $member = Member::create($request->only(['name', 'email', 'grade']));\n\n    return redirect()\n        ->route('members.index')\n        ->with('status', \"Welcome to the library, {$member->name}!\");\n}\n```\n\nThe flash message is printed with `{{ session('status') }}`, so a name with HTML in it is shown safely as text.\n\n</details>\n",
  "part-07-forms-and-validation/ch20-validation-rules.md": "# Chapter 20: Validation Rules\n\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\n\nA careful librarian never adds a donation form to the register without checking it first. Is the title filled in? Is the number of copies a real number, not \"lots\"? Is this ISBN already in our register? If something is wrong, she hands the form back with a kind note: \"Please fill in the author.\" She does not make you write everything again; she circles only the problem. Laravel **validation** does exactly this for your forms.\n\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\n\n- Check form input with `$request->validate([...])`.\n- Use common rules: `required`, `string`, `max`, `integer`, `min`, `email`, `unique`, `exists`, `date`.\n- Show error messages with `@error` and `$errors`.\n- Keep what the user typed with `old()`.\n- Allow a record's own value in `unique` when updating.\n\n## 🛂 Why Validate on the Server?\n\nYou can add `required` to an HTML input, and the browser will check it. That is helpful, but it is **not** protection. Anyone can switch off browser checks or send a request without a browser. **The server must always check.** Validation is part of the trust (*amānah*) of keeping the library's records correct.\n\n## ✅ `$request->validate()`\n\nReplace `$request->only(...)` in `store` from Chapter 19:\n\n```php\n// app/Http/Controllers/BookController.php\npublic function store(Request $request)\n{\n    $validated = $request->validate([\n        'title' => 'required|string|max:255',\n        'author' => 'required|string|max:255',\n        'isbn' => 'required|string|max:20|unique:books,isbn',\n        'copies' => 'required|integer|min:0',\n    ]);\n\n    Book::create($validated);\n\n    return redirect()\n        ->route('books.index')\n        ->with('status', 'Thank you! The book has been added.');\n}\n```\n\nHow it works:\n\n```text\nForm sent\n    |\n    v\nvalidate() checks every rule\n    |\n    +-- all pass --> returns clean data\n    |                -> create() -> redirect\n    |\n    +-- any fail --> goes BACK to the form\n                     with errors + old input\n```\n\n- If every rule passes, `validate()` returns an array of **only the validated fields**. Extra fields are dropped.\n- If any rule fails, Laravel **stops** the method, sends the user back to the form, and stores the error messages and the typed values for one page view.\n\nRules are joined with `|`. You can also write them as an array: `'title' => ['required', 'string', 'max:255']`.\n\n## 📏 Common Rules\n\n| Rule | Passes when the value… |\n|------|-------------------------|\n| `required` | is present and not empty |\n| `string` | is text |\n| `max:255` | is at most 255 characters (or at most 255 for numbers) |\n| `min:0` | is at least 0 (for numbers) or 0 characters |\n| `integer` | is a whole number |\n| `email` | looks like an email address |\n| `date` | is a valid date |\n| `after_or_equal:today` | is today or later |\n| `unique:books,isbn` | is not already in the `isbn` column of `books` |\n| `exists:members,id` | **is** in the `id` column of `members` |\n| `nullable` | may be empty (other rules then skip it) |\n\n## 🖍️ Showing Errors\n\nLaravel puts the messages in a variable called `$errors`, which every view has. Show them next to each field with `@error`:\n\n```blade\n{{-- resources/views/books/create.blade.php --}}\n<form method=\"POST\" action=\"{{ route('books.store') }}\">\n    @csrf\n\n    <label for=\"title\">Title</label>\n    <input type=\"text\" id=\"title\" name=\"title\" value=\"{{ old('title') }}\">\n    @error('title')\n        <p class=\"error\">{{ $message }}</p>\n    @enderror\n\n    <label for=\"copies\">Copies</label>\n    <input type=\"number\" id=\"copies\" name=\"copies\" value=\"{{ old('copies', 1) }}\">\n    @error('copies')\n        <p class=\"error\">{{ $message }}</p>\n    @enderror\n\n    {{-- author and isbn fields follow the same pattern --}}\n\n    <button type=\"submit\">Save book</button>\n</form>\n```\n\n- `@error('title') ... @enderror` runs only if `title` has an error. Inside, `$message` holds the text.\n- `old('title')` puts back what the user typed, so they do not have to type it again. `old('copies', 1)` uses `1` when there is no old value.\n\nSuppose a student leaves the title empty, types `-2` for copies, and enters an ISBN that already exists. The form comes back showing:\n\n```text\nThe title field is required.\nThe copies field must be at least 0.\nThe isbn has already been taken.\n```\n\n### All errors at the top\n\nTo list every error in one box, use `$errors->any()` and `$errors->all()`:\n\n```blade\n@if ($errors->any())\n    <div class=\"error-box\">\n        <p>Please fix these:</p>\n        <ul>\n            @foreach ($errors->all() as $error)\n                <li>{{ $error }}</li>\n            @endforeach\n        </ul>\n    </div>\n@endif\n```\n\n## 💬 Friendlier Messages\n\nPass your own messages as a second array. The key is `field.rule`:\n\n```php\n$validated = $request->validate([\n    'title' => 'required|string|max:255',\n    'isbn' => 'required|string|max:20|unique:books,isbn',\n], [\n    'title.required' => 'Please write the book title.',\n    'isbn.unique' => 'This ISBN is already in our library.',\n]);\n```\n\nNow an empty title shows `Please write the book title.`\n\n## ✏️ Updating: Unique, but Not Against Itself\n\nWhen you edit book 3 and keep its ISBN, `unique:books,isbn` fails, because book 3 itself already has that ISBN. Tell the rule to **ignore** the book being edited:\n\n```php\nuse Illuminate\\Validation\\Rule;\n\npublic function update(Request $request, Book $book)\n{\n    $validated = $request->validate([\n        'title' => 'required|string|max:255',\n        'isbn' => ['required', 'string', 'max:20', Rule::unique('books', 'isbn')->ignore($book)],\n        'copies' => 'required|integer|min:0',\n    ]);\n\n    $book->update($validated);\n\n    return redirect()->route('books.show', $book)->with('status', 'Book updated.');\n}\n```\n\nIn the edit form, use `old()` with the current value as the default:\n\n```blade\n<input type=\"text\" id=\"title\" name=\"title\" value=\"{{ old('title', $book->title) }}\">\n```\n\n## 📚 Validating a Loan\n\n```php\n// app/Http/Controllers/LoanController.php\n$validated = $request->validate([\n    'book_id' => 'required|exists:books,id',\n    'member_id' => 'required|exists:members,id',\n    'due_on' => 'required|date|after_or_equal:today',\n]);\n```\n\n`exists` makes sure nobody borrows a book or uses a member number that is not in the database. If someone picks yesterday as the due date, they see:\n\n```text\nThe due on field must be a date after or equal to today.\n```\n\nLaravel turns `due_on` into `due on` for messages.\n\n## 📦 Bigger Forms: Form Requests\n\nWhen rules grow long, move them out of the controller into a **Form Request** class:\n\n```bash\nphp artisan make:request StoreBookRequest\n```\n\nPut the rules in its `rules()` method, return `true` from its `authorize()` method for now, and type-hint it: `public function store(StoreBookRequest $request)`. Then `$request->validated()` gives the clean data. It works the same way, just tidier.\n\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\n\n**Saving `$request->all()` instead of the validated data.**\n\n```php\n$request->validate([...]);\nBook::create($request->all());   // wrong\n```\n\nWhat happens: every field the browser sent is passed to `create()`, including extra ones you never checked. `$fillable` catches some, but it is easy to slip. Save what `validate()` returned:\n\n```php\n$validated = $request->validate([...]);\nBook::create($validated);         // right\n```\n\n**Missing `old()`.** The form comes back with errors, and every field is empty. Users must type everything again. Add `value=\"{{ old('field') }}\"` to every input.\n\n## ✍️ Try It\n\nAdd validation to your book `store` and `update` methods. Then try to save an empty form, a negative number of copies and a duplicate ISBN. Check each message appears next to the right field.\n\n*Hint:* one book from your seeder has ISBN `978-0-00-000001-1`.\n\n## 📝 Recap\n\n- Always validate on the server with `$validated = $request->validate([...])`.\n- If a rule fails, Laravel goes back to the form with `$errors` and the old input.\n- Show messages with `@error('field') {{ $message }} @enderror`; refill fields with `old()`.\n- `unique` and `exists` check the database; use `Rule::unique(...)->ignore($book)` when updating.\n- Save only the validated data.\n\n## 🏆 Practice\n\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\nWhat does `$request->validate()` do when a rule fails, and what does it return when all rules pass?\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\nWhen a rule fails, it stops the method and sends the user back to the form with error messages and their old input. When all rules pass, it returns an array with only the validated fields.\n\n</details>\n\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\nWrite rules for a new member: name required, at most 100 characters; email required, a valid email, not already used in `members`; grade required, a whole number from 6 to 10.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\n```php\n$validated = $request->validate([\n    'name' => 'required|string|max:100',\n    'email' => 'required|email|unique:members,email',\n    'grade' => 'required|integer|min:6|max:10',\n]);\n```\n\n(`between:6,10` would also work for the grade.)\n\n</details>\n\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\nBuild the \"Borrow a book\" form with full validation.\n\n1. The form has a `<select>` of members and a `<select>` of books with copies left, plus a due date.\n2. Validate with `exists` and `after_or_equal:today`.\n3. After saving the loan with `borrowed_on` set to today, decrease the book's copies by one.\n4. Show `old()` values so a mistake does not reset the choices.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\n\n```php\n// app/Http/Controllers/LoanController.php\npublic function create()\n{\n    return view('loans.create', [\n        'members' => Member::orderBy('name')->get(),\n        'books' => Book::where('copies', '>', 0)->orderBy('title')->get(),\n    ]);\n}\n\npublic function store(Request $request)\n{\n    $validated = $request->validate([\n        'member_id' => 'required|exists:members,id',\n        'book_id' => 'required|exists:books,id',\n        'due_on' => 'required|date|after_or_equal:today',\n    ]);\n\n    $validated['borrowed_on'] = today();\n    $loan = Loan::create($validated);\n    $loan->book->decrement('copies');\n\n    return redirect()->route('loans.index')->with('status', 'Happy reading!');\n}\n```\n\n```blade\n{{-- resources/views/loans/create.blade.php --}}\n<form method=\"POST\" action=\"{{ route('loans.store') }}\">\n    @csrf\n    <label for=\"member_id\">Member</label>\n    <select id=\"member_id\" name=\"member_id\">\n        @foreach ($members as $member)\n            <option value=\"{{ $member->id }}\" @selected(old('member_id') == $member->id)>\n                {{ $member->name }}\n            </option>\n        @endforeach\n    </select>\n    @error('member_id') <p class=\"error\">{{ $message }}</p> @enderror\n\n    {{-- the book select follows the same pattern --}}\n\n    <label for=\"due_on\">Due on</label>\n    <input type=\"date\" id=\"due_on\" name=\"due_on\" value=\"{{ old('due_on') }}\">\n    @error('due_on') <p class=\"error\">{{ $message }}</p> @enderror\n\n    <button type=\"submit\">Borrow</button>\n</form>\n```\n\n`@selected(...)` adds `selected` to the option when the condition is true. A stronger version would also check that the book still has a copy left at the moment of saving.\n\n</details>\n",
  "part-07-forms-and-validation/ch21-csrf-protection.md": "# Chapter 21: CSRF Protection\n\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\n\nThe library only accepts official request slips that carry the librarian's **secret stamp**. One day, a slip appears in the tray: \"Remove all science books from the register.\" It looks real, but it has no stamp. Someone else wrote it and slipped it in. The librarian refuses it, and the books stay safe. Laravel stamps every form it makes with a secret **token**, and refuses any form that arrives without the right one. That protection is called **CSRF protection**.\n\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\n\n- Explain what a **CSRF attack** is, in plain words.\n- Describe how Laravel's CSRF token stops it.\n- Use `@csrf` in every form and understand the \"419 Page Expired\" error.\n- Send the token with JavaScript requests.\n- Explain why GET requests must never change data, and why logout is a POST form.\n\n## 🎭 What is CSRF?\n\n**CSRF** stands for **Cross-Site Request Forgery**. \"Forgery\" means a fake made to look real. \"Cross-site\" means it comes from a different website.\n\nHere is how an attack could work against a library app **without** protection:\n\n1. Mrs Rahman, the librarian, logs in to the library app. Her browser keeps a **session cookie**, so the app knows it is her.\n2. Later, in another tab, she opens a harmful page on a different website.\n3. That page contains a hidden form that sends `DELETE /books/3` to the library app, and submits itself with JavaScript.\n4. Her browser sends the request **with her cookie**, because cookies go with every request to that site.\n5. The app sees a logged-in librarian asking to delete a book, and does it.\n\n```text\n Harmful site           Library app\n+------------+         +-------------+\n| hidden form|-------->| DELETE      |\n| auto-submit| cookie  | /books/3    |\n+------------+  sent   | \"it's her!\" |\n                       +-------------+\n```\n\nShe never clicked \"Delete\". The other site **forged** the request, using her login.\n\nModern browsers make this harder: Laravel's session cookie is marked `SameSite=Lax`, which stops browsers sending it with most requests started by other sites. But older browsers and some tricks get around that, so Laravel adds a stronger guard: the token.\n\n## 🔐 How the Token Stops It\n\nLaravel gives each visitor's session a long random **CSRF token**. Every form made by your app includes that token in a hidden field. When a POST, PUT, PATCH or DELETE request arrives, Laravel compares the token in the form with the one in the session:\n\n```text\nForm token == session token?\n    |\n    +-- yes --> continue to the route\n    |\n    +-- no  --> stop: 419 Page Expired\n```\n\nThe harmful site **cannot read** the token. Browsers do not let one website read another website's pages. So its forged form has no token, or a wrong one, and Laravel refuses it.\n\nThis check is done by middleware called `ValidateCsrfToken`. Laravel runs it on every web route automatically. You do not add it yourself.\n\n## 🏷️ `@csrf` in Every Form\n\n```blade\n<form method=\"POST\" action=\"{{ route('books.store') }}\">\n    @csrf\n    ...\n</form>\n```\n\n`@csrf` prints a hidden input. View the page source and you will see something like:\n\n```html\n<input type=\"hidden\" name=\"_token\" value=\"kQ3vX9...a long random string...\" autocomplete=\"off\">\n```\n\nYour token will be different, and it changes when your session changes. Keep `@csrf` in **every** form that uses POST, including forms with `@method('PUT')` or `@method('DELETE')`.\n\n## ⛔ The 419 Error\n\nIf the token is missing or wrong, Laravel shows:\n\n```text\n419 | Page Expired\n```\n\nYou will see this in three common cases:\n\n| Cause | Fix |\n|-------|-----|\n| You forgot `@csrf` in the form | Add `@csrf` |\n| The page was open so long that the session expired | Reload the page and submit again |\n| You cleared cookies, or logged in on another tab | Reload the page |\n\nThe default session lasts 120 minutes (`SESSION_LIFETIME=120` in `.env`).\n\n## 📡 Tokens with JavaScript\n\nIf your page sends data with JavaScript's `fetch()`, it must send the token too. Put it in a `<meta>` tag in the layout:\n\n```blade\n{{-- layouts/app.blade.php, inside <head> --}}\n<meta name=\"csrf-token\" content=\"{{ csrf_token() }}\">\n```\n\nThen read it and send it as a header:\n\n```js\nconst token = document.querySelector('meta[name=\"csrf-token\"]').content;\n\nfetch('/loans/7/return', {\n    method: 'POST',\n    headers: { 'X-CSRF-TOKEN': token },\n});\n```\n\nLaravel accepts the token from the `_token` field **or** the `X-CSRF-TOKEN` header.\n\n## 🚫 GET Must Never Change Data\n\nCSRF checks only run on POST, PUT, PATCH and DELETE. GET requests are not checked, because GET is supposed to only **read**. So a GET route that changes data has no protection:\n\n```php\n// DANGEROUS: any website can trigger it with a simple link\nRoute::get('/books/{book}/delete', [BookController::class, 'destroy']);\n```\n\nA harmful page could show a friendly-looking link to `http://library.example.org/books/3/delete`. One click by a logged-in librarian, and the book is gone. Browsers send the session cookie with links like this, and GET has no token check. Use the proper resource route with a DELETE form (Chapter 19).\n\n### Logout is a POST form\n\nLogging out changes the session, so it is a POST too. Starter kits (Chapter 22) do it like this:\n\n```blade\n<form method=\"POST\" action=\"{{ route('logout') }}\">\n    @csrf\n    <button type=\"submit\">Log out</button>\n</form>\n```\n\nIf logout were a plain link, any website could log your users out with one link.\n\n## 🧩 Excluding a Route (Rarely)\n\nSometimes another **server** (not a browser) must send POST requests to your app, such as an online donation service reporting a gift. It cannot know your token. You can exclude that one URL in `bootstrap/app.php`:\n\n```php\n->withMiddleware(function (Middleware $middleware): void {\n    $middleware->validateCsrfTokens(except: [\n        'webhooks/donations',\n    ]);\n})\n```\n\nSuch routes need **another** way to prove who is calling, such as a secret signature. Never exclude routes just to make a 419 error go away.\n\n## 🛡️ What CSRF Protection Does Not Do\n\nCSRF protection checks **where a request came from**. It does not:\n\n- check whether the data is correct (that is **validation**, Chapter 20),\n- stop harmful text being shown on a page (that is **escaping** with `{{ }}`, Chapter 10),\n- decide who is allowed to do what (that is **authorization**, Chapter 24).\n\nLaravel gives you each tool. A safe app uses all of them.\n\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\n\n**\"Fixing\" a 419 error by turning protection off.**\n\n```php\n$middleware->validateCsrfTokens(except: ['*']);   // wrong: protection gone\n```\n\nThe error disappears, and so does the protection for every form. Fix the real cause instead:\n\n```blade\n<form method=\"POST\" action=\"{{ route('books.store') }}\">\n    @csrf        {{-- right: the missing line --}}\n```\n\n**Putting `@csrf` outside the form.** If `@csrf` sits above `<form>`, the hidden input is not part of the form, and the token is not sent. Put it **inside**, just after the opening tag.\n\n## ✍️ Try It\n\nOpen your \"Add a Book\" page and view the source. Find the `_token` input. Then remove `@csrf`, submit the form, and see the 419 page. Put `@csrf` back.\n\n*Hint:* in most browsers, **Ctrl+U** opens the page source.\n\n## 📝 Recap\n\n- CSRF is a forged request from another site that uses a logged-in user's cookie.\n- Laravel puts a secret token in each form and checks it on POST, PUT, PATCH and DELETE.\n- Put `@csrf` inside every POST form; a missing or old token gives **419 Page Expired**.\n- JavaScript sends the token in the `X-CSRF-TOKEN` header.\n- GET must never change data; logout is a POST form with `@csrf`.\n\n## 🏆 Practice\n\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\nWhat does CSRF stand for, and what HTTP code does Laravel show when the token is missing?\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\nCross-Site Request Forgery. Laravel shows **419 Page Expired**.\n\n</details>\n\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\nA classmate writes this logout link. Explain the problem and rewrite it.\n\n```blade\n<a href=\"/logout\">Log out</a>\n```\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\nA link sends GET, which has no CSRF check, so any website could log users out with a link of its own. And logging out changes data (the session). Use a POST form:\n\n```blade\n<form method=\"POST\" action=\"{{ route('logout') }}\">\n    @csrf\n    <button type=\"submit\">Log out</button>\n</form>\n```\n\n</details>\n\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\nAudit your library app for CSRF safety.\n\n1. List every form in your views and check each has `@csrf` inside it.\n2. Run `php artisan route:list --method=GET` and check that no GET route creates, changes or deletes data.\n3. If your app uses `fetch()` anywhere, check it sends `X-CSRF-TOKEN`.\n4. Write one sentence for each finding: what you checked and what you fixed.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\n\n1. `books/create`, `books/edit`, the delete button, `members/create` and `loans/create` all have `@csrf` inside `<form>`. Fixed: the delete button had `@csrf` above `<form>`; moved inside.\n2. All GET routes only show pages. Fixed: an old `/books/{book}/delete` GET route was removed; the DELETE form is used instead.\n3. The \"Mark returned\" button used `fetch()` without the header; added the `<meta name=\"csrf-token\">` tag and the `X-CSRF-TOKEN` header.\n4. These sentences are the report.\n\n</details>\n",
  "part-08-authentication/ch22-auth-scaffolding.md": "# Chapter 22: Auth Scaffolding\n\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\n\nWhen a new school opens, nobody builds the front gate's lock from scratch. The school buys a **tested lock** from a trusted maker, fits it, and gives keys to the right people. Making your own lock is a great way to *learn* how locks work, but the gate itself should use one that experts have tested for years. Laravel's **starter kits** are that tested lock for logins. In this chapter you meet them; in Chapter 23 you build a simple lock by hand to see how it works inside.\n\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\n\n- Explain **authentication** and how it differs from **authorization**.\n- Describe what Laravel already has for users: the `users` table, the `User` model and password hashing.\n- Create a project with a Laravel 12 **starter kit** (React, Vue or Livewire).\n- Know that **Breeze** was the starter kit for Laravel 11 and earlier.\n- Protect passwords as an *amānah*.\n\n## 🔑 Authentication vs Authorization\n\n| Word | Question it answers | Library example |\n|------|--------------------|-----------------|\n| **Authentication** | *Who are you?* | Mrs Rahman logs in with her email and password |\n| **Authorization** | *What may you do?* | Only librarians may delete books |\n\nThis chapter and the next cover authentication. Chapter 24 covers both: keeping pages for logged-in users, and keeping some pages for librarians only.\n\n## 🧱 What Laravel Already Gives You\n\nEven a brand-new project is ready for users:\n\n- **The `users` table**, made by the first migration (Chapter 16): `name`, `email`, `password`, `remember_token` and more.\n- **The `User` model** in `app/Models/User.php`.\n- **The `sessions` table**, where Laravel remembers who is logged in.\n- **The `auth` and `guest` middleware** (Chapter 9).\n\nLook inside `User.php` (shortened):\n\n```php\nclass User extends Authenticatable\n{\n    protected $fillable = ['name', 'email', 'password'];\n\n    protected $hidden = ['password', 'remember_token'];\n\n    protected function casts(): array\n    {\n        return [\n            'email_verified_at' => 'datetime',\n            'password' => 'hashed',\n        ];\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- `$hidden` keeps the password out of arrays and JSON, so it is never sent to a browser by accident.\n- The `'password' => 'hashed'` cast means: whenever a password is saved, **hash** it first.\n\n## 🔒 Hashing Passwords\n\nA **hash** is a one-way scramble. You can turn a password into a hash, but you cannot turn the hash back into the password. Laravel uses **bcrypt**, the same method as PHP's `password_hash()`, which you met in the PHP book.\n\nTry it in Tinker:\n\n```text\n> $hash = Hash::make('river-stone-42')\n= \"$2y$12$2DhQkYLOxAFvDwUqSsKMHeehLomC1q5eNSd9ntNn2NB8iZLctXWma\"\n\n> Hash::check('river-stone-42', $hash)\n= true\n\n> Hash::check('river-stone-43', $hash)\n= false\n```\n\nYour hash will look different every time, even for the same password, because bcrypt adds a random **salt**. `$2y$` means bcrypt and `12` is how hard it works. To log someone in, Laravel hashes what they typed and compares; it never needs the real password.\n\n### Passwords are an *amānah*\n\nPeople often reuse passwords. If your app leaked them, a member's other accounts could be at risk. So:\n\n- **Never** store a plain password, print it, log it, or email it.\n- **Never** put real people's passwords in seeders or Git.\n- Use the tools that hash for you, and let users reset forgotten passwords instead of \"telling\" them their old one.\n\n## 🚀 Laravel 12 Starter Kits\n\nA **starter kit** is a ready-made app with login, registration, password reset, email verification, a dashboard and profile settings. You start a **new** project with it. In Laravel 12 the kits are:\n\n| Kit | Front end | Good for |\n|-----|-----------|----------|\n| **Livewire** | Blade + Livewire components | Students who know Blade (closest to this book) |\n| **React** | React + Inertia | Teams that use React |\n| **Vue** | Vue + Inertia | Teams that use Vue |\n\n(Newer versions of the installer may offer more choices.) The kits also need **Node.js** and **npm**, which build the CSS and JavaScript.\n\n### Try one in a practice project\n\nInstall the Laravel installer once:\n\n```bash\ncomposer global require laravel/installer\n```\n\nThen create a new practice project (not your `library-app`):\n\n```bash\ncd C:\\xampp\\htdocs\nlaravel new library-portal\n```\n\nThe installer asks a few questions. Choose the **Livewire** starter kit and Laravel's built-in authentication, and say yes to installing npm packages. For the database you can keep SQLite for this practice project, or pick MySQL and set it up like Chapter 3. Then:\n\n```bash\ncd library-portal\nnpm install\nnpm run build\nphp artisan migrate\nphp artisan serve\n```\n\nVisit `http://127.0.0.1:8000/register`, create an account with a made-up email such as `test@example.org`, and you land on `/dashboard`. Log out and log in again at `/login`. Open phpMyAdmin (or the SQLite file): your password is stored as a long `$2y$12$...` hash.\n\n### What the kit added\n\nExplore the new project. You will find:\n\n- routes for login, register, logout, password reset and email verification,\n- Blade or JavaScript pages for each form,\n- a `dashboard` route protected by the `auth` middleware,\n- tests that check it all works.\n\nAll of it uses the same ideas as this book: routes, controllers or components, validation, `@csrf`, the `auth` middleware and the `User` model. Logout is a **POST** form with `@csrf`, as Chapter 21 explained.\n\n## 🕰️ Laravel 11 and Earlier: Breeze\n\nYou will still find older tutorials and school projects using **Laravel Breeze**. Breeze was the simple starter kit for Laravel 11 and earlier. You added it to an existing project:\n\n```bash\ncomposer require laravel/breeze --dev\nphp artisan breeze:install\nnpm install\nnpm run build\nphp artisan migrate\n```\n\nFor a new Laravel 12 project, use the starter kits above instead. If you see `breeze:install` in a tutorial, check which Laravel version it was written for.\n\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\n\n**Saving a password without hashing it.**\n\n```php\n// a model WITHOUT the 'hashed' cast\nUser::create([\n    'name' => 'Hamza',\n    'email' => 'hamza@example.org',\n    'password' => $request->password,   // stored as plain text!\n]);\n```\n\nWhat happens: the password sits in the database as plain text, and logging in fails, because Laravel compares the typed password against a hash. Keep the `'password' => 'hashed'` cast in `User`, or hash it yourself:\n\n```php\n'password' => Hash::make($request->password),\n```\n\nDo **not** do both, or the password is hashed twice and nobody can log in.\n\n**Skipping `npm run build`.** A starter-kit page with no styling, or a \"Vite manifest not found\" error, means the front-end files were never built. Run `npm install` and `npm run build`.\n\n## ✍️ Try It\n\nCreate the `library-portal` practice project with the Livewire kit. Register two made-up users, then find them in the `users` table. Are the two hashes the same length? Do they start the same way?\n\n*Hint:* both start with `$2y$12$` and are 60 characters long.\n\n## 📝 Recap\n\n- Authentication asks *who are you?*; authorization asks *what may you do?*\n- Laravel ships with a `users` table, a `User` model, sessions, and `auth`/`guest` middleware.\n- The `'password' => 'hashed'` cast hashes passwords with bcrypt; `Hash::check()` compares them.\n- Laravel 12 starter kits (Livewire, React, Vue) give you a tested login system in a new project.\n- Breeze was the starter kit for Laravel 11 and earlier.\n\n## 🏆 Practice\n\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\nWhat is the difference between authentication and authorization? Give a library example of each.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\nAuthentication checks **who** you are (logging in with email and password). Authorization checks **what you may do** (only librarians may delete books).\n\n</details>\n\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\nA student says: \"Hashing is like locking the password in a box. The app can unlock it to check it.\" What is wrong with this idea?\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\nA hash cannot be \"unlocked\". It is one-way. To check a password, Laravel hashes what the user typed and compares the result with the stored hash (`Hash::check()`). The real password is never stored and never recovered.\n\n</details>\n\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\nWrite a one-page \"Login Safety Plan\" for the library app.\n\n1. Which starter kit would you choose for a team that knows Blade, and why?\n2. List three things the app must **never** do with passwords.\n3. Which pages should only logged-in users see? Which only librarians?\n4. What should happen when a member forgets their password?\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\n\n1. The Livewire kit, because its pages are Blade templates, which the team already knows.\n2. Never store passwords as plain text, never show or email them, never put real passwords in seeders or Git.\n3. Logged-in users: \"My loans\", profile. Librarians only: add/edit/delete books, record returns, member list.\n4. They use \"Forgot password?\": the app emails a reset link with a token that expires, and they choose a new password. Nobody ever sees the old one.\n\n</details>\n",
  "part-08-authentication/ch23-login-and-register.md": "# Chapter 23: Login & Register\n\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\n\nTo join the library, you fill in a **membership form** and the librarian gives you a card. Next time, you show the card at the gate. The gatekeeper checks it, nods, and lets you in. When you leave, you collect your card from the desk and go. Register, log in, log out: three steps every website with accounts has. In this chapter you build all three by hand, so you understand what the starter kits do for you.\n\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\n\n- Build a **register** page that creates a user with a hashed password.\n- Build a **login** page with `Auth::attempt()`.\n- Build a **logout** button as a POST form with `@csrf`.\n- Show different links to guests and logged-in users with `@guest` and `@auth`.\n- Slow down password guessing with `throttle`.\n\n> We build this inside your `library-app` to learn how login works. For a real school app, use a starter kit (Chapter 22). It also handles password resets, email checks and many edge cases.\n\n## 🗺️ The Routes\n\n```php\n// routes/web.php\nuse App\\Http\\Controllers\\Auth\\LoginController;\nuse App\\Http\\Controllers\\Auth\\RegisterController;\n\nRoute::middleware('guest')->group(function () {\n    Route::get('/register', [RegisterController::class, 'create'])->name('register');\n    Route::post('/register', [RegisterController::class, 'store']);\n\n    Route::get('/login', [LoginController::class, 'create'])->name('login');\n    Route::post('/login', [LoginController::class, 'store'])->middleware('throttle:5,1');\n});\n\nRoute::post('/logout', [LoginController::class, 'destroy'])\n    ->middleware('auth')\n    ->name('logout');\n```\n\n- The `guest` middleware sends logged-in users away from the login and register pages.\n- The login page **must** be named `login`. The `auth` middleware sends visitors there by that name (Chapter 24).\n- `throttle:5,1` allows 5 login attempts per minute. After that, Laravel answers **429 Too Many Requests**. This slows down anyone guessing passwords.\n- Logout is POST, for the reasons in Chapter 21.\n\nCreate the controllers in an `Auth` folder:\n\n```bash\nphp artisan make:controller Auth/RegisterController\nphp artisan make:controller Auth/LoginController\n```\n\n## 📝 Register\n\n```php\n// app/Http/Controllers/Auth/RegisterController.php\nnamespace App\\Http\\Controllers\\Auth;\n\nuse App\\Http\\Controllers\\Controller;\nuse App\\Models\\User;\nuse Illuminate\\Http\\Request;\nuse Illuminate\\Support\\Facades\\Auth;\n\nclass RegisterController extends Controller\n{\n    public function create()\n    {\n        return view('auth.register');\n    }\n\n    public function store(Request $request)\n    {\n        $validated = $request->validate([\n            'name' => 'required|string|max:255',\n            'email' => 'required|email|max:255|unique:users,email',\n            'password' => 'required|string|min:8|confirmed',\n        ]);\n\n        $user = User::create($validated);   // the 'hashed' cast hashes the password\n\n        Auth::login($user);\n        $request->session()->regenerate();\n\n        return redirect()->route('books.index')\n            ->with('status', \"Welcome, {$user->name}!\");\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- `confirmed` means the form must also have a field `password_confirmation` with the same value.\n- `User::create()` saves the user. The `'hashed'` cast from Chapter 22 hashes the password first.\n- `Auth::login($user)` logs the new user in straight away.\n- `session()->regenerate()` gives the visitor a **new session id**. This blocks an attack where someone plants a session id before login.\n\nThe view:\n\n```blade\n{{-- resources/views/auth/register.blade.php --}}\n@extends('layouts.app')\n\n@section('content')\n<h2>Join the Library</h2>\n<form method=\"POST\" action=\"{{ route('register') }}\">\n    @csrf\n    <label for=\"name\">Name</label>\n    <input id=\"name\" name=\"name\" value=\"{{ old('name') }}\" required>\n    @error('name') <p class=\"error\">{{ $message }}</p> @enderror\n\n    <label for=\"email\">Email</label>\n    <input id=\"email\" type=\"email\" name=\"email\" value=\"{{ old('email') }}\" required>\n    @error('email') <p class=\"error\">{{ $message }}</p> @enderror\n\n    <label for=\"password\">Password</label>\n    <input id=\"password\" type=\"password\" name=\"password\" required>\n    @error('password') <p class=\"error\">{{ $message }}</p> @enderror\n\n    <label for=\"password_confirmation\">Type the password again</label>\n    <input id=\"password_confirmation\" type=\"password\" name=\"password_confirmation\" required>\n\n    <button type=\"submit\">Register</button>\n</form>\n@endsection\n```\n\nNotice: **no** `old('password')`. Never send a password back to the page.\n\nIf the two passwords differ, the form returns with:\n\n```text\nThe password field confirmation does not match.\n```\n\n## 🔑 Log In\n\n```php\n// app/Http/Controllers/Auth/LoginController.php\nnamespace App\\Http\\Controllers\\Auth;\n\nuse App\\Http\\Controllers\\Controller;\nuse Illuminate\\Http\\Request;\nuse Illuminate\\Support\\Facades\\Auth;\n\nclass LoginController extends Controller\n{\n    public function create()\n    {\n        return view('auth.login');\n    }\n\n    public function store(Request $request)\n    {\n        $credentials = $request->validate([\n            'email' => 'required|email',\n            'password' => 'required|string',\n        ]);\n\n        if (Auth::attempt($credentials, $request->boolean('remember'))) {\n            $request->session()->regenerate();\n\n            return redirect()->intended(route('books.index'));\n        }\n\n        return back()\n            ->withErrors(['email' => 'These credentials do not match our records.'])\n            ->onlyInput('email');\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- `Auth::attempt()` finds the user by email, hashes the typed password, and compares. It returns `true` and logs them in, or returns `false`.\n- The second argument turns on **\"Remember me\"**: the user stays logged in after closing the browser.\n- `redirect()->intended(...)` sends the user to the page they were trying to reach before login, or to the book list.\n- On failure, we give **one** message for both \"wrong email\" and \"wrong password\". Saying \"no account with that email\" would tell a stranger which emails are members. Protecting that is part of *amānah*.\n- `onlyInput('email')` refills the email field but not the password.\n\nThe form:\n\n```blade\n{{-- resources/views/auth/login.blade.php --}}\n<form method=\"POST\" action=\"{{ route('login') }}\">\n    @csrf\n    <label for=\"email\">Email</label>\n    <input id=\"email\" type=\"email\" name=\"email\" value=\"{{ old('email') }}\" required>\n    @error('email') <p class=\"error\">{{ $message }}</p> @enderror\n\n    <label for=\"password\">Password</label>\n    <input id=\"password\" type=\"password\" name=\"password\" required>\n\n    <label><input type=\"checkbox\" name=\"remember\"> Remember me</label>\n\n    <button type=\"submit\">Log in</button>\n</form>\n```\n\nA wrong password shows:\n\n```text\nThese credentials do not match our records.\n```\n\n## 🚪 Log Out\n\n```php\n// app/Http/Controllers/Auth/LoginController.php\npublic function destroy(Request $request)\n{\n    Auth::logout();\n\n    $request->session()->invalidate();\n    $request->session()->regenerateToken();\n\n    return redirect('/');\n}\n```\n\n`invalidate()` empties the session. `regenerateToken()` makes a new CSRF token, so old forms cannot be reused.\n\n## 👀 Who is Logged In?\n\n| Code | Gives |\n|------|-------|\n| `Auth::check()` | `true` if someone is logged in |\n| `Auth::user()` or `auth()->user()` | the logged-in `User`, or `null` |\n| `Auth::id()` | the user's id |\n| `$request->user()` | the same user, inside a controller |\n\nIn Blade, `@auth` and `@guest` show different things:\n\n```blade\n{{-- resources/views/partials/nav.blade.php --}}\n@auth\n    <span>Hello, {{ auth()->user()->name }}</span>\n    <form method=\"POST\" action=\"{{ route('logout') }}\">\n        @csrf\n        <button type=\"submit\">Log out</button>\n    </form>\n@endauth\n\n@guest\n    <a href=\"{{ route('login') }}\">Log in</a>\n    <a href=\"{{ route('register') }}\">Join</a>\n@endguest\n```\n\nWhen Aisha is logged in, the menu shows:\n\n```text\nHello, Aisha  [Log out]\n```\n\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\n\n**Not naming the login route `login`.**\n\n```php\nRoute::get('/sign-in', [LoginController::class, 'create']);   // no name\n```\n\nWhat happens: when a guest opens a page protected by `auth`, Laravel tries to send them to the route named `login` and shows `Route [login] not defined.` Fix: add `->name('login')` to the login page route.\n\n**Forgetting `regenerate()` after login.** The login seems to work, but the visitor keeps the session id from before login. An attacker who had planted that id could share the logged-in session. Always call `$request->session()->regenerate()` after `Auth::attempt()` or `Auth::login()`.\n\n## ✍️ Try It\n\nAdd the register, login and logout pages to `library-app`. Register yourself with a made-up email, log out, then try logging in with a wrong password six times in one minute. What happens on the sixth try?\n\n*Hint:* the sixth try shows **429 | Too Many Requests**. Wait a minute and try again.\n\n## 📝 Recap\n\n- Register: validate (with `confirmed`), `User::create()`, `Auth::login()`, regenerate the session.\n- Log in: validate, `Auth::attempt($credentials, $remember)`, regenerate, `redirect()->intended()`.\n- Give one vague error for wrong email or password; never refill the password field.\n- Log out with a POST form: `Auth::logout()`, `invalidate()`, `regenerateToken()`.\n- `@auth` / `@guest` and `auth()->user()` show who is logged in; `throttle` slows down guessing.\n\n## 🏆 Practice\n\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\nWhat does `Auth::attempt()` do, and what does it return?\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\nIt looks up the user by email, hashes the typed password and compares it with the stored hash. If they match, it logs the user in and returns `true`; otherwise it returns `false`.\n\n</details>\n\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\nWhy does the login error say \"These credentials do not match our records.\" instead of \"No member uses that email\"?\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\nA specific message would tell a stranger which emails belong to members. That leaks private information. One vague message for both cases protects members' privacy (*amānah*).\n\n</details>\n\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\nLink users to library members.\n\n1. Write a migration that adds a nullable `user_id` foreign key to `members`.\n2. Add `member()` to `User` (`hasOne`) and `user()` to `Member` (`belongsTo`).\n3. Make a \"My loans\" page at `/my-loans` that shows the logged-in user's loans, or `You have no books borrowed.`\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\n\n```php\n// database/migrations/..._add_user_id_to_members_table.php\npublic function up(): void\n{\n    Schema::table('members', function (Blueprint $table) {\n        $table->foreignId('user_id')->nullable()->constrained();\n    });\n}\n\npublic function down(): void\n{\n    Schema::table('members', function (Blueprint $table) {\n        $table->dropConstrainedForeignId('user_id');\n    });\n}\n```\n\n```php\n// app/Models/User.php\npublic function member(): HasOne\n{\n    return $this->hasOne(Member::class);\n}\n\n// app/Models/Member.php\npublic function user(): BelongsTo\n{\n    return $this->belongsTo(User::class);\n}\n```\n\n```php\n// routes/web.php\nRoute::get('/my-loans', function () {\n    $member = auth()->user()->member;\n    $loans = $member ? $member->loans()->with('book')->get() : collect();\n\n    return view('loans.mine', ['loans' => $loans]);\n})->middleware('auth')->name('loans.mine');\n```\n\n```blade\n{{-- resources/views/loans/mine.blade.php --}}\n@forelse ($loans as $loan)\n    <p>{{ $loan->book->title }}, due {{ $loan->due_on->format('d M Y') }}</p>\n@empty\n    <p>You have no books borrowed.</p>\n@endforelse\n```\n\n`collect()` makes an empty collection, so `@forelse` works even for users with no member card.\n\n</details>\n",
  "part-08-authentication/ch24-protecting-routes.md": "# Chapter 24: Protecting Routes\n\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\n\nThe school library has three kinds of spaces. The **reading hall** is open to every visitor. The **borrowing desk** needs a library card. The **records room**, where the register is kept, is only for librarians. Nobody would put the register in the reading hall. Your app needs the same three levels: pages for everyone, pages for logged-in users, and pages for librarians only.\n\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\n\n- Protect routes and groups with the `auth` middleware.\n- Explain what happens to a guest who opens a protected page.\n- Split a resource into public and protected actions, in the right order.\n- Give librarians extra rights with a **Gate** and `->can()`.\n- Show or hide buttons in Blade with `@can`.\n\n## 🔐 Logged-in Users Only\n\nThe `auth` middleware (Chapter 9) lets a request through only if someone is logged in:\n\n```php\n// routes/web.php\nRoute::get('/my-loans', [LoanController::class, 'mine'])\n    ->middleware('auth')\n    ->name('loans.mine');\n```\n\nWhen a **guest** opens `/my-loans`:\n\n1. `auth` sees nobody is logged in.\n2. It remembers the page they wanted.\n3. It redirects them to the route named `login` (Chapter 23).\n4. After they log in, `redirect()->intended(...)` sends them back to `/my-loans`.\n\n```text\nGuest -> /my-loans\n           |\n       auth: who are you?\n           |\n           v\n        /login  -- correct password --+\n                                      |\n        /my-loans  <-- intended() ----+\n```\n\n### Protect a whole group\n\n```php\nRoute::middleware('auth')->group(function () {\n    Route::get('/my-loans', [LoanController::class, 'mine'])->name('loans.mine');\n    Route::get('/profile', [ProfileController::class, 'show'])->name('profile');\n});\n```\n\nStarter kits also use `verified` (`->middleware(['auth', 'verified'])`) for pages that need a confirmed email address.\n\n## 📚 Public and Protected Parts of One Resource\n\nEveryone may **browse** books. Only logged-in staff may **add, edit or delete** them. Split the resource:\n\n```php\n// routes/web.php\nuse App\\Http\\Controllers\\BookController;\n\n// Protected: register FIRST, because it contains /books/create\nRoute::middleware('auth')->group(function () {\n    Route::resource('books', BookController::class)->except(['index', 'show']);\n});\n\n// Public\nRoute::resource('books', BookController::class)->only(['index', 'show']);\n```\n\nCheck it with `php artisan route:list --name=books -v`. The `-v` option shows each route's middleware. The create, store, edit, update and destroy routes list `auth`; index and show do not.\n\n## 🛂 Librarians Only (Authorization)\n\nBeing logged in is not enough to delete books. Members log in too. We need **authorization**: deciding what a logged-in user may do.\n\n### Step 1: mark librarians\n\nAdd a column with a migration:\n\n```bash\nphp artisan make:migration add_is_librarian_to_users_table\n```\n\n```php\npublic function up(): void\n{\n    Schema::table('users', function (Blueprint $table) {\n        $table->boolean('is_librarian')->default(false);\n    });\n}\n\npublic function down(): void\n{\n    Schema::table('users', function (Blueprint $table) {\n        $table->dropColumn('is_librarian');\n    });\n}\n```\n\nRun `php artisan migrate`. In `User.php`, add `'is_librarian' => 'boolean'` to `casts()`. Do **not** add it to `$fillable`: no form should ever be able to set it (Chapter 14).\n\nMake one user a librarian in Tinker. A query-builder `update()` does not use `$fillable`, so it works here:\n\n```text\n> User::where('email', 'rahman@example.org')->update(['is_librarian' => true])\n= 1\n```\n\n### Step 2: define a Gate\n\nA **Gate** is a named yes-or-no rule about a user. Define it in `app/Providers/AppServiceProvider.php`:\n\n```php\n// app/Providers/AppServiceProvider.php\nuse App\\Models\\User;\nuse Illuminate\\Support\\Facades\\Gate;\n\npublic function boot(): void\n{\n    Gate::define('manage-books', function (User $user) {\n        return $user->is_librarian;\n    });\n}\n```\n\nNow `manage-books` is true for librarians and false for everyone else.\n\n### Step 3: use it on routes\n\nAdd the `can:manage-books` middleware after `auth`. It asks the gate before the controller runs:\n\n```php\nRoute::middleware('auth')->group(function () {\n    Route::resource('books', BookController::class)\n        ->except(['index', 'show'])\n        ->middleware('can:manage-books');\n});\n```\n\nOn a single route there is a shorter way to write the same check: `->can('manage-books')`.\n\nWhen a logged-in member who is not a librarian opens `/books/create`, Laravel shows:\n\n```text\n403 | This action is unauthorized.\n```\n\n**403 Forbidden** means \"I know who you are, but you may not do this\". Compare: a guest gets sent to the login page instead.\n\n### Checking inside a controller\n\nYou can also check in a method:\n\n```php\npublic function destroy(Book $book)\n{\n    Gate::authorize('manage-books');   // 403 if not allowed\n\n    $book->delete();\n\n    return redirect()->route('books.index')->with('status', 'Book removed.');\n}\n```\n\nChecking in **both** the route and the controller is fine. Extra care with the library's records is never wasted.\n\n## 🙈 Hide Buttons People Cannot Use\n\nProtecting the route is what keeps the app safe. Hiding the button keeps the page tidy and honest. Use `@can`:\n\n```blade\n{{-- resources/views/books/show.blade.php --}}\n<h2>{{ $book->title }}</h2>\n\n@can('manage-books')\n    <a href=\"{{ route('books.edit', $book) }}\">Edit</a>\n    <form method=\"POST\" action=\"{{ route('books.destroy', $book) }}\">\n        @csrf\n        @method('DELETE')\n        <button type=\"submit\">Delete</button>\n    </form>\n@endcan\n```\n\nA librarian sees the buttons; a member does not. Remember: hiding a button is **not** protection. Someone could still type the URL. The route's `can` check is what stops them.\n\n## 🏁 Your Finished Library App\n\nLook how far you have come. Your app now has:\n\n| Part | Chapters |\n|------|----------|\n| Routes, names, groups | 4–6 |\n| Controllers, resources, middleware | 7–9 |\n| Blade layouts, loops, safe output | 10–12 |\n| Eloquent models and relationships | 13–15 |\n| Migrations, seeders, factories | 16–18 |\n| Forms, validation, CSRF | 19–21 |\n| Login, logout, protected routes | 22–24 |\n\nIdeas to keep growing: a \"Mark returned\" button that increments copies, a search box with `where('title', 'like', ...)`, email reminders for overdue books, and tests with `php artisan test`. The official documentation at laravel.com/docs is your next textbook.\n\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\n\n**Registering the public `show` route before the protected `create` route.**\n\n```php\nRoute::resource('books', BookController::class)->only(['index', 'show']);   // first\n\nRoute::middleware('auth')->group(function () {\n    Route::resource('books', BookController::class)->except(['index', 'show']);\n});\n```\n\nWhat happens: `/books/create` matches `/books/{book}` first. Route model binding looks for a book with id `create`, finds none, and shows **404 Not Found**. Put the group with `create` **first**, as shown above.\n\n**Only hiding buttons.** If `@can` hides the Delete button but the route has no `can:manage-books`, any logged-in member can still send a DELETE request and remove books. Always protect the **route**.\n\n## ✍️ Try It\n\nAdd `is_librarian`, the `manage-books` gate and the protected book routes to your app. Make two users: one librarian, one member. Log in as each and try `/books/create`.\n\n*Hint:* the member should see 403, a guest should be sent to `/login`, and the librarian should see the form.\n\n## 📝 Recap\n\n- `->middleware('auth')` lets only logged-in users through; guests go to the `login` route and come back with `intended()`.\n- Split resources with `only()` / `except()`, and register the group containing `create` first.\n- Authorization decides what a user may do: define a Gate in `AppServiceProvider`.\n- Use `can:manage-books` on routes and `Gate::authorize()` in controllers; failures give 403.\n- `@can` hides buttons, but only route checks give real protection.\n\n## 🏆 Practice\n\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\nWhat is the difference between being sent to `/login` and seeing a 403 page?\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\nBeing sent to `/login` means the app does not know who you are (you are a guest), which is authentication. A **403** means the app knows who you are, but you are not allowed to do this, which is authorization.\n\n</details>\n\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\nWrite the routes so that everyone can see `/loans/overdue`, logged-in users can see `/my-loans`, and only librarians can open `/loans/create`.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\n```php\n// routes/web.php\nRoute::get('/loans/overdue', [LoanController::class, 'overdue'])->name('loans.overdue');\n\nRoute::middleware('auth')->group(function () {\n    Route::get('/my-loans', [LoanController::class, 'mine'])->name('loans.mine');\n\n    Route::get('/loans/create', [LoanController::class, 'create'])\n        ->can('manage-books')\n        ->name('loans.create');\n});\n```\n\n(You could define a separate gate such as `manage-loans` with the same rule.)\n\n</details>\n\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\nFinish the library app's security.\n\n1. Protect every librarian action (books, members, loans) with `auth` and a gate.\n2. Show a \"Librarian\" link in the menu only to librarians.\n3. Make `members.show` visible to librarians **and** to the member who owns that card. (Hint: define a gate `view-member` that receives the user and the member.)\n4. Test with three accounts: a guest, a member and a librarian. Write down what each sees.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\n\n```php\n// app/Providers/AppServiceProvider.php\nGate::define('view-member', function (User $user, Member $member) {\n    return $user->is_librarian || $member->user_id === $user->id;\n});\n```\n\n```php\n// app/Http/Controllers/MemberController.php\npublic function show(Member $member)\n{\n    Gate::authorize('view-member', $member);\n\n    return view('members.show', ['member' => $member]);\n}\n```\n\n```blade\n{{-- resources/views/partials/nav.blade.php --}}\n@can('manage-books')\n    <a href=\"{{ route('books.create') }}\">Librarian</a>\n@endcan\n```\n\nResults: the guest is sent to `/login`; the member sees only their own member page and gets 403 on others; the librarian sees everything. (`user_id` comes from the Chapter 23 challenge.)\n\n</details>\n"
};
